/* Machine translations of meier in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['meier/guyer'] = {"meier:1":["The doctrine of reason or the art of reason (*logica, philosophia instrumentalis, philosophia rationalis*) is a science which treats the rules of learned cognition and of learned presentation.",[]],"meier:2":["In order that the doctrine of reason contain no wholly arbitrary, contrived and unnatural laws, its rules must be derived 1) from the experiences of the effects of human reason, 2) from the nature of human reason, 3) from the universal fundamental truths on which the whole of human cognition rests.",[]],"meier:3":["The aim of the doctrine of reason is either the perfection of a learned cognition and of a learned presentation which are suited merely to the learned by profession, or which are also becoming and useful to other learned people.",[]],"meier:4":["The doctrine of reason is a means without which one can attain no learned cognition and science, and through the proper use of which a learned cognition and science is attained §.1.",[]],"meier:5":["Philosophy (*philosophia*) is a science of the more universal properties of things, insofar as they are cognized without belief. Now since learned cognition and learned presentation comprehend many species under themselves, their perfections and imperfections are more universal properties of things, which are completely proven in the doctrine of reason without deriving their truth from testimonies. The doctrine of reason is accordingly a part of philosophy.",[]],"meier:6":["The doctrine of reason treats either of a completely certain learned cognition and the presentation of it, or of probable learned cognition and the presentation of it §.1. The former is the doctrine of reason of wholly certain learned cognition (*analytica*), and the latter the doctrine of reason of probable learned cognition (*dialectica, logica probabilium*). We treat the first doctrine of reason.",[]],"meier:7":["In the doctrine of reason the rules of learned cognition and of learned presentation are either applied to the particular species of them or not. The former is the practicing doctrine of reason (*logica practica, utens*) and the latter the teaching doctrine of reason (*logica theoretica, docens*).",[]],"meier:8":["If the doctrine of reason is so constituted as it can and must be constituted by virtue of its nature, then it has, among others, a threefold utility. 1) It promotes the learning and spreading of all sciences, and of the whole of learnedness. Every student should therefore rightly make the beginning with the learning of the doctrine of reason. 2) It improves the understanding and reason, and shows how one must use these cognitive powers in order to cognize the truth in a proper way. 3) It promotes the whole of virtue, in that it improves the free will; procures that cognition on which virtue rests; and has an indispensable influence on the improvement of conscience.",[]],"meier:9":["The doctrine of reason treats I. Of learned cognition. 1. Of learned cognition in general. 2. Of the extensiveness of learned cognition. 3. Of the magnitude of learned cognition. 4. Of the truth of learned cognition. 5. Of the clarity of learned cognition. 6. Of the certainty of learned cognition. 7. Of practical learned cognition. 8. Of learned concepts. 9. Of learned judgments. 10. Of learned syllogisms. II. Of the method of learned cognition. III. Of learned presentation. 1. Of the use of words. 2. Of the learned style of writing. 3. Of a learned speech. 4. Of learned writings. IV. Of the character of a learned person. The doctrine of reason. Of learned cognition. Of learned cognition in general.",[]],"meier:10":["Experience teaches that we represent to ourselves infinitely many things. A representation (*repraesentatio perceptio*) is related as a picture which the painterly skill of the soul draws in its interior.",[]],"meier:11":["Cognition (*cognitio*) is either a sum total of many representations, or that action through which a representation of a thing is effected. One can also, without fearing a noticeable error, take representations and cognition to be one and the same.",[]],"meier:12":["From representation and cognition is distinguished that which we represent to ourselves, and which we cognize. The latter is called the object of cognition and of representation (*obiectum cognitionis et repraesentationis*).",[]],"meier:13":["We are conscious of our representations and our cognition (*conscium esse, adpercipere*) insofar as we distinguish them and their object from other representations and things. Consciousness is a twofold representation: a representation of the object, and a representation of its difference from others. Consciousness is related like light in the corporeal world, which discloses to us the difference of bodies.",[]],"meier:14":["If we are conscious of a representation, then we are conscious of it either merely considered as a whole, so that we distinguish nothing from one another in it itself; or we are also conscious of the manifold in it. In the first case we have an indistinct or a confused cognition (*cognitio indistincta et confusa*), but in the other a distinct one (*cognitio distincta*); for example, if we see a human being from afar, then we have an indistinct cognition of his face as long as we do not catch sight of the parts and features of the face. But if he comes nearer to us, and we begin to become aware of his eyes, his nose and the features of his face, then we attain a distinct cognition of his face.",[]],"meier:15":["That from which a thing, whether it be either a cognition or the object of one, can be cognized, is the ground of it (*ratio*), and what can be cognized from the ground is the sequence of it (*rationatum*). Light is the ground of the visibility of bodies, and this visibility is a sequence of light. The interconnection of things (*nexus, consequentia*) consists in this, when the one is the ground of the other, or contains it in itself. The ground of a thing is either so constituted that we can derive our whole cognition of it from it, in such a way that besides it nothing further is required in order to cognize everything that is encountered in the thing; or it is not so constituted. The former is the sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*), and the latter the insufficient ground (*ratio insufficiens*).",[]],"meier:16":["Everything that is possible and actual has a ground, and everything also has a sufficient ground.",[]],"meier:17":["If we cognize something, then we cognize it either in a distinct way from grounds, or not. If the first is the case, then we have a rational cognition (*cognitio rationalis*). For such a cognition three things are required: 1) a cognition of a thing, 2) a cognition of its ground, and 3) a distinct cognition of the interconnection of the thing with its ground. For example: if I cognize that all human beings can err because they have a limited understanding, and I think: whoever has a limited understanding can err; now all human beings have a limited understanding, therefore they can all err: then I have a rational cognition of the truth that all human beings can err.",[]],"meier:18":["Any cognition whatever, insofar as it is not rational, is called a common or a historical cognition (*cognitio vulgaris, historica*). All things can be cognized historically, and one may even cognize their grounds; as long as one does not distinctly have insight into the interconnection of the sequences with their grounds, so long one has only a merely historical cognition.",[]],"meier:19":["A more perfect historical cognition is a beautiful cognition (*cognitio pulcra, aesthetica*), and the beautiful sciences occupy themselves with the rules through whose observance historical cognition is beautified.",[]],"meier:20":["Although historical cognition is very different from rational cognition §.17. 18, in such a way that the most beautiful historical cognition of all does not even deserve to be called a rational cognition §.19; yet the former is indispensable to the latter, in that a human being cannot attain a rational cognition of a thing if he does not previously possess a historical cognition of it.",[]],"meier:21":["Learned and philosophical cognition (*cognitio erudita et philosophica*) is a rational cognition which is perfect in a higher or more noticeable degree.",[]],"meier:22":["If the manifold in a cognition agrees toward one aim, or contains the sufficient ground of it: then therein consists the perfection of the cognition (*perfectio cognitionis*). The perfections of cognition take place in it either insofar as it is distinct or insofar as it is indistinct §.14. The former are called the logical perfections of cognition (*perfectio cognitionis logica*), and the latter its beauties (*pulcritudo et perfectio aesthetica cognitionis*). E.g. mathematical certainty is a logical perfection, and painterly vividness a beauty of cognition.",[]],"meier:23":["Insofar as a cognition is not perfect, insofar it is an imperfect cognition (*imperfectio cognitionis*). The imperfections of cognition take place in it either insofar as it is distinct, or insofar as it is indistinct §.14. The former are called the logical imperfections of cognition (*imperfectio cognitionis logica*), and the latter its uglinesses (*deformitas, imperfectio cognitionis aesthetica*). E.g. the swinish and smutty in everyday jokes is an ugliness of cognition; but a false syllogism is a logical imperfection of it.",[]],"meier:24":["Learned cognition must be adorned with the perfections of cognition §.21. 22. Consequently it possesses either merely the logical perfections of cognition, in that it is either not beautiful at all or perhaps even ugly at the same time; or it possesses, besides the logical perfections, the beauties of cognition §.22. 23. The former is a merely learned cognition (*cognitio mere erudita*), and the latter a cognition which is beautiful and learned at the same time (*cognitio aesthetico-logica*). The latter is more perfect than the first, and the first must not be sought alone.",[]],"meier:25":["The more we cognize, the more perfect is our cognition §.22. The first perfection of learned cognition thus consists in its extensiveness (*vastitas, ubertas cognitionis eruditae*), which is ascribed to a cognition insofar as it represents many objects to us.",[]],"meier:26":["The greater and more important our cognition is, the more perfect it is, because a great thing comprehends much in itself §.22. The second perfection of learned cognition accordingly consists in its magnitude and importance (*dignitas, magnitudo et maiestas cognitionis eruditae*), which belongs to a cognition insofar as it is great and important. E.g. the cognition of God is more important than the cognition of the clothes of the Romans.",[]],"meier:27":["Because a false cognition is no cognition at all, the truth of cognition (*veritas cognitionis eruditae*) is the third perfection of it. It can be called the fundamental perfection of cognition, because without it cognition is no cognition at all, and thus also capable of no perfection.",[]],"meier:28":["Since in a distinct representation we represent more to ourselves than in an indistinct one §.14, the distinctness of learned cognition is the fourth perfection of it §.25.",[]],"meier:29":["The consciousness of the truth of a cognition is its certainty (*certitudo subiective spectata*). Now since both the truth of cognition and also the consciousness of it is a perfection §.13. 17, the certainty of learned cognition is the fifth perfection of it.",[]],"meier:30":["A learned cognition is practical insofar as it contributes its part to the arrangement of our free actions (*cognitio erudita practica*), and therein consists the sixth perfection of it §.22.",[]],"meier:31":["The more extensive, important, correct, distinct, certain and practical a learned cognition is, the more perfect it is §.25-30. Now since every rational human being must everywhere strive after the greatest perfection that is possible to him; he must, if he endeavors to attain a learned cognition, seek 1) to reach all logical perfections of it, 2) each of them in the most possible degree, and besides this also 3) the beauties of cognition §.22.",[]],"meier:32":["Whoever accordingly wants to attain the most perfect learned cognition of all must not be content with a merely learned cognition §.24. 31. But although not all his learned representations can be beautiful at the same time, yet his learned cognition, considered as a whole, must at the same time be a beautiful cognition, if it is otherwise to be improved in as high a degree as is possible.",[]],"meier:33":["A learned cognition can 1) be logically perfect and imperfect at the same time. E.g. a correct, distinct and certain cognition can have the fault that it is not practical; 2) be logically perfect in a higher degree than imperfect, or more imperfect than perfect; 3) be logically perfect and beautiful or ugly at the same time; 4) logically perfect and neither beautiful nor ugly; 5) logically imperfect and at the same time beautiful or ugly §.22. 23.",[]],"meier:34":["Whoever wants to attain a learned cognition which is at the same time beautiful §.32 must 1) not make it logically perfect in such a way and to such a degree that all beauty of it is thereby prevented; 2) he must not beautify it in such a fashion and to such a degree that the required logical perfection of it is thereby made impossible; 3) he must seek chiefly to obtain the logical perfections, and he must apply the beauties only more sparingly, as an ornament.",[]],"meier:35":["If some perfections in learned cognition cannot be attained at the same time, then one must let go of the smaller and less necessary perfections in order to obtain the greater and more necessary ones. One must accordingly, in a learned cognition which is not to be merely learned, often relax logical strictness in trifles in order to attain the greater beauty of cognition.",[]],"meier:36":["The imperfections of learned cognition are either lacks or faults. A lack of learned cognition (*defectus cognitionis eruditae*) arises when certain rules of its perfection are not observed and also not transgressed. E.g. if a principal concept is not defined at all, then the rules of definitions are neither observed nor transgressed. A fault of learned cognition (*vitium cognitionis eruditae*) arises when the rules of its perfection are transgressed. E.g. if one defines a concept falsely. Although one must avoid all lacks and faults, yet one must guard more against the latter, because one can say that every fault is connected with a lack, and a fault is accordingly a greater imperfection than a mere lack.",[]],"meier:37":["Common and historical cognition can be much more perfect than merely learned cognition, namely if it is very beautiful §.22.23. E.g. an unlearned general and minister can possess a much more perfect cognition than a learned and pedantic bookworm. This advantage belongs to common cognition only contingently, if learned cognition is not as perfect as it could and should be.",[]],"meier:38":["Learned cognition is always necessarily more perfect than common cognition, if they are equal to one another in the remaining respects §.18. 21.",[]],"meier:39":["Although common cognition is very useful, and in infinitely many cases sufficient to promote our welfare, indeed although many a common cognition can contingently be more useful than many a learned one: yet learned cognition is necessarily more useful than common cognition, if they are equal to one another in the remaining respects. For 1) since it is more perfect §.38, it also improves the cognitive power in a higher degree than common cognition; 2) for that very reason it procures a greater gratification than common cognition; 3) it is more in conformity with and more becoming to the character of humanity than common cognition; 4) it promotes the discovery of new truths more than common cognition; and 5) it can be applied and exercised much more skillfully and better than common cognition.",[]],"meier:40":["A learned cognition which is at the same time beautiful procures all the utilities of learned cognition §.39, and all the utilities of beautiful cognition. And since at the same time it prevents all the harm of merely learned and of merely beautiful cognition: it is, among all species of human cognition, the most useful and most serviceable cognition. Of the extensiveness of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:41":["The imperfection of learned cognition which is opposed to its extensiveness §.25 is the paltriness of learned cognition (*angustia eruditae cognitionis*), and it always arises from ignorance (*ignorantia*) or from the complete lack of cognition of things and their grounds. In the measure in which the extensiveness of a human being's learned cognition increases, his ignorance decreases, and the greater a human being's ignorance is, the paltrier is his learned cognition.",[]],"meier:42":["To the extensiveness of learned cognition a twofold ignorance is opposed: 1) a total ignorance (*ignorantia totalis*), if we do not even have a historical cognition of a thing; and 2) an ignorance of the grounds of things (*ignorantia rationum*), with which an excellent historical cognition of them can still take place.",[]],"meier:43":["The ignorance of a human being is 1) an absolutely necessary and unavoidable ignorance (*ignorantia absolute necessaria et invincibilis*), which he cannot avoid on account of the limitations of his cognitive power; and 2) a voluntary one (*ignorantia arbitraria et vincibilis*), the opposed cognition of which he could attain if he wanted.",[]],"meier:44":["The sum total of all those things which a human being can cognize in a learned way, without detriment to the rest of his whole perfection, is the horizon, or the circle of vision, of his learned cognition (*horizon seu sphaera cognitionis eruditae*). All things are thus excluded from it with respect to which a human being must remain ignorant necessarily or voluntarily §.43.",[]],"meier:45":["A thing is raised above the horizon of human learned cognition (*res supra horizontem eruditae cognitionis humanae posita*) if the ignorance of it in a human being is absolutely necessary, although it is not unworthy of a learned cognition. The human understanding is too weak to be in a position to cognize these important things in a learned way. Now although one must not despise such things as go beyond our horizon, and although one must, without sufficient ground, pass nothing off as a thing that is raised above the horizon of our cognition; yet one must seek as far as possible to avoid the vain and harmful striving after a learned cognition of such things as go beyond our horizon.",[]],"meier:46":["A thing is lowered beneath the horizon of human learned cognition (*res infra horizontem eruditae cognitionis humanae posita*) which could indeed be cognized by a human being in a learned way, but which is not great enough to be worthy of such a cognition. Just as one must not, without sufficient ground, pass a thing off as something which is lowered beneath the horizon of human learned cognition, so one must also not take pains to cognize in a learned way such things as are actually lowered beneath the horizon of human learned cognition. Otherwise one makes oneself ridiculous and contemptible, and one neglects on that account the learned cognition of more important and more necessary things.",[]],"meier:47":["A thing is outside the horizon of learned human cognition (*res extra horizontem cognitionis humanae eruditae posita*) which could indeed be cognized by a human being in a learned way, which is also not unworthy of a human learned cognition, but the learned cognition of which would hinder a human being in his remaining occupations. No one must, without sufficient ground, pass a thing off as something which is encountered outside the horizon of his learned cognition; but also no one must strive after a learned cognition of such things as are situated outside the horizon of his learned cognition, because otherwise he would meddle in others' affairs, and on that account neglect his own work.",[]],"meier:48":["All those things which are neither raised above the horizon of human learned cognition, nor lowered beneath it, nor encountered outside it, are situated within the domain of the horizon of human learned cognition (*res intra horizontem cognitionis humanae eruditae posita*), and they make up the learned horizon §.44.",[]],"meier:49":["The most extensive learned cognition of all of a human being consists in the learned cognition of all things that are situated within the domain of his horizon §.48. 25. Now since every learned person must attain the most extensive learned cognition that is possible to him; he must seek to measure out the boundaries and the domain of his horizon as exactly as the weakness of human beings allows §.44.",[]],"meier:50":["And even if a human being should have attained the most extensive learned cognition of all, yet much necessary ignorance still remains, which redounds neither to his honor nor to his disgrace §.43. But as regards voluntary ignorance, it is either praiseworthy or blameworthy. Praiseworthy ignorance (*ignorantia laudabilis*) is the ignorance of such things as are encountered beneath and outside the horizon of learned cognition §.46.47.",[]],"meier:51":["Blameworthy ignorance (*ignorantia illaudabilis*) is the ignorance of such things as are encountered within the horizon of learned cognition §.48. Whoever thus wants to attain the most extensive learned cognition of all must seek to avoid all blameworthy ignorance, and no other ignorance besides §.49.",[]],"meier:52":["It is a ridiculous folly of many paltry heads among the learned when they count their blameworthy ignorance to themselves as a merit, and, with Socrates, who possessed a very extensive learnedness, pretend of themselves: that they know nothing, except that they know nothing.",[]],"meier:53":["The more things someone cognizes in a learned way, the more extensive is his learned cognition. A higher or noticeable, and consequently rarer, degree of the extensiveness of learned cognition is called much-knowing (*polyhistoria*). It is undeniably a great perfection of learned cognition, if only one is not misled by greed for polyhistory 1) to overstep the limitations of one's learned horizon, and 2) to neglect all too noticeably the remaining perfections of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:54":["Whoever wants to make his learned cognition extensive enough must 1) learn many principal parts of learnedness. E.g. philosophy, theology, history, philology, etc. 2) From each principal part many parts, e.g. from philosophy the doctrine of reason, metaphysics, physics, right of nature, etc. 3) Each of these parts he must in turn learn extensively, e.g. the doctrine of reason, and 4) of each single truth he must in turn seek to attain an extensive learned cognition, e.g. of the omniscience of God.",[]],"meier:55":["An extensive learned cognition is exhaustive and complete (*completa cognitio erudita*) if it is sufficient for its aims, or if we cognize in a learned way as much as the whole aim of our learned cognition requires. Extensiveness without exhaustiveness is not perfect enough, and one must accordingly learn to distinguish the foremost truths from the side matters in each object of learned cognition, so that through the learned cognition of the former one may attain a complete learnedness.",[]],"meier:56":["Whoever studies merely in a cavalier-like way, in that he merely runs hastily through an extensive outline of all parts of learnedness; goes through a few parts of learnedness indeed somewhat more extensively, but yet quite briefly; and perhaps here and there investigates some particular materials because they especially please him on account of some cause or other: his learned cognition is a poor skeleton of learnedness, and deserves a very slight regard §.55.",[]],"meier:57":["Because it is impossible that a human being attain an exhaustively extensive learned cognition all at once, one must constantly seek to expand learned cognition: so that one does not go backward in it by omitting to go further forward.",[]],"meier:58":["Because, through the expansion of learned cognition, the cognitive powers are at the same time made more capable, one need not fear that one will overload one's head through the constant expansion of learned cognition, if only in this occupation one does not overstep the boundaries of the learned horizon.",[]],"meier:59":["Although art is long and human life short, this consideration must rather incite us to learn as much as is possible with the proper haste, than that it should instill in us a timidity and discouragement which hinders us in the expansion of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:60":["In order that one not regard the expansion of learned cognition as unnecessary and useless, one must not set before oneself too small, slight and base an end, which one endeavors to reach through one's learned cognition.",[]],"meier:61":["Since it is naturally necessary that we human beings forget much, one must for that very reason expand learned cognition greatly, so that one can forget much and notwithstanding still retain much. The truths which we forget have in any case not been wholly useless, because they have increased the degree of our cognitive powers.",[]],"meier:62":["The extensiveness of learned cognition arises from a twofold source, both of which must be together: 1) from the extensiveness and the rich content of the object, if it contains much in itself which can be cognized by a human being in a learned way (*vastitas obiectiva*); and 2) from the extension of the cognitive powers (*vastitas subiectiva*), by virtue of which one is in a position to cognize much of a thing in a learned way.",[]],"meier:63":["Because it is impossible that a human being learn all parts of learnedness in an equal degree of perfection, each must select for himself one of them with which he occupies himself the most, and of which he endeavors to attain the most perfect learned cognition. This is his principal science (*scientia eruditi principalis*). Each must choose as his principal science that part of learnedness 1) which can be cognized by human beings in a very high degree of perfection, and 2) for which he possesses the most skill, rational desire, and other means of furtherance.",[]],"meier:64":["Each must 1) learn his principal science most extensively and exhaustively, and 2) investigate all other parts of learnedness in relation to his principal science. The more closely another part of learnedness is combined with the principal science, the more perfectly and extensively one must seek to cognize it in a learned way §.63.",[]],"meier:65":["The paltriness of learned cognition causes, among others, a threefold harm: 1) learned pedantry and charlatanry (*pedantismus et charlataneria eruditorum*), by virtue of which one esteems all too highly the little that one understands, and wholly despises everything else; 2) a ridiculous conceit and a vain arrogance; and 3) little learnedness can make a human being unhappy his whole life long, in that he can fall into just such circumstances in which that which he has learned is not demanded of him, and that which he has not learned is expected of him. Of the magnitude of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:66":["The magnitude of learned cognition requires not only a great object, but the cognition must also be great enough for the object §.26. The greater the object, and the more proportioned the cognition is, the greater is the learned cognition.",[]],"meier:67":["The object of learned cognition is, considered by itself, great (*magnitudo eruditae cognitionis obiectiva absoluta*) if it contains much manifold in itself which can be cognized in a learned way. For example: God, philosophy, history, etc.",[]],"meier:68":["The object of learned cognition is great with respect to its sequences (*magnitudo eruditae cognitionis obiectiva respectiva*) 1) if it is important (*res digna, gravis*), that is, if it has great sequences, e.g. if the happiness of human beings, the welfare of the fatherland, etc. rests on it; 2) if it is fruitful (*res foecunda*), that is, if many sequences flow from it. E.g. godliness, for it is profitable for all things.",[]],"meier:69":["Whoever wants to make his learned cognition truly great must seek to cognize nothing but absolutely great, important and fruitful things. The more the object contains in itself, the greater its sequences are, and the more sequences it has, the greater, more important and more fruitful it is; and the greater, more important and more fruitful is the learned cognition of it, insofar namely as one considers it with respect to its object.",[]],"meier:70":["The magnitude of learned cognition which belongs to it itself (*magnitudo cognitionis eruditae subiectiva*) consists in this, when it is proportioned to the magnitude of its object §.66. The greater the object is, the more extensive, correct, distinct, certain and practical must the learned cognition of it be, and the more time and diligence must be spent on attaining it. The less great the object is, the less perfect must the learned cognition be, and the less time and diligence must be spent on attaining it.",[]],"meier:71":["The imperfection of learned cognition which is opposed to its magnitude is called its triviality (*parvitas, vilitas cognitionis eruditae*); and it arises either from the triviality of the object, or from this, when the learned cognition is not proportioned to the object §.66.",[]],"meier:72":["The object of learned cognition is, considered by itself, small (*parvitas cognitionis eruditae obiectiva absoluta*) if it contains little in itself which can be cognized in a learned way. E.g. the hairpins of Roman womenfolk.",[]],"meier:73":["The object of learned cognition is, with respect to its sequences, small (*parvitas cognitionis eruditae obiectiva respectiva*) 1) if it is not important (*res leviores*), that is, if it has no great sequences, e.g. the doctrine of the composition of bodies out of monads; 2) if it is unfruitful (*res infoecunda, sterilis*), that is, if it does not have many sequences, e.g. the doctrine of the incorporeality of the soul.",[]],"meier:74":["If a thing is small in all respects §.72. 73, then it is worthy of no learned cognition: for it is lowered beneath the horizon of it §.46. The less a thing contains in itself, the fewer and smaller sequences it has, the smaller it is. Among these trifles one must also count the vulgar and base things (*res plebeiae, abiectae*), the learned investigation of which would even be contrary to honorable morals, e.g. the outbursts of vices among the rabble.",[]],"meier:75":["The triviality of learned cognition which belongs to it itself (*parvitas cognitionis eruditae subiectiva*) consists in this, when it is not proportioned to the objects §.71. Consequently 1) if one seeks a more perfect learned cognition with more effort, and spends more time on it, than the object deserves; and 2) if one does not cognize an object as perfectly, does not investigate it with as much effort, and does not spend as much time on it, as it deserves. E.g. whoever assails errors more learnedly and diligently than vices, his learned cognition is not proportioned enough.",[]],"meier:76":["Whoever wants to make his learned cognition truly perfect must 1) if it is possible for him, choose as his principal science the greatest §.67. 68 part of learnedness §.63; 2) the greater a part of learnedness is, the more effort and diligence he must spend on it, and the more perfect must the learned cognition of it be; 3) the greater the truths are, the more effort and time he must spend on them, and the more perfect must his learned cognition of them be.",[]],"meier:77":["Whoever possesses no mature and manly judgment cannot possibly judge of the true worth of things, and it is thus impossible for him to reach the magnitude of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:78":["Because all objects of learned cognition stand in a universal combination, they are all infinitely great, important and fruitful §.67. 68. An object is thus called a trifle only in relation to us, because it is impossible for us to cognize its magnitude, importance and fruitfulness in a learned way.",[]],"meier:79":["Just as a learned cognition together with its object is not to be esteemed great because this or that small mind makes a great fuss about it; so one must also not take it to be small because it is passed off by that very same one as a thing of slight worth.",[]],"meier:80":["A learned cognition is not for that reason a trifle of slight worth and unfruitful because this or that dry and unfruitful head is not capable of deriving many and important sequences from it. The unfruitfulness of the soil is not to be imputed to the plant.",[]],"meier:81":["The application of a great learned cognition to small, ridiculous, vulgar and contemptible cases can indeed contingently make it ridiculous, contemptible and vulgar; but it must not on that account be counted among the trifles of slight worth.",[]],"meier:82":["If the great objects of learned cognition are represented and presented by this or that one in a contemptible and ridiculous way, then they must not on that account be counted among the contemptible trifles.",[]],"meier:83":["A learned person must not, through his own fault, through wretched application, and through ridiculous representations and expressions, make learned cognition ridiculous and contemptible §.81. 82.",[]],"meier:84":["If a learned cognition should not be important and fruitful in our present circumstances, because we do not yet have insight into its consequences, then it can nevertheless become important and fruitful in the future, and it is therefore on that account no trifle. We must also plant trees for our descendants, whose fruits we do not enjoy.",[]],"meier:85":["One disgraces oneself if one does not hold abstract cognition, subtleties and profound distinctions to be great and important matters, because they demand much laborious reflection.",[]],"meier:86":["The intermediate truths in an extensive doctrinal system have indeed not much significance in themselves; but because without them one cannot cognize the principal truths in a properly learned way, they are on that account great and worthy objects of our learned cognition.",[]],"meier:87":["A learned investigation is not important on account of the fact that it demands much effort, diligence and time, and because it cannot be undertaken without great learning: for there are also very difficult farces.",[]],"meier:88":["A part of learning is not to be counted among trifles on account of the fact that it is filled with many trifles.",[]],"meier:89":["A learned cognition which is not great in one respect can be great in another respect, and it must therefore not be held to be a trifle §.67. 68. E.g., a cognition can be not important, but nevertheless fruitful.",[]],"meier:90":["Because different learned persons can have chosen for themselves different principal sciences §.63, a learned investigation can be great with respect to the one, and small with respect to the other.",[]],"meier:91":["A great mind possesses the aptitude to have only a great learned cognition. His inclination toward it and his aversion to all trifles compel him always first to investigate the greatness of the matter, before he takes pains to cognize it in a learned way, so that he may know whether it is worthy of a learned cognition, and of which degree of the perfection of that cognition.on the truth of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:92":["A false or incorrect cognition (*cognitio falsa*), is a cognition which is no cognition, and yet seems to be a cognition. A false learned cognition (*cognitio erudita falsa*), only seems to be a learned cognition, and it is either no cognition at all, or at least no learned cognition. E.g., those who assume the vegetative soul of plants, and derive the growth of plants from it, have a false learned cognition.",[]],"meier:93":["A true or correct cognition (*cognitio vera*), not only seems to be a cognition, but it also is one in fact. A true learned cognition (*cognitio erudita vera*), not only seems to be a learned cognition, but it also deserves this name in fact. E.g., whoever convincingly demonstrates the actuality of God from the contingency of this world has a true learned cognition.",[]],"meier:94":["The characteristics of the correctness and incorrectness of cognition (*criteria veritatis et falsitatis cognitionis*), are the grounds from which it can be cognized that a cognition is true, or that it is false. And they are either present in the cognition itself, or not. The former are the internal, and the latter the external characteristics of correctness and incorrectness (*criteria interna et externa veritatis et falsitatis cognitionis*).",[]],"meier:95":["The first internal characteristic of the truth of a cognition consists in its internal possibility (*possibilitas cognitionis interna*), insofar as it represents something possible, and contains nothing that is contrary to each other, even if one considers it entirely alone. The internal impossibility of cognition (*impossibilitas cognitionis interna*), when it represents nothing, and when the manifold in it conflicts with each other, is therefore the first internal characteristic that it is false §.94.93. 92.",[]],"meier:96":["The second internal characteristic of the truth of a cognition consists in its being possible in an interconnection (*possibilitas cognitionis hypothetica*). Consequently 1) if it is a sequence of correct grounds, and 2) a ground of correct sequences, §.94. 93. Accordingly a cognition is true if it is not impossible, and is in accordance with the principle of sufficient ground §.16.",[]],"meier:97":["A cognition is false if it is impossible in the interconnection (*impossibilitas in nexu*); consequently if it has no or false grounds, and no or false sequences §.96. And this is the second internal characteristic of its incorrectness §.94.",[]],"meier:98":["We must not assume: 1) that a cognition is true, because we do not become aware of any internal impossibility in it; 2) that it is false, because we do not become aware of any internal possibility in it; 3) that a cognition is true whose groundlessness and false grounds and sequences we do not become aware of; 4) that a cognition is false of which we cognize no correct grounds and sequences. For we human beings are not omniscient.",[]],"meier:99":["If we represent a thing to ourselves otherwise than it is, then we believe we cognize it, and yet do not cognize it. Accordingly our cognition is false§.92. Moreover all possible things have an internal possibility, grounds and sequences §.15.16. Accordingly the logical truth of cognition (*veritas cognitionis logica*), consists in its agreement with its object, and its logical incorrectness (*falsitas cognitionis logica*) in its not agreeing with its object §.95. 96. 97.",[]],"meier:100":["If a cognition contains nothing true, then it is entirely false (*falsitas totalis*), and if it contains nothing false, then it is entirely true (*veritas totalis*). But it can be true and false at the same time, but in different respects, (*veritas et falsitas partialis*). Accordingly one must not accept an extensive cognition entirely, because perhaps even the most and most important in it is true; and not reject it entirely, because perhaps even the most and most important in it is false. For example, the doctrinal systems of different religions.",[]],"meier:101":["The more and the more manifold pieces a cognition contains in itself which are possible together, the greater these pieces are: and the more and greater correct grounds and sequences it has, the more correct is the cognition §.95. 96. Whoever therefore wants to improve his learned cognition as far as possible must seek to attain in it the greatest possible degree of truth.",[]],"meier:102":["A cognition which is correct in a higher degree is called an exact cognition (*cognitio exacta, exasciata*); but one which is true in a smaller degree is called a crude one (*cognitio crassa*). All crude cognition must be avoided, and indeed so much the more, the cruder it is, or the more and more important falsehood it contains. On the contrary the perfect learned cognition must be as exact as possible §.101, and the greater the objects are, the more exactly one must seek to cognize them §.70. But the smaller they are, the less correct may their cognition be §.70.",[]],"meier:103":["Learned cognition can be false in a threefold way: 1) if the cognition of the things is false, although the cognition of the grounds is correct; 2) if the cognition of the grounds is false, although the cognition of the things is correct; 3) if the representation of the interconnection between true grounds and sequences is incorrect §.100. A true learned cognition must therefore be a correct cognition of the things, of the grounds, and of their interconnection at the same time §.21.",[]],"meier:104":["By truths(*veritates*) one also understands the true cognition itself, and then all truths are either dogmatic (*veritates dogmaticae*), or historical (*veritates historicae*). The former can and must be cognized from the internal characteristics of truth, but the latter only from the external ones. E.g., that there is a GOD is a dogmatic truth; but that David is the second king of the Israelites is a historical one. A doctrinal system (*systema*) is a multitude of dogmatic truths which are combined with one another in such a way that taken together they make up a cognition which one can consider as a whole.",[]],"meier:105":["The more truths occur in a doctrinal system, the greater and more correct they are, the more perfect it is §.104. For the most exact truth of a doctrinal system it is required: 1) that all its parts are correct in the most exact way; 2) that none contradicts the other; and 3) that they are all combined, in that each one is either a ground of the rest, or a sequence, or both at the same time §.95. 96. Because there is no truth outside doctrinal systems §.96. 104, learned cognition must be systematic, if it is otherwise to be perfectly correct.",[]],"meier:106":["All truths are either merely aesthetic truths (*veritates mere aestheticae*), which must be cognized merely beautifully, and with which learned cognition must never occupy itself; or merely learned (*veritates mere eruditae*), which can only be cognized in a learned way, and with which alone learned cognition must not occupy itself, for it would otherwise be merely learned §.32; or both at the same time (*veritates aesthetico-eruditae*), and these are the foremost object of a properly perfect learned cognition.",[]],"meier:107":["Because truth is not the only perfection of learned cognition, a learned person can never be excused if he occupies himself with a learned investigation merely on account of the fact that it is true. It is not good to think and to say all truths.",[]],"meier:108":["Each must seek to cognize his principal science most exactly §.102, and in the greatest possible degree systematically §.105 §.63.",[]],"meier:109":["Error (*cognitio erronea, error*) consists in our holding false cognition to be true, and true cognition to be false. Consequently 1) every erroneous cognition is false §.99; 2) not every false cognition is erroneous, namely when we cognize that it is false §.99; 3) error arises from false cognition. If we had no false cognition at all, then we could also have no errors. Error is worse than merely false cognition, for it is a hidden poison. Learned cognition can therefore be erroneous in a threefold way §.103.",[]],"meier:110":["If we transgress the rules of the 98th paragraph, then error arises §.109. The first source of all errors is accordingly ignorance §.41, when there is united with it precipitancy, by virtue of which we deny that of which we have no cognition.",[]],"meier:111":["Error is either an avoidable one (*error vincibilis*), or an unavoidable one (*error invincibilis*). The former arises from an avoidable, and the latter from an unavoidable ignorance §.43. The former is only a blameworthy blemish of learned cognition, but the latter cannot and may not be avoided.",[]],"meier:112":["The more extensive, important and fruitful the error is, and the more easily it could have been prevented, the greater it is. The greater the error is, the more it disgraces learned cognition, and the more carefully it must be prevented. Consequently each must seek especially to prevent the errors in his principal science §.108.",[]],"meier:113":["A cognition is manifestly false (*cognitio aperte falsa*), if its incorrectness is discovered merely from considering it, e.g., a quadrangular triangle. But if, in order to discover its incorrectness, one must undertake a more extensive investigation, then it is in a hidden way false (*cognitio cuius falsitas latet*), e.g., matter can think. An error through which a manifestly false cognition is accepted as true is a preposterous, absurd and stupid error (*cognitio absurda, absona*). Not all errors are absurdities.",[]],"meier:114":["All truths are either absolutely necessary (*veritates absolute necessariae*), or contingent truths (*veritates contingentes*). With the former it is entirely impossible that they should be false, e.g., there is a GOD. But the latter could also be false, e.g., this world is actual. Because error is easier to avoid with the former, it is greater than error in contingent truths §.112.on the clarity of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:115":["A mark, a characteristic of cognition and of things (*nota, character cognitionis et rei*) is that in the cognition or the things which, when it is cognized, is the ground on account of which we are conscious of them; or they are the distinguishing pieces of cognition and its objects. Where therefore there is a consciousness, there marks are cognized §.13. E.g., reason is a mark of the human being, and of the cognition which we have of him.",[]],"meier:116":["Marks in turn have their marks §.115. Consequently all marks of a thing are either immediate marks (*notae immediatae, proximae*), or mediate (*notae mediatae, remotae*). The latter are marks of marks, but the former only marks of the thing, although they are not marks of its marks. E.g., reason is an immediate mark of the human being, but because reason is a faculty of having distinct insight into the interconnection of things, the faculty is a mediate mark of the human being. Consciousness rests on the immediate marks §.13.",[]],"meier:117":["Marks are either negative (*notae negativae*) or affirmative(*notae affirmativae, positivae*). Through the former we represent to ourselves something as absent in the thing, and we thereby cognize only what it is not; e.g., the irrationality of irrational animals. Through the latter we represent to ourselves something as present in the thing, and we cognize through them what the thing is, e.g., the reason of human beings. Although both kinds can cause a consciousness, the affirmative are nevertheless better marks than the negative.",[]],"meier:118":["Marks are either more important (*notae graviores*), or lesser marks (*notae leviores*). The former disclose a greater, and the latter a lesser difference of the thing. Either fruitful (*notae foecundae*), or unfruitful marks (*notae infoecundae*). The former disclose a manifold difference from many things, but the latter not. Reason is an important and fruitful mark of a human being; but that he is a thing, a lesser and more unfruitful one. The more important and fruitful the marks accordingly are, the more they promote consciousness.",[]],"meier:119":["Since marks are grounds §.115, they are either sufficient grounds of consciousness, or insufficient grounds §.15. The former are sufficient (*notae sufficientes*), and the latter insufficient marks (*notae insufficientes*). The former are better marks than the latter. The faculty of thinking is an insufficient mark of a spirit, but that it has understanding, a sufficient one.",[]],"meier:120":["Marks are either absolutely necessary and unalterable (*notae absolute necessariae et invariabiles*), or contingent and alterable marks (*notae contingentes et variabiles*). The former are so constituted that without them the thing cannot be represented, e.g., the reason of the human being; but the latter are so constituted that without them the thing can nevertheless be represented, e.g., the actual thinking of the human being. The former are better marks than the latter.",[]],"meier:121":["Marks in a thing can either be represented without considering it in interconnection with other things outside it, or not. The latter are external marks or relations (*notae externae, relationes*), e.g., the dominion of a human being. The former are internal marks (*notae internae*). The latter are either necessary or contingent §.120. The latter are called contingent properties (*modi*), e.g., the learning of a human being. The former are either the grounds of all the remaining determinations, or not. The latter are the attributes (*attributa*), e.g., the faculty of thinking in a human being. The former are called the essential pieces (*essentialia*), e.g., the reason of the human being. The sum total of all essential pieces is the essence (*essentia*).",[]],"meier:122":["The more marks of a thing we cognize, the greater these marks are, and the more perfectly we cognize the marks, the greater and better is consciousness §.13. Consequently the affirmative, important, fruitful, internal, necessary and sufficient marks cause a greater and better consciousness than those opposed to them§.117-121.",[]],"meier:123":["A representation, insofar as we are conscious of it, is called a thought (*cogitatio*). Consequently not every representation and cognition is a thought. And what has been proved of consciousness holds also of thinking §.115.116. 122.",[]],"meier:124":["A cognition either contains as many marks as are required for consciousness, or not. The former is a clear cognition (*cognitio clara*), which is the same as the thought, and the cognition of which we are conscious §.123. The latter is an obscure cognition (*cognitio obscura*), which is therefore neither a thought, nor connected with consciousness §.123. The former is more perfect than the latter. If we cannot recall a word, and yet it seems as if it were running about before our mouth, then we have an obscure representation of it.",[]],"meier:125":["Obscure cognition is either absolutely obscure (*cognitio absolute obscura*), or relatively (*cognitio relative obscura*). The former would have to be so obscure that it would be absolutely impossible to make it clear. No true cognition is absolutely obscure, and one must accordingly hold nothing to be absolutely obscure. All true obscure cognition is only relatively obscure, that is, the powers of this or that thinking being are not sufficient to make it clear. And then either the object is chiefly to blame for this obscurity, or the human being to whom the cognition is obscure. The former is the obscurity of the things (*cognitio obiective obscura*), and the latter the obscurity in the head of the one to whom the cognition is so obscure (*cognitio subiective obscura*). E.g., the things which are too far removed from us, or are too small, are to blame that we sense them obscurely. But whoever finds the doctrine of reason obscure is himself to blame for it. Finally an obscure cognition is either entirely obscure (*cognitio totaliter obscura*), if we are not conscious of it at all, or only in part (*cognitio partialiter obscura*), if we are conscious and also not conscious of it at the same time. All our clear cognition is always in part obscure to us, because we are capable of thinking through no single thing completely.",[]],"meier:126":["One human being can cognize what the other does not cognize, and accordingly the one can cognize marks which are unknown to the other §.115. Consequently the one can cognize clearly what the other cognizes only obscurely §.124. Accordingly one must not infer: 1) what is clear to me is also clear to others; 2) what is obscure to me is also obscure to others; 3) what is clear to me now will also be clear to me in the future; 4) what is obscure to me now will also remain obscure to me in the future.",[]],"meier:127":["The more marks that are required for consciousness are unknown to us, and the greater the marks unknown to us are, and the more power is required to make a cognition clear, the greater is its obscurity. Before a cognition can become clear, therefore, one must often apply much time and effort to diminish its obscurity.",[]],"meier:128":["Because we have much clear cognition whose marks we indeed cognize §.115, but not clearly, there are actually present in our soul obscure representations, which make up the materials out of which the soul gradually composes its clear cognition.",[]],"meier:129":["The obscurity of cognition arises from a threefold source: 1) when a human being does not possess powers enough to make it clear. This lack of powers is either necessary or not, and in the latter case the human being either should have acquired the powers or not; 2) from the lack of attention, which arises either from a necessary lack of powers, or because we may not pay heed to a certain cognition, or because we do not pay heed to it although we should; 3) from ignorance of such things without which a certain cognition cannot become clear, whether this ignorance be necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy §.43.50. 51. All obscurity of cognition is accordingly either necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy. Consequently in the improvement of cognition one must seek to avoid only the contingent and blameworthy obscurity; and the obscurity which is necessary and praiseworthy in the one can be contingent and blameworthy in the other.",[]],"meier:130":["Obscure cognition 1) can be true, but it can also be false §.92.93. Accordingly not every obscure cognition is false; 2) is no learned cognition, insofar as it is obscure. It is an unavoidable evil in the learned cognition of human beings.",[]],"meier:131":["That action by which the obscurity of cognition is diminished, and the clarity of cognition is produced and increased, is called the unwrapping or development of cognition (*evolutio, explanatio cognitionis*), just as the opposed action is called its enwrapping (*cognitionis involutio*). For the former there is a) preliminarily required three things: 1) one investigate as far as possible whether the obscurity of the cognition one wants to develop is necessary or contingent, not culpable or culpable. In the latter case only may we venture the development. 2) One investigate whether its development belongs to the horizon of our clear cognition or not. In the first case only is this work permitted to us. 3) One investigate whether this development does not presuppose some other clear cognition without which it cannot take place. And if this matter is in fact so, then one must not undertake this work before we have not acquired this other clear cognition §.129. b) For the unwrapping itself three things are required: 1) one direct one's attention to the thing which one wants to cognize clearly; 2) one compare it with other things different from it, so that one may cognize its marks §.115; 3) one abstract from all remaining things, or one obscure them, in paying heed to the object. The more often, more strongly and longer one pays heed to a thing, with the more things one compares it, the more strongly one abstracts from other things, the better and sooner the cognition of it develops, even should one at the beginning perform this work in vain a few times.",[]],"meier:132":["Through a clear cognition we are either capable of distinguishing the object constantly and in all circumstances from all possible remaining things, or not. In the first case our cognition is exhaustively clear (*cognitio complete clara*), e.g., the representation of the color red; but in the other non-exhaustively clear (*cognitio incomplete clara*), e.g., when we indeed taste that a wine is Rhine wine, but we cannot taste of what sort it is. The former is more perfect than the latter, because the latter contains more obscurity than the former §.124, and we attain it through the cognition of the necessary, unalterable and sufficient marks §.119. 120. 121.",[]],"meier:133":["If we have a clear cognition, then we are either conscious of everything that is met with in the object, or not. If the first, then the cognition is entirely clear (*cognitio totaliter clara*). If the latter, then it is only in part clear (*cognitio partialiter clara*). No human clear cognition is entirely clear §.125, and a cognition can be exhaustively clear although it is not entirely clear §.132. Meanwhile a cognition is so much the more perfect, the more its clarity approaches total clarity§.124.",[]],"meier:134":["The more marks we cognize, consequently from the more and more agreeing things we can distinguish a thing; the greater the marks, and the better we cognize all marks; the more easily we can be conscious of a cognition and its object: the clearer is our cognition §.124, and therefore also the more perfect. One must therefore, in the improvement of a cognition, seek to attain the greatest possible degree of its clarity.",[]],"meier:135":["A cognition which is clearer through the multitude of marks is called a vivid cognition (*cognitio extensive clarior, vivida*); e.g., the melodious song of the nightingale runs resounding through the valleys. But one which is clearer through the magnitude of the marks and their clearer representation is clearer in strength (*cognitio intensive clarior*). Only the doctrine of reason occupies itself with the latter degree of clarity §.1. 17. 21.",[]],"meier:136":["If we forget marks of clear cognition, then a clearer cognition can be transformed into a less clear, and finally into an entirely obscure cognition §.124. Whoever therefore wants to forestall the enwrapping of clear cognition §.131 must forestall forgetfulness.",[]],"meier:137":["A cognition which is considered with respect to the strength of clarity is either distinct, or confused §.14. 135. In the former the marks too are clear, but in the latter obscure §.14. 115. 124. Because therefore distinctness is a manifold clarity, distinct cognition is more perfect than confused §.124.",[]],"meier:138":["Because all learned cognition is distinct §.21. 17, confused cognition, insofar as it is confused, is not learned§.137. Just as therefore the beautiful sciences occupy themselves with the improvement of the confused half of human cognition, so a learned person must improve the distinct half of it through the doctrine of reason. Obscurity and confusion are in learned cognition a necessary evil, which one either also seeks to improve incidentally or not. In the latter case there arises a merely learned cognition, and in the first a learned cognition which is at the same time beautiful §.24.",[]],"meier:139":["The action by which a certain degree of distinctness is produced in our cognition is called the analysis of cognition (*resolutio, analysis, anatomia cognitionis*). A cognition can be analyzed if it can be made distinct by some thinking being or other (*cognitio resolubilis*). But insofar as it cannot become distinct, insofar is it a cognition which cannot be analyzed (*cognitio irresolubilis*). And then it is either altogether impossible that it become distinct, or it only cannot become distinct through the powers of this or that thinking being. If the first, then it absolutely cannot be analyzed (*cognitio absolute irresolubilis*). If the latter, then it only relatively cannot be analyzed (*cognitio respective irresolubilis*). If a true cognition absolutely could not be analyzed, then its marks would have to be absolutely obscure§.137. Now since this is impossible §.120, all true cognition which cannot be analyzed is so constituted only relatively.",[]],"meier:140":["Insofar as we have a distinct cognition of a thing, insofar do we comprehend it (*concipere*). What can be cognized distinctly is comprehensible (*conceptibile*). What cannot be cognized distinctly is incomprehensible (*inconceptibile*), either absolutely (*absolute inconceptibile*), if it absolutely cannot be analyzed; or only relatively (*relative inconceptibile*), if it relatively cannot be analyzed. All possible things are only relatively incomprehensible §.139, and what we cognize in a learned way we always comprehend insofar §.21.17.",[]],"meier:141":["What we do not comprehend is not on that account an absurd thing §.140. And since a cognition can be distinct to one human being which is confused or even obscure to the other §.126, one must not infer: what we do not comprehend, others also do not comprehend; what we comprehend, others also comprehend; what I comprehend now, I will also comprehend in the future; what I do not comprehend now, I will also not comprehend in the future. These inferences hold also when the discussion is of mere possibility or impossibility.",[]],"meier:142":["If one wants to analyze a cognition, then one must 1) develop it §.131, if it should not already be clear in us beforehand. 2) One must think the clear cognition through, or gradually develop its marks, according to §.131. The more marks, and the better marks §.116-121 one develops, the better it is. 3) One reflect on the developed marks, or one represent them to oneself together as One cognition; otherwise we would think the object only piecemeal, and that is not yet a distinct cognition of the whole object. 4) One represent to oneself the developed marks in just the same interconnection and in just the same order as they are found in the object. Otherwise the distinct cognition does not agree with the object, and all disorder puts us into confusion. 5) One must obscure all remaining things and representations that have not been drawn into the reflection, or abstract from them; so that the cognition does not at once become confused again.",[]],"meier:143":["The more and greater clear marks of a thing we cognize, the better we cognize the marks, consequently the more extensive, proportioned, correct, clearer, more certain and more practical: the more distinct is the cognition §.137.25-30.",[]],"meier:144":["A distinct cognition is either entirely distinct (*cognitio totaliter distincta*), or only in part (*cognitio partialiter distincta*). The former contains no obscurity and confusion at all, but the latter contains such. No human distinct cognition is entirely distinct §.133. But it is so much the more distinct, the nearer its distinctness comes to total distinctness § 142.",[]],"meier:145":["The confusion of cognition arises, 1) from the sources of obscurity, for it consists in the obscurity of the marks §.137. 129. 2) From the necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy lack of reflection; likewise 3) of reflection; 4) from the lack of order in thinking, and 5) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy lack of abstraction §.142.",[]],"meier:146":["There is therefore not only a confusion in all our cognition which we absolutely cannot prevent; but there is also a confusion which we could prevent, but we may not, because it is met with below or outside our horizon §.146. Before we venture on the analysis of a cognition, therefore, we must investigate beforehand as far as possible whether we can or may perform it.",[]],"meier:147":["The marks of a distinct cognition are clear §.137. Therefore they are either distinct or confused §.137. In the first case we have a complete cognition (*cognitio adaequata*), but in the other an incomplete one (*cognitio inadaequata*), e.g., vice is an acquired aptitude to sin. An acquired aptitude is a facility in acting which we have obtained through practice, and we sin when we undertake evil free actions. This is a complete cognition of vice, but it would be incomplete if I had no distinct cognition of aptitude and of sin. Completeness is a manifold distinctness, and therefore a perfection of cognition §.137. Incompleteness consists in the confusion of the cognition of the marks, and therefore from the sources of confusion in general §.145. The more distinct marks a cognition contains, and the more distinct they are, the more complete is the cognition. And the most perfect learned cognition must be as complete as possible §.21.",[]],"meier:148":["Completeness arises from the analysis of the marks §.147. 142. The more remote the marks are which we again analyze, the more complete the cognition becomes §.116. 147. A higher degree of completeness is a profound cognition (*cognitio profunda, purior*). And in analyzing a cognition, we bring this business to an end when we discover the marks which we cannot analyze further. But we stop in the analysis when we do not further analyze marks which we nevertheless could analyze. Now since it would be a loathsome work if we always wanted to bring the analysis of all our distinct cognition to an end, we must always have a rational end for why we analyze a cognition, and as soon as we have attained it, we must interrupt this work for this time. It is in any case impossible that a human being avoid all incompleteness in his cognition §.144.",[]],"meier:149":["Distinct cognition is either exhaustively distinct (*cognitio complete distincta*) or non-exhaustively (*cognitio incomplete distincta*) §.132. The former is more perfect than the latter §.132. E.g., if I represent to myself a spirit as a being which can think, then I have a distinct but non-exhaustive cognition of it. But if I represent it to myself as a thing which has understanding, then my distinct cognition of a spirit is at the same time exhaustive.",[]],"meier:150":["If one wants to make a distinct cognition of a thing complete, then one must 1) seek to discover those marks which are encountered in no other thing apart from it; or 2) one must discover as many marks as, taken together, belong to no other thing. E.g., virtue is a facility in free lawful actions. Although each of these marks is also encountered in things that are not virtues, they are nevertheless, taken together, to be found in no other thing. And accordingly the sum total of these four marks makes the distinct representation of virtue into a complete distinct representation.",[]],"meier:151":["A distinct cognition is either a determinate (*cognitio determinata*) or an indeterminate cognition (*cognitio indeterminata*). The former is completely distinct, and contains not a single mark more than is absolutely necessary for completeness. E.g., a spirit is a being that has understanding. The latter either contains too few marks, and that is the incompletely distinct cognition §.149; or it contains more clear marks than are necessary for completeness, and that is an all too prolix cognition (*cognitio nimis prolixa*). E.g., a spirit is a thinking being which possesses understanding and free will. If one therefore separates from a completely distinct cognition those marks which either are not necessary for completeness, or which follow from other marks of the same cognition, then it becomes determinate. Determination is only a perfection of merely learned cognition §.24, if one wants to make easier for the weak profound understanding of the human being the comprehending of a thing.",[]],"meier:152":["The most adequate cognition of all can little by little become less adequate, inadequate, confused, and finally entirely obscure, if for the sake of forgetfulness the clear cognition becomes obscure §.136. Whoever therefore wants to prevent this loss of perfections must seek to guard against forgetfulness. We human beings are never entirely secure in the possession of our perfections, and we must therefore everywhere diligently be on our guard.",[]],"meier:153":["Whoever wants to make learned cognition properly perfect with regard to its clarity must proportion the degree of clarity to the magnitude of the objects §.70. Consequently, the more extensive and greater the object is, by so many more, better and greater kinds and degrees of clarity must the learned cognition of it be illuminated. In the opposite case one must, in this matter, also conduct oneself oppositely.",[]],"meier:154":["Every learned person must, if it is otherwise possible for him, choose as his principal science that part of learning which is capable of the most manifold and greatest clarity; and everyone must seek to attain the clearest cognition of all in his principal science §.63. 64. He must therefore be a bright head, or love the clearest truths preferentially, and constantly think as clearly as possible. A dark head hardly deserves the name of a learned person. On the certainty of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:155":["Certainty (*certitudo subiective spectata*) is the consciousness of truth, or the clear cognition of truth §.29. If one therefore wants to possess a certain learned cognition, then it must be true according to the rules of the fourth section, and clear in the proper way according to the rules of the fifth section.",[]],"meier:156":["If we cognize clearly neither that something is true, nor that it is false, then our cognition of it is uncertain (*incertitudo*). Uncertainty is therefore to be encountered only as an imperfection in our cognition. A cognition that is not uncertain to us is either certainly true (*certo vera cognitio*), if we are conscious of its truth, or certainly false (*certo falsa cognitio*), if we are conscious of its incorrectness §.155.",[]],"meier:157":["All certainty is either a distinct or a confused cognition of truth §.155. 137. The latter is sensible certainty (*certitudo sensitiva*), and if it is perfect in a higher degree, the aesthetic (*certitudo aesthetica*). The former is rational certainty (*certitudo rationalis*), and if it is perfect in a higher degree, it is called the logical or learned (*certitudo iogica, erudita*). No human certain cognition can be merely rational and learned §.144.",[]],"meier:158":["Rational certainty is either an adequate certainty (*certitudo adaequata*), if we are in turn rationally certain of the characteristics of truth; or an inadequate one (*certitudo inadaequata*), if we are only sensibly certain of the characteristics of truth §.157. 147. E.g., what thinks is actual, I think, therefore I am actual. Because I have a distinct certainty of the first proposition, I am to that extent adequately certain of the last proposition. But because the second proposition is only sensibly certain to me, the last proposition is to that extent only inadequately certain to me.",[]],"meier:159":["Certainty is either a complete (*completa certitudo*) or an incomplete certainty (*incompleta certitudo*) §.132.149. Determinate certainty (*certitudo determinata*) is a complete certainty which contains no more characteristics of truth than are indispensable for complete certainty §.151.",[]],"meier:160":["Through complete certainty the mind is always so set at rest that it is raised above all rational fear of the opposite; incomplete certainty, however, cannot entirely drive away this rational fear §.159. Meanwhile a human being can often be freed from all fear of the opposite, and notwithstanding this not even be certain at all.",[]],"meier:161":["Determinate certainty, if it is moreover as adequate as possible, is mathematical certainty (*certitudo mathematica*).",[]],"meier:162":["We have a total certainty of a thing (*certitudo totalis*) if everything that we cognize of it is certain to us; but if not all of this is certain to us, then the thing is only partly certain to us (*certitudo partialis*).",[]],"meier:163":["A certain cognition is called 1) convincing (*cognitio convincens*), insofar as it is completely certain, and the production of such a certain cognition is called conviction (*convictio*); 2) undeniable (*cognitio evidens, indubitata*), insofar as we cognize clearly that that of which we are convinced cannot possibly be false; 3) thorough (*cognitio solida*), insofar as the certainty is adequate; or also if it is as great and of the kind as is required.",[]],"meier:164":["The more things we cognize certainly, and the more we cognize certainly of each object; the greater the things are that we cognize certainly, and the more clearly we cognize the truth: the greater is the certainty §.155. Whoever therefore wants properly to improve learned cognition must make it as certain as is possible, by producing certainty of all kinds, and in each kind the greatest degree of certainty, as much, namely, as the remaining rules of the perfection of learned cognition permit.",[]],"meier:165":["We must not conclude: 1) What is certain to me is also certain to other people, and conversely §.126. 2) What is uncertain to me is, considered in itself, uncertain, or even false §.156. 3) What is uncertain to me is also uncertain to other people, and conversely §.126. 4) What is now certain to me will in future always also remain certain to me §.126. 5) What is now uncertain to me will also henceforth always remain uncertain to me §.126. 6) If not everything in an object is certain, then it has no certainty at all §.162.",[]],"meier:166":["Because we attain some kinds, especially of logical certainty, only little by little, one must partly not become vexed if certainty will not ensue as quickly as we sometimes wish; partly one must be mistrustful, if we have perhaps in some case become certain all too quickly, whether one has not perhaps overlooked something. One must, in the attaining of certainty, make haste slowly.",[]],"meier:167":["If a cognition is not certain of the kind and in the degree that it could and should be, then it is called a shallow cognition (*cognitio superficiaria*).",[]],"meier:168":["We give a cognition our assent, or we accept it (*assentiri, ponere aliquid*) if we take it to be true; we reject it (*tollere aliquid*) if we take it to be false; and we withhold our assent (*suspendere iudicium*) if we do neither of the two. If we accept or reject an uncertain cognition, then we do this either because we cognize some characteristics of correctness or incorrectness, or we cognize none at all of these characteristics. In the latter case we are overhasty (*praecipitantia*), and the uncertain cognition that we accept or reject out of overhastiness is a begged cognition, a prejudice, a preconceived opinion (*praecaria cognitio, praeiudicium, praeconcepta opinio*). Begged cognition is no learned cognition at all §.21. 17, and all prejudices can be avoided in learned cognition, and they are accordingly an inexcusable blemish of it.",[]],"meier:169":["Because the correctness and incorrectness of things does not depend on our insights, 1) that which we accept through a prejudice can be false, and what we reject through a prejudice can be true. 2) That which we accept through a prejudice can in fact be true, and what we reject through a prejudice can actually be false. 3) In every prejudice there is always something erroneous and false, because we believe ourselves to be certain, although we are nevertheless not certain §.168.",[]],"meier:170":["A logical prejudice (*praeiudicium logicum*) is a prejudice through which the perfection of learned cognition, especially its thoroughness, is hindered. For example: a) the prejudice of all too great trust (*praeiudicium nimiae confidentiae*), if one in an overhasty way takes something to be logically perfect. 1) The prejudice of all too great authority (*praeiudicium autoritatis*), if we accept or reject something because a human being accepts and rejects it whom we honor so much that we take him to be worthy of imitation. 2) Logical egoism (*egoismus logicus*), if someone takes something to be logically perfect because he himself is the author of it. 3) The prejudice of antiquity (*praeiudicium antiquitatis*), if we take something to be true because it is an old opinion. 4) The prejudice of novelty (*praeiudicium novitatis*), if we take something to be true because it is a new opinion. 5) The prejudice of the adopted doctrinal system (*praeiudicium systematis*), if one accepts something merely because it is in accordance with our doctrinal system, and rejects it because it is contrary to it. 6) The prejudice of lazy trust (*praeiudicium pigritiae*), if one believes one gets just as far in learned cognition through a lesser diligence as through a greater. 7) The prejudice of shallowness (*praeiudicium corticis*), if one believes that one can get just as far with a shallow cognition as with a thorough one. b) The prejudice of all too great mistrust (*praeiudicium nimiae diffidentiae*), if one in an overhasty way takes something to be logically imperfect. 1) The prejudice of antiquity (*praeiudicium antiquitatis*), if one rejects something because it is old. 2) The prejudice of novelty (*praeiudicium novitatis*) if one rejects something because it is new. 3) The prejudice of nationality (*nazarethismus*), if one rejects something because it stems from a certain people. 4) The prejudice of the mistrust which one places in oneself (*praeiudicium nimiae diffidentiae in se ipsum positae*), if one trusts oneself too little, etc.",[]],"meier:171":["If we accept or reject an uncertain cognition for the sake of some characteristics of correctness and incorrectness §.168, then we either cognize more and stronger grounds to accept it than to reject it, and then our cognition is probable (*cognitio probabilis, verosimilis*), or we cognize more and stronger grounds to reject it than to accept it, and so we have an improbable cognition (*cognitio improbabilis*); or the grounds on both sides are equal to one another, and then it is a doubtful cognition (*cognitio dubia*). E.g., it is probable that the planets are populated, but it is improbable that the sun is. As often as we can come to no decision, so often do we have a doubtful cognition.",[]],"meier:172":["Because human beings are so infinitely different from one another in their cognition, the one can accept or reject through a prejudice a thing which the second takes to be quite certainly true, the third quite certainly false, the fourth probable, the fifth improbable, and the sixth doubtful. The first always acts wrongly. The two following cannot possibly both be right or wrong. The three last, however, can all together be right §.159. 168. 171.",[]],"meier:173":["Probability, improbability and doubtfulness are uncertainties §.171, and therefore not to be found in the objects of our cognition §.156. The most probable things of all can be false, and the most improbable true.",[]],"meier:174":["Prejudices one must take to be neither true nor false §.168, just as also doubtful cognition §.171. Improbable cognition one must take to be false, and probable to be true, yet both under a constant fear of the opposite §.171. To completely certain cognition we must give our assent with the greatest confidence, without fearing the opposite §.159.",[]],"meier:175":["The more and greater grounds we cognize to accept something, the fewer and smaller grounds we cognize to reject it, the better we cognize the former and the worse the latter: the greater is the probability, and the greater the improbability of the opposite §.171. So great a degree of probability, which in our regular conduct is as good as a complete certainty, is called moral certainty (*certitudo moralis*).",[]],"meier:176":["If we take something to be true or false, then one calls each cognition of a ground for the opposite a doubt (*dubium*). Doubts are cognized either in a merely obscure and indistinct manner, or in a rational and learned way. The former are called scruples (*scrupulus*), and the others objections (*obiectio*).",[]],"meier:177":["All doubts are either true doubts (*dubium verum*) or false (*dubium falsum*) §.176. 92. 93, which, if they seem to be true, have a great illusion. If one clearly cognizes the incorrectness in a false doubt, then the doubt is resolved or answered (*dubium resolvitur, seu ad dubium respondetur*). All doubts are either answerable or unanswerable, and both either absolutely or relatively (*dubium absolute et respective resolubile aut irresolubile*). Consequently 1) doubts can be raised against all truths, and nothing is false because doubts are raised against it; 2) against a truth doubts can be raised which are unanswerable to this or that human being; 3) against no truth can absolutely unanswerable doubts be raised. Settled truths (*veritates indubitatae, extra omnem dubitationis aleam positae*) are either such truths as are completely certain; or against which no other doubts can be raised than those that are answerable and have already been answered. Truths that are not settled are unsettled truths (*veritates non indubitatae*).",[]],"meier:178":["For full conviction of a truth 1) it is not required that the unknown doubts against it be answered: nor 2) that the scruples be answered, for they deserve no answer at all; 3) nor that all known doubts be answered, there can even be some among them which are unanswerable to a human being, and who notwithstanding this can be fully convinced; 4) it is required that all doubts which make the mind waver be answered; 5) the answering of doubts always serves for the increase of certainty, and for strengthening in conviction §.176.",[]],"meier:179":["The uncertainty of cognition arises 1) from the limitation of our cognitive power, for which we either are or are not to blame; 2) from the lack of the proper attention of reflection, of diligence and of patience §.129. 145, which can be either necessary or contingent, blameworthy or praiseworthy; 3) from a necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy ignorance of such truths without the cognition of which another truth cannot become certain to us § 43. 50. 51; 4) from an all too strong attention to doubts §.176.",[]],"meier:180":["There is in human cognition an uncertainty 1) which is entirely unavoidable, and which serves us neither for disgrace nor for honor; 2) which we may not avoid, even if we could, because its object is encountered outside our horizon, or beneath it, and that serves a human being for honor; 3) which a human being can and should avoid, because the opposed certainty belongs within his horizon §.178. This last always serves us for disgrace, and whoever wants to improve his learned cognition as much as possible must seek to avoid only all uncertainty of the third kind.",[]],"meier:181":["An opinion (*opinio*) is any uncertain cognition, insofar as we accept it, and at the same time cognize that it is not certain. An opinion is either accepted as a ground from which we explain the appearances in the world, or not. The latter is a common opinion (*opinio vulgaris*). The former is a philosophical or learned opinion (*hypothesis philosophica, erudita*), e.g., if one assumes the efflux of a magnetic matter, in order to explain thereby how the magnet draws iron to itself.",[]],"meier:182":["All opinions 1) have in them the nature of all uncertain cognition; 2) common opinions are, at least in learned cognition, to be despised altogether; 3) what is an opinion to some is not an opinion to all; 4) one must not make too much fuss about learned opinions, for they belong to the more imperfect half of learned cognition; 5) one must not entirely despise them, because they are the transition from historical cognition to fully certain learned cognition §.181.",[]],"meier:183":["With learned opinions one must observe the following rules: 1) one must take no learned opinion to be certainly true, and not show as much zeal for it as for certain truths. 2) One must accept them only in case of necessity, when we can as yet have no better learned cognition. 3) One must accept no manifestly false opinion, which either is internally impossible, or contradicts another undeniable truth, or contradicts the appearances. An appearance does not contradict an opinion because we cannot derive it from it. 4) One must be ready to give up the most elegant and most learned opinion as soon as one discovers its incorrectness. 5) One must not accept a learned opinion before one has brought it to some probability. One must therefore, among other things, be able to explain most appearances from it. 6) One must constantly endeavor to transform an opinion into a certain cognition. 7) One must not have an all too great inclination toward opinions, and not invent all too many of them. 8) One must not take an opinion to be true because it is new, elegant, wondrous, harmless, edifying, and has required much effort, learning, wit and acuity before it was invented.",[]],"meier:184":["Certainty and conviction are either true or merely apparent. The error through which we imagine ourselves to be convinced, although we are nevertheless not convinced, is called persuasion in the bad sense (*persuasio malo significatu*). Since through it the certainty of learned cognition is hindered, one must seek to guard against it as much as possible. It arises, however: 1) from ignorance of the rules of the doctrine of reason; 2) from the lack of a certain cognition, for then one does not yet know how one feels in a true conviction; 3) from prejudices §.169. 170. 171; 4) from an all too great negligence and hastiness. Now insofar as it lies in the capacity of a human being to clear these causes of persuasion out of the way, to that extent he is also in a position to avoid this fault himself.",[]],"meier:185":["If one now guards against persuasion, and arrives at a thorough conviction, then one attains a science (*scientia subiective spectata*), that is, a learned cognition, insofar as it is completely certain.",[]],"meier:186":["The greater, more important and more fruitful the object of our learned cognition is, the more certain must our cognition be §.70, that is, we must seek to make the cognition of it thorough according to the most and best kinds of certainty, and in the highest degrees of it. With smaller objects it is exactly the reverse.",[]],"meier:187":["Everyone must not only choose for himself, as his principal science, that part of learning which is capable of the greatest certainty, as far as is otherwise possible for him; but he must also seek to raise his chosen principal science to the most possible degree of certainty §.63.64.",[]],"meier:188":["Complete certainty consists in the consciousness of truth §.155, consequently that it is possible and grounded §.94.96, so that no fear of the opposite remains §.160. Consequently we are then conscious that the opposite is false, impossible and ungrounded §.95. 97. Consequently complete certainty arises from the consciousness of the necessity of the truth §.114.",[]],"meier:189":["Complete certainty arises either from our clearly cognizing that the truth is absolutely necessary, or that it is necessary only in this world §.113. If we cognize the first with mathematical certainty, then therein consists mathematical certainty of the first rank (*certitudo mathematica primi ordinis*). In the second case we have mathematical certainty of the second rank (*mathematica certitudo secundi ordinis*), e.g., with our experiences.",[]],"meier:190":["The consciousness of the contingency of the truth causes uncertainty, and at most only an incomplete certainty §.189.113. Arbitrary truths (*hypothesis*) are those whose truth depends on the choice of human beings, and their arbitrariness does not hinder full certainty of them.",[]],"meier:191":["The proof (*probatio*) is that which is added to a truth so that it becomes certain. The ground of proof (*probatio materialiter sumta, ratio probans*) is the ground from which the truth can be clearly cognized, and those are the characteristics of truth §.94. The sequence of the proof (*probatio formaliter sumta, consequentia*) is the interconnection of the truth with the ground of proof §.15. Every proof accordingly consists of the ground of proof and the sequence, and it can cause either a complete or an incomplete certainty §.159. If the first, then it is called a sufficient proof or a demonstration (*probatio sufficiens, demonstratio*); if the second, then it is an insufficient proof (*probatio insufficiens*). Of the latter it is treated in detail in the doctrine of reason of probable cognition.",[]],"meier:192":["The cognition of a truth is either a demonstrable (*cognitio demonstrabilis*) or an indemonstrable cognition (*cognitio indemonstrabilis*). The latter becomes certain to us as soon as we cognize it distinctly, the former, however, not. The latter is fully certain without proof, the former, however, not. Let one beware of taking either begged cognition or demonstrable cognition to be indemonstrable §.168.",[]],"meier:193":["If we want to attain complete certainty of a truth through the proof and from it, or if a proof is to be a demonstration, §.191; then 1) every ground of proof that is contained in the proof must be completely certain. Accordingly all demonstrable grounds of proof must be proved again until one comes to nothing but indemonstrable grounds of proof; 2) the sequence must be completely certain §.191. Apart from these two items nothing further is required for a proof if it is to be a demonstration §.15.",[]],"meier:194":["If 1) even only a single ground of proof in a proof is false, or 2) uncertain, 3) a single sequence in the proof is false, or 4) uncertain, or 5) if several of these faults are encountered together in a proof, then it cannot be a demonstration §.193.",[]],"meier:195":["Just as someone can take an insufficient proof to be a demonstration, so also can someone take a demonstration to be an, insufficient proof, or even a false proof §.165. Consequently one must not reject a demonstration at all because it does not have the proper effect on our mind.",[]],"meier:196":["Through a demonstration we seek to become certain either that something is true, or that something is false §.191. 156. In the first case one can demonstrate a truth in a twofold way: 1) in an immediate manner (*demonstratio directa, ostensiva*), if we derive the truth from its characteristics; 2) in a mediate manner (*demonstratio indirecta, apogogica, deductio ad absurdum*), if we demonstrate the incorrectness of its opposite, and conclude from that its truth. Likewise one can demonstrate that something is false, or refute it (*refutatio*), a) in a mediate manner (*refutatio mediata*), if we demonstrate the truth of its opposite; b) in an immediate manner (*refutatio immediata*), if we derive the incorrectness of it from its characteristics. One can, however, prove that something is false: α) if we prove that it is impossible and ungrounded, β) that it is contrary to other undeniable truths, and γ) that something false follows from it.",[]],"meier:197":["A demonstration causes either a distinctly or indistinctly complete certainty §.191. 188. The latter are the aesthetic demonstrations (*demonstratio aesthetica*), the former, however, the logical, philosophical and learned (*demonstratio logica, philo sophica, erudita*). A logical demonstration which causes a mathematical certainty is a mathematical demonstration (*demonstratio mathematica*) §.161.",[]],"meier:198":["We can always conclude: 1) if the whole proof is correct and certain, then the proved thing is also correct and certain; 2) if the proved thing is false and uncertain, then there is a fault in the proof §.194. We cannot, however, conclude: 1) if the proof is false, then the proved thing is also false; 2) if the proof is uncertain, then the proved thing is also only uncertain; 3) what we cannot prove is false; 4) if the proved thing is true and certain, then the proof is also true and certain.",[]],"meier:199":["The demonstrating spirit (*spiritus demonstrationis*) consists in the sufficient skill of a human being for demonstrating, and the natural drive toward such a certain cognition as one attains through demonstrating. Without this spirit no one can demonstrate, and no one should venture upon this matter without it.",[]],"meier:200":["The all too great love of demonstrating is the craving to demonstrate (*pruritus demonstrandi*). This learned disease manifests itself through the following items: 1) if one seeks to demonstrate what one cannot and may not demonstrate; 2) if one even demonstrates in a spurious way, only so that one may arouse the illusion of demonstrating among the dim-witted; 3) if one stops at mere demonstrating alone, and does not properly use and apply the demonstrated truths; 4) if one is a pedant in demonstrating, or altogether despises that which is not demonstrated, whether it actually is not demonstrated, or we merely do not take it to be so; 5) if one, like a charlatan, exalts demonstrating too highly, and makes too much boasting of it, even when one demonstrates useless trifles.",[]],"meier:201":["A sensation (*sensatio*) is a representation of a present thing, and in that we clearly sense something, we experience it. Experience (*experientia*) therefore consists in that cognition which is clear through sensing. Clear sensations are immediate experience (*experientia immediata*), and the remaining clear cognition, which is derived from immediate experience through a shorter proof, is called mediate experience (*experientia mediata*). E.g., that I think is a sensation and immediate experience, but that I have a faculty to think is a mediate experience.",[]],"meier:202":["If something is certain to us, then it is certain to us either from experience, or from other grounds, and in the first case either from our own experience, or from the experience of other people. Consequently we have a threefold source of all proofs §.191, namely our own experience, the experience of other people, and other grounds that are not experiences.",[]],"meier:203":["If we 1) conduct a proof from our own immediate experience, then we need only clearly represent to ourselves a single case in which we have sensed something §.201. 2) With proofs from mediate experience not only must at least One immediate experience be adduced, but the remaining grounds of proof together with the sequence must also be certain §.201. 193. 3) That of which we are certain even through immediate experience is no indemonstrable cognition §.192. 201. 4) Experience gives us a complete certainty, in that it contains all characteristics of the truth of the object §.201. 94. 96. 5) Experience gives us only a complete certainty of the second rank §.189, and 6) it represents the truth to us only from the external characteristics §.94.",[]],"meier:204":["If we prove a truth from other grounds of proof which are no experience, then we conduct a proof from reason (*probatio ex ratione*). In such a proof 1) no ground of proof must occur which is an experience, and 2) all grounds of proof of it must be completely certain without experience, if it is to be a demonstration §.193.",[]],"meier:205":["All experience, and what we prove from it, is cognition from behind (*cognitio a posteriore*), the remaining rational cognition, however, is called cognition from before (*cognitio a priore*). If we are certain of a truth both from our own experience and also from reason, then one calls this the union of reason and experience (*connubium rationis et experientiae*).",[]],"meier:206":["From other people's experience we become certain by means of belief. Whoever gives out an actual thing as true, so that another may also take it to be true, is called a witness (*testis*), and his action a testimony (*testimonium, testari*). To believe (*credere*) means to accept something for the sake of a testimony. Belief (*fides, fides historica*) is the assent that we give to a thing for the sake of a testimony. The object of belief consists in past, present and future things, but not in other truths.",[]],"meier:207":["The authority of a witness (*autoritas testis*) consists in that degree of his honor by means of which he is held to be worthy of imitation in his cognition. We can believe no witness who stands in no authority with us. And this authority consists 1) in the competence of the witness (*dexteritas testis*), if he possesses sufficient powers not only to get a correct experience, but also to designate it in a correct way; 2) in the sincerity of the witness (*sinceritas testis*), or in the inclination of his will to designate his experiences as he takes them to be true. Neither of the two can, without the other, procure for a witness the proper authority.",[]],"meier:208":["An eyewitness (*testis oculatus*) is a witness who has himself experienced the thing to which he testifies. An ear-witness (*testis auritus*) is not an eyewitness, but he has only experienced the testimony of others about the thing.",[]],"meier:209":["For the competence of an eyewitness it is required: 1) he must have been present at the thing to which he testifies; 2) he must be in a position to get a correct experience; 3) he must have a good and faithful memory, or write down his experiences immediately; 4) he must possess the gift of designating his own cognition in a correct and adequate way.",[]],"meier:210":["An ear-witness is, with regard to the testimony to which he testifies, an eyewitness, and he must accordingly possess all the competence required for that §.209. Insofar, however, as he is an ear-witness, the following items are required for his competence: 1) he must repeat only the testimonies of competent eyewitnesses, and he must therefore always be able to adduce a competent eyewitness who is his guarantor. A common report (*fama*) is a testimony of many ear-witnesses about a thing whose eyewitness is unknown. These ear-witnesses live either at one time, and then their report is a public rumor (*rumor sine capite*), or at different times, and then it is an oral tradition (*oralis traditio*). The common report lacks the necessary authority; 2) he must possess understanding enough to grasp rightly what he hears or reads; 3) he must possess a faithful memory, in order to communicate the reports again to others just as he has received them.",[]],"meier:211":["With the sincerity of a witness it depends merely on his good will not to lie §.207. That witness who promises himself from a true testimony either sheer good, or more good than evil, and who promises himself from the lie sheer evil, or more evil than good, he speaks the truth. And in the opposite case he lies. This good or evil can concern his soul or his body, or his external state; he can hope for it or fear it from GOD, from those human beings to whom he gives the testimony, or of whom he testifies, etc., whether he now deceives himself in this matter or not.",[]],"meier:212":["The certainty of a testimony rests 1) on the internal indicators of its truth §.95. 96; 2) on the external indicators of its truth, namely on the sufficient authority of the witness, which must at least be very probable §.207, 94. The testimonies of a competent and sincere witness cannot be false.",[]],"meier:213":["What we believe, we do not beg for §.212. 168.Unbelief (*incredulitas*) is the facility of not believing a certain or probable testimony. Credulity (*credulitas*) is the facility of believing an improbable testimony. Both faults are unreasonable §.174.",[]],"meier:214":["A witness is credible (*testis fide dignus*) if one can cognize in a learned way at least probably that he has sufficient authority; the testimony of such a witness is a credible testimony (*testimonium fide dignum*). Reasonable  or seeing belief (*fides oculata, rationalis*) is the facility of believing only credible witnesses.",[]],"meier:215":["There is 1) a union of experience and of belief (*connubium experientiae et fidei*), if we cognize something both from our own experience and from credible testimonies; 2) a union of belief and of reason (*connubium fidei et rationis*), if we are certain of a thing both from reason and through belief; and 3) a union of reason, of belief and of experience (*connubium rationis experientiae et fidei*), if we cognize a truth convincingly by all three ways. The union of belief with the remaining sources of cognition is also called mixed belief (*fides mixta*); but if we accept something merely for the sake of belief, then it is called pure belief (*fides pura*). Since we can demonstrate neither the competence nor the sincerity of human witnesses, belief gives only a probability, and at most only a moral certainty §.175.of practical learned cognition.",[]],"meier:216":["A cognition is practical (*cognitio practica*) insofar as it can move us in a noticeable way to do or to omit an action. All more perfect cognition that is not practical is called a speculative cognition (*cognitio speculativa, speculatio*). All learned cognition is accordingly either practical or speculative.",[]],"meier:217":["A cognition in which we represent to ourselves that something ought to be done or omitted is  also called practical, insofar as it is opposed to theoretical cognition (*cognitio theoretica, theoria*), the cognition that does not represent to us that something ought to be done or omitted. All learned cognition is either practical or theoretical, and both kinds belong either to practical or to speculative cognition §.216.",[]],"meier:218":["Practical cognition is better than speculative cognition §.216, because in it a greater harmony of the manifold is encountered §.22. Whoever therefore wants to improve his learned cognition as much as possible, he must guard against speculation, and seek sheer practical cognition.",[]],"meier:219":["No true learned cognition is speculative by its nature, but only on account of the lack of insight of a learned man, who cannot see, or does not want to see, its interconnection with the conduct of the human being. In the latter case the learned man disgraces himself.",[]],"meier:220":["It is unreasonable if one would take for a speculation: 1) all profound, determinate, precise and difficult learned cognition; 2) all erudition in general; 3) all theory: 4) that learned cognition which is not practical in this or that human being: 5) all cognition that does not have an immediate influence on the moral conduct of a human being.",[]],"meier:221":["A cognition is either practical on account of its object (*cognitio obiective practica*), or on account of its constitution (*cognitio subiective practica*), or both at once. In the first case it must have an object which can be cognized in the way that is required for a practical cognition. In the other case it is so constituted that it can act on the power of desire.",[]],"meier:222":["A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 1) if the cognized truths are good and useful. And in this aim all true learned cognition is practical §.39. 221.",[]],"meier:223":["A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 2) insofar as it lies at the ground of the reasonable actions by which human perfection is preserved §.221, and the lack of which one at once notices in the actions of a human being.",[]],"meier:224":["A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 3) if its object consists in the rules which we must observe if we want to preserve our entire welfare in the best way §.221.",[]],"meier:225":["A learned cognition is practical on account of its object: 4) if its object, in a remote and mediate way, has an influence on our entire good conduct §.221, e.g., the cognition of such truths without which the remaining practical truths§.222-224 could either not at all, or yet not in a learned way, be cognized by us human beings.",[]],"meier:226":["Since no human being is in a position to cognize all practical truths, that learned cognition is called preeminently and absolutely practical (*cognitio obiective et comparative s. eminenter practica*) which is practical in a higher degree, e.g., which is noticeably more useful to us than another, and which has an immediate or very near influence on our conduct.",[]],"meier:227":["All learned cognition is called comparatively speculative (*cognitio comparative speculativa*) if it is practical in a very small degree. E.g., 1) if it has a very small and un noticeable  influence on our regular conduct; 2) if it has a very small use; 3) if it hinders us from a more necessary and more useful cognition.",[]],"meier:228":["On account of the different way of life and principal science, a learned cognition can rightly be taken by the one for practical, and by the other for speculative §.226. 227. Indeed, someone can, on account of his slight cognitive powers, and out of negligence, not see the interconnection of a learned cognition with our conduct, who will therefore take it for a speculative cognition.",[]],"meier:229":["Insofar as a learned cognition is practical, insofar is it also useful. One must accordingly 1) take no truth and no learned cognition for entirely useless §.222. 2) A learned cognition is not useless because it is of no use to this or that human being, and is perhaps even harmful to him. 3) A learned cognition which is not useful in one aim can be useful in another aim. 4) One must not infer: what is not useful to us now, that will also not be useful to us in the future.",[]],"meier:230":["The more uses a learned cognition procures, the greater they are, and the greater and more manifold its influence on our entire conduct is, the more practical it is §.216.",[]],"meier:231":["In order that every learned man improve his learned cognition as much as possible, he must 1) cog nize most perfectly those truths that are practical in the highest degree. The degree of diligence and of the perfection of learned cognition must be proportioned to the degree of the practical in its object §.70. 2) Each must, where possible, choose for his principal science that part of erudition which is practical in the highest degree §.63.",[]],"meier:232":["A cognition that is practical according to its constitution is called a living and stirring cognition (*cognitio viva, movens*). A living reasonable cognition (*cognitio rationalis viva*) is a reasonable cognition that is so constituted that through it reasonable desires and aversions are aroused; or it contains motives (*motiva*), that is, such reasonable representations of good and evil through which desires and aversions arise. All reasonable cognition that is not so constituted is called a reasonable dead cognition (*cognitio rationalis mortua*).",[]],"meier:233":["That constitution of cognition by virtue of which it effects desires and aversions is called the life of cognition (*cognitionis vita*), which, if it consists in motives §.232, is called the reasonable life of cognition, or then the cognition stirs and moves in a reasonable way (*vita rationalis cognitionis*).",[]],"meier:234":["A learned cognition can 1) with regard to its object be uncommonly practical, and notwithstanding this be dead; 2) it can be false and uncommonly living; the life of cognition is thus no indicator of its truth; 3) a learned cognition can be very extensive, great, correct, certain, and distinct, and notwithstanding this be dead §.232. Life is accordingly yet another perfection of learned cognition, which is different from all its remaining perfections.",[]],"meier:235":["The practical in learned cognition becomes actually practical only through life. Living reasonable cognition is accordingly more perfect than dead, even if they both should otherwise be adorned with all the remaining logical perfections in equal degree.",[]],"meier:236":["If reasonable and learned cognition is to stir reasonably, then it must 1) be an intuitive cognition. A cognition is intuitive (*cognitio intuitiva*) if we represent to ourselves the object more strongly than its signs; but if we represent these to ourselves more strongly than that, then the cognition is symbolic (*cognitio symbolica*). All learned cognition is either intuitive or symbolic. If one occupies attention, thinking and reflection chiefly with the object of learned cognition, then it becomes a distinct intuitive cognition.",[]],"meier:237":["Pleasure (*voluptas*) is the intuitive cognition of perfection, and the intuitive cognition of imperfection is displeasure (*taedium*). A cognition causes us an indifference (*indifferentia*) if it causes us neither a pleasure nor a displeasure. If therefore reasonable and learned cognition is to stir reasonably, then it must 2) arouse pleasure and displeasure; a) about itself it must arouse in us sheer pleasure: because by virtue of all rules of the doctrine of reason it must be uncommonly perfect, and the feeling of its perfection must impel us to desire and to attain it. To whom therefore it is all the same whether he cognizes a thing in a learned way or not, his learned cognition is not living.",[]],"meier:238":["Reasonable and learned cognition must, if the [sic] is to stir reasonably, b) arouse about the object either pleasure, or vexation, or both at once, in that it represents it to us either as good, or as evil, or as both at once, in an intuitive way. Then the learned cognition contains motives §.232; not only to  desire it itself §.237, but also either to desire or to abhor its object.",[]],"meier:239":["If reasonable and learned cognition is to stir us reasonably, then it must 3) represent to us in a learned way that all the good and all the evil on account of which it makes us pleased or displeased will be actual in our following states.",[]],"meier:240":["If reasonable and learned cognition is to stir us reasonably, then it must 4) represent to us in a learned way that it stands, or can stand, in our power to make actual the good on account of which it pleases us, and to prevent the evil on account of which it causes us a displeasure.",[]],"meier:241":["If learned cognition is to be really living, then it must 1) cause no equilibrium, or yet soon lift it. We stand, namely, in an equilibrium (*aequilibrium*) if the motives on both sides are equal and opposed to one another; 2) outweigh the sensible life of indistinct cognition, or this must not contradict it at all.",[]],"meier:242":["If reasonable and learned cognition is really living, then it causes a powerful and lasting resolution, which breaks out through the deed, in that we actually act in the way that is in accordance with the learned cognition.",[]],"meier:243":["The genuine life of learned cognition (*vita vera cognitionis eruditae*) consists in the true use of it (*usus verus cognitionis eruditae*), if we effect all true uses of it, or in deed make ourselves more perfect through it. But if we make ourselves thereby more imperfect or only more perfect according to illusion, then therein consists its misuse (*abusus cognitionis eruditae*), and that is the un genuine  life of it (*vita cognitionis eruditae spuria*). A perfect learned cognition must be living in a genuine way.",[]],"meier:244":["The more and greater motives a learned cognition contains, the better they are cognized, and the more and greater desires and aversions are aroused through it, the more living is the learned cognition.",[]],"meier:245":["One must not infer: 1) the learned cognition that does not stir me does not stir others either; 2) that which stirs me also stirs others.",[]],"meier:246":["The lack of life of learned cognition arises from ignorance §.236-240. 41. According now as this is either necessary or contingent, praiseworthy or blameworthy §.43.50. 51, according to that is the lack of life also constituted. It cannot be demanded that all learned cognition stir constantly and in equal degree.",[]],"meier:247":["The greater and more practical the objects are, the more strongly must their learned cognition stir; but the smaller and less practical they are, the less must their learned cognition stir §.70. All our learned cognition must stir us in a certain aim §.237.",[]],"meier:248":["Each must choose for his principal science that part of erudition which according to its nature is capable of the most living learned cognition of all; and each must seek to cognize his principal science most livingly §.63. 64. of learned concepts.",[]],"meier:249":["A concept (*conceptus*) is a representation of a thing in a thing that possesses the faculty to think. Accordingly all our representations are concepts.",[]],"meier:250":["As many different kinds of cognition as there are, so many different kinds of concepts there are also §.249. Consequently concepts are either learned concepts (*conceptus eruditus, logicus*), or not §.21. The former is a distinct concept which is logically perfect in a noticeable degree. An unlearned concept (*conceptus ineruditus*) is a concept that possesses no noticeable degree of logical perfection. And then it is either a beautiful concept, or not §.19. The former is an aesthetic concept (*conceptus pulcher, aestheticus*), but the latter a common one (*conceptus vulgaris*). A learned concept is either at the same time beautiful, or not. The latter is a merely learned concept (*conceptus mere eruditus*). But the former a concept that is not merely learned (*conceptus non mere eruditus*). And such are the best concepts §.32.",[]],"meier:251":["A learned concept must be in accordance as much as possible with the rules of the extensiveness, of the magnitude, of the truth, of the clarity, of the certainty, and of the practical constitution of learned cognition §.41-248.",[]],"meier:252":["The object of learned concepts is not determined by their nature. Of every possible thing a learned concept is possible §.250.",[]],"meier:253":["Each must only chiefly improve according to the doctrine of reason those concepts that belong to his principal science, §.64, and in every  doctrinal system chiefly the principal concepts. It is not possible and necessary to improve all our concepts according to the doctrine of reason, and just as little to improve all our learned concepts in an equal degree. The greater a concept is, the more one must improve it §.70.",[]],"meier:254":["We have only three ways to arrive at concepts: experience, abstraction, and arbitrary combination.",[]],"meier:255":["All our sensations are concepts §.249. 201. A concept of experience (*conceptus per experientiam formatus*) is a concept that we attain through experience. E.g., the concepts of the alterations of our soul, of our body and of other things outside us. A concept of experience we attain either through immediate, or through mediate experience. §.201.",[]],"meier:256":["Through immediate experience we can attain only concepts of actual things, insofar as they are present to us, and indeed these concepts contain only affirmative and alterable marks. Mediate concepts of experience can also represent to us other objects, and something else in them. §.201.",[]],"meier:257":["Since concepts of experience can be learned only insofar as they are distinct §.250, one must carefully analyze them §.142. And this work is often furthered 1) by the use of the weapons of the senses, e.g., magnifying glasses. 2) By the anatomy of corporeal things, by which one cognizes the parts and the way of their composition. And thereby one attains the concept of the essence of a corporeal thing. 3) By attention to that from which a thing arises and how it arises, if one is present at its arising, and  from this one also attains concepts of the essence. 4) If one seeks to experience the object often in different circumstances: for then one discovers more easily the best marks, through which the concept of experience becomes exhaustive.",[]],"meier:258":["All concepts of experience are true and certain §.202, and they represent the objects to us as they are constituted, because otherwise we would be inhabitants of another world. But if we take a concept for a concept of experience which is not one, or something for an object of the concept of experience which is not one: then it does seem as if the concept of experience were false, but the fault lies in another concept.",[]],"meier:259":["We make a concept through logical separation (*conceptus per abstractionem logicam formatus*) if we hold agreeing concepts of different things against one another, and represent to ourselves distinctly the marks that they have in common with one another, alone. To that end 1) one takes some concepts that are different, and similar at the same time. E.g., a rational animal and a nonrational animal; 2) each of them one analyzes §.142; 3) the marks different in them one abstracts, or one obscures them; 4) the remaining marks one gathers together into one concept, e.g., an animal.",[]],"meier:260":["All concepts which are made through logical separation are separated or abstract concepts (*conceptus abstractus, notio*). Concepts that are not separated are called singular concepts (*conceptus singularis, idea*). E.g., Leibniz. All immediate concepts of experience are singular concepts §.255.201. What is represented as a mark of the other is contained in it and belongs to it (*in altero contineri, ipsi convenire*). The separated concept is  thus contained in all those concepts from which it can be separated §.259. The separated concept contains under itself those from which it has been separated, and these are contained under it (*conceptus alios sub se continet, et conceptus sub alio continentur seu ad eum referuntur*).",[]],"meier:261":["A separated concept is called a higher concept (*conceptus superior*) insofar as it contains others under itself; but insofar as a concept is contained under another, insofar is it called a lower concept (*conceptus inferior*). The lower concept always contains marks which are not contained in the higher §.259, and these are called the difference of the lower concepts (*differentia conceptus inferioris*). A separated concept which com prehends under itself only singular concepts is called a species (*species*); but one which also contains separated concepts under itself is called a genus (*genus*).",[]],"meier:262":["The sum total of all concepts that are contained under a separated concept is its domain (*sphaera notionis*). The more abstract and higher a concept therefore is, that is: the more often logical separation is repeated with it, the greater is its domain. A separated concept belongs either to more concepts than those that are contained under another, or to fewer, or neither of the two. In the first case it is a wider concept than the other (*conceptus latior*), in the second, a narrower (*conceptus angustior*), and in the third they are reciprocal concepts (*conceptus reciproci*), of which neither is wider than the other. Insofar as a separated concept belongs to all that are contained under another, insofar is it called a universal concept (*conceptus universalis*), but insofar as it does not belong to all of them, a particular one (*conceptus particularis*). A separated  concept can be called a higher and lower, wider and narrower, universal and particular concept in different aims. Every separated concept is universal in a certain aim. Abstract learned cognition is therefore called universal learned cognition (*cognitio erudita universalis*).",[]],"meier:263":["With universal cognition one can 1) infer: What belongs to or contradicts the higher concepts, that also belongs to, or contradicts, all lower concepts that are contained under them. §.260. 2) One cannot infer: what belongs to or contradicts a lower concept, that also belongs to or contradicts other lower concepts which belong with that one to a higher concept. §.261. 3) One can infer: what belongs to or contradicts all and every lower concept, that also belongs to or contradicts their higher concept §.259. 261.",[]],"meier:264":["Abstract cognition furthers 1) the distinctness and completeness of cognition §.143. For the more abstract it is, the less it contains in itself §.259, and the more easily it can thus be thought through without much confusion; 2) the extensiveness of cognition §.263. 25. 3) The thoroughness of cognition §.263. 163. 4) The use and the employment of learned cognition §.39.",[]],"meier:265":["A separated concept contains nothing that is not contained in the lower concepts §.259; if these are therefore true, then it cannot possibly be false. If one therefore wants to prove a separated concept, then one need only show that it has been separated from true concepts. If one separates from a false concept the marks which contradict the remaining ones, then one gets a true concept §.95.",[]],"meier:266":["A concept is made through learned arbitrary combination (*combinatio conceptu um  arbitraria, logica, erudita, philosophica, rationalis*) if one represents to oneself two concepts as One, of which one has cognized in a learned way that they are not contrary to one another. To that end one takes 1) a separated concept, whether one has already long since separated it, or separates it only now; 2) a difference of a lower concept, of which we are either already convinced, or only afterwards become convinced, that it does not contradict the separated concept. This difference has either been left out by the separation from the abstract concept, or not. In the first case we get a lower concept from which we have separated the abstract one; but in the other a new lower concept; 3) one represents to oneself the abstract concept, taken together with this difference, as One concept.",[]],"meier:267":["A learned concept which has been made through arbitrary combination must be proved or refuted. Both happen 1) from experience, if one shows that their objects either are actual, or are not actual; 2) from reason, either in a mediate or in an immediate way §.196. E.g., if one either shows that, and how, their objects can become actual, or that they cannot become actual.",[]],"meier:268":["A definition, or a logical definition (*definitio, definitio logica*) is a determinate concept of a thing. The object of the definition is the defined thing, or the defined concept (*definitum*)§.151.",[]],"meier:269":["A description (*descriptio*) is an indeterminate concept, and it is either an exhaustive or an exhaustive description §.151 (*descriptio completa, vel incompleta*). No description is a definition, and must still much less be taken for  one §.268. Meanwhile they can be very perfect, useful and necessary learned concepts.",[]],"meier:270":["Definitions must be in accordance with six rules: 1) A definition must be in accordance with the rules of the extensiveness of learned cognition  . §.41-65. Consequently it must be an exhaustive concept. §.268.151. 55. Consequently a) it must not contain in itself fewer marks than are required for an exhaustive concept; nor b) more than are necessary.§.269. Hence the definition must, among all possible exhaustive concepts of a thing, be the very shortest, that is: contain as few marks as exhaustiveness permits. c) It must not be wider than the defined thing §.262; consequently its marks, taken together, must belong to no other thing than solely the defined thing. d) It must not be narrower than the defined thing. §.262. Consequently the defined concept and the definition are reciprocal concepts §.262.",[]],"meier:271":["If in a doctrinal system one defines the higher concepts earlier than the lower, then one can, if one is to define a concept which is not the highest, compose its definition out of the nearest higher concept and out of the difference §.261. Thereby the definitions become short and exhaustive, the definitions hang together more systematically, they are more in accordance with the nature of the lower concepts, and they pave an easier way to demonstrations.",[]],"meier:272":["Since the defined concept and the definition are reciprocal concepts §.270, one can infer: 1) To whatever the defined concept belongs, to that the definition also belongs; 2) to whatever the definition belongs, to that the defined concept also belongs; 3) to whatever the defined concept does not belong, to that the definition also does not belong;  4) to whatever the definition does not belong, to that the defined concept also does not belong §.262.",[]],"meier:273":["2) A definition must be in accordance with the rules of the magnitude of learned cognition. §.66-91. It must therefore consist of the greatest, most important and most fruitful marks, of the essence, essential components and attributes, and as often as possible, of affirmative marks. §.115-121. It is therefore a fault a) if a definition consists of contingent properties: then it would not even be an exhaustive concept. §.121. b) If it consists of relations: then it would also be no exhaustive concept. §.121. c) If it consists without need of negative marks. If in a doctrinal system one is to define concepts opposed to one another, then the opposition must be evident from the definitions, and thus one of the two must be defined negatively.",[]],"meier:274":["3) A definition must be in accordance with the rules of the truth of learned cognition. §.92-114. The truth of definitions requires besides this that they must be in accordance with all rules which one must observe with definitions. A regular definition (*definitio legitima*) is in accordance with all rules of definitions; but one which is contrary to one or more of these rules is an irregular definition (*definitio illegitima*).",[]],"meier:275":["4) A definition must be in accordance with all rules of the distinctness of learned cognition. §.115-155. Yet it can be an incomplete concept. §.147. Now since all its marks must be clear §.137, one can either presuppose them as clear partly from common life, partly from other doctrinal systems; or one must first define them before one posits them as marks in the definition §.268.",[]],"meier:276":["If a definition is absolutely obscure, or has absolutely obscure marks, then it is no definition at all §.275. 125. But if it is only relatively obscure, or contains marks that are only relatively obscure, then it can indeed be a good definition, but not for him to whom it is obscure §.125.",[]],"meier:277":["The defined concept is not clearer than it itself is. In order therefore that the definition not become obscure and indistinct §.275, one must take care that the defined concept be posited neither as an immediate nor as a mediate mark in its own definition §.116. If the latter happens, then one calls this fault the return in defining (*circulus in definiendo*).",[]],"meier:278":["5) A definition must be in accordance with the rules of the certainty of learned cognition§.155-215. Consequently one must prove with every definition a) that it is either a correct concept of experience §.258, or a correct separated §.265, or a correct arbitrary concept §.267, b) that it is regular §.274.",[]],"meier:279":["6) A definition must be in accordance with the rules of practical learned cognition §.216-248. Consequently one must choose for a definition the most important and most fruitful marks, from which can most easily be proved the most useful and most serviceable of what we must investigate about the defined thing in order to cognize it in a practical way.",[]],"meier:280":["A definition either represents the essence of the defined thing, or not §.273. The former is a real definition (*definitio realis, genetica*), but the latter a nominal definition (*definitio nominalis*). A nominal definition contains either the essential components of the  defined thing, or its attri butes§.273. The former is an essential nominal definition (*definitio essentialis*), but the latter an accidental nominal definition (*definitio accidentalis*).",[]],"meier:281":["If one wants to make a real definition, then 1) one seeks to cognize the essence of the defined thing, either through mediate experience, e.g., §.257, or through separation, or through a proof from reason, or through arbitrary combination; 2) one makes of the essence a determinate concept §.280.",[]],"meier:282":["If one wants to make a nominal definition, then one seeks 1) to cog nize the essential components or attributes of the thing to be defined, either from mediate experience §.256, or through a proof from reason, or from the essence, if it is already known to us. 2) One selects those essential components and attributes, and as many of them, as are necessary for a perfect regular definition §.270-279.",[]],"meier:283":["Since the things to be defined can have many essential components and attributes, one can select at one's pleasure those which make up a nominal definition §.282; if only one otherwise observes the rules of definitions. And to that extent nominal definitions are arbitrary.",[]],"meier:284":["Just as we cannot define all concepts §.139.150. 268, so we also need not define all concepts, but only those that belong in the horizon of our learned cognition, and without whose definition our learned cognition could not reach the required degree of its perfection. Excess in defining is called the mania for defining (*pruritus definiendi*).",[]],"meier:285":["The logical division of concepts (*divisio logica*) consists in a distinct representation of all lower concepts that are opposed to one another, and that are contained under one and the very same higher concept. This higher concept is called the divided concept (*divisum*), and the lower concepts, the members of the division (*membra dividentia*). If one accordingly wants to make a logical division, 1) one takes a separated concept; 2) one discovers all differences of its lower concepts that are opposed to one another; 3) these differences one combines one after another, through an opposition, with the higher concept. E.g., all concepts are either obscure or clear concepts.",[]],"meier:286":["A division of a member of the division is called a subdivision (*subdivisio*), and different divisions of a concept, which are made in different aims, are called codivisions (*codivisiones*). It is clear by itself what the divided concept of the subdivision (*subdivisum*), and of the codivisions (*codivisum*) is. Likewise the members of the subdivision (*membra subdividentia*), and of the codivisions (*membra codividentia*) are.",[]],"meier:287":["If a logical division is to be correct, then 1) the divided concept must not be wider than the members of the division, when they are taken together through an opposition (*disiunctive*); or no member of the division must be left out, because otherwise not all lower concepts that are opposed to one another would be adduced §.285. 262. 2) The divided concept must not be narrower than the members of the division, when they are taken together through an opposition §.262. Otherwise one would mix among the  members of the division a concept which is no lower concept of the divided concept §.285. Consequently the divided concept, and the members of the division, when they are taken together through an opposition, are reciprocal concepts §.262, and the division must exhaust the divided concept.",[]],"meier:288":["3) The members of the division must be opposed in such a way that none belongs to the other §.285. 260. 4) The divided concept must contradict no member of the division: for it is contained in it §.285. 260. 5) The members of the subdivision must not be posited under the members of the division: otherwise not all members would be opposed to one another n. 3. 4. §.286. 6) The number of the members of the division must be determined merely through the nature of the divided concept, and of the aim in which it is divided.",[]],"meier:289":["To whatever the divided concept belongs, to that also *one* member of the division belongs; to whatever *one* member of the division belongs, to that also the divided concept belongs; to whatever the divided concept does not belong, to that none of the members of the division belongs; to whatever none of the members of the division belongs, to that the divided concept also does not belong §.287.",[]],"meier:290":["Logical divisions 1) further the invention of learned concepts through arbitrary combination §.285. 266. 2) Serve us in putting our abstracted concepts into a proper order and combination, and in retaining them all the more easily; 3) convince us of the universality of our abstract cognition.",[]],"meier:291":["One must take care with divisions that one does not, together with their subdivisions, heap them up too much, because otherwise a great confusion arises; and one must avoid in them everything forced. on learned judgments.",[]],"meier:292":["Concepts that belong to one another §.260 agree with one another; but those that do not belong to one another are contrary to one another, or conflict with one another (*repugnare*). The agreement and the conflict of several concepts are the logical relations of concepts (*logica conceptuum relatio*). A judgment (*iudicium*) is a representation of a logical relation of some concepts; and insofar as everything is abstracted from it without which its truth can be cognized in a learned way, it is called a logical judgment (*iudicium logicum*), which is a learned judgment (*iudicium eruditum*) if it is in accord with all the rules of learned cognition, as much as is possible.",[]],"meier:293":["That concept of which we represent to ourselves in a judgment that another belongs or does not belong to it is the subject (*subiectum*); the other, on the contrary, of which we represent to ourselves that it belongs or does not belong to the subject, is the predicate (*praedicatum*). The representation of the agreement of several concepts is the concept of combination (*copula*). Negation (*negatio*) is the representation of the absence of a thing, and the representation of the absence of the concept of combination is the negation of the concept of combination (*negatio copulae*). It is therefore the representation of the conflict of the predicate with the subject §.292.",[]],"meier:294":["In a logical judgment we represent to ourselves either that the predicate belongs to the subject, or does not belong §.292. 293. The former is an affirming judgment (*iudicium affirmans, affirmativum*), the latter a negating one (*iudicium negans, negativum*). E.g., the soul can think, matter cannot think. In a negating judgment there is the negation of the concept of combination §.293. And if in a judgment there is a negation either in the subject or the predicate, or in both at once, if only the concept of combination is not negated, then it is an affirming judgment, which is called an infinite judgment (*iudicium infinitum*). One can therefore transform all negating judgments into affirming ones, if one puts the negation away from the concept of combination to the predicate. E.g., the soul is not mortal, the soul is immortal.The constitution of judgments (*qualitas iudicii*) consists in their affirmation and negation.",[]],"meier:295":["An affirming judgment is true if the predicate belongs to the subject, and indeed just as the former is affirmed of the latter; but it is false if the predicate does not belong to the subject, at least does not belong to it in the way in which it is affirmed of it. A negating judgment is true if the predicate does not belong to the subject, and indeed in the way in which it is negated of it; but it is false if the predicate belongs to the subject, at least is not so contrary as it is negated of it §.294. 99. If a true judgment is held to be false, and a false one to be true, then it is an erroneous judgment §.109.",[]],"meier:296":["The truth and incorrectness of a judgment lies in the concept of combination and in its negation §.295. 293. Consequently 1) the subject and predicate of a false judgment can be true; 2) the subject and predicate of a true affirming judgment can be false; 3) the subject can be true and the predicate false, or conversely, in a true negating judgment.",[]],"meier:297":["All true judgments have a ground and a sufficient ground of their truth §.16. This ground is called the condition of judgments (*hypothesis, conditio iudicii*). Consequently the truth and incorrectness of judgments can be cognized from it. It is accordingly the distinguishing mark and the ground of proof of truth §.94. 191.",[]],"meier:298":["The conditions of judgments are 1) either sufficient or insufficient conditions §.297.119. 191; 2) either internal or external conditions §.297. 94; 3) either absolutely necessary or contingent conditions. The former are the essence, the essential parts, the attributes or the definition of the subject, and the latter its contingent constitutions and relations §.297. 121. 273. If the contingent condition of a judgment is combined with its subject, then it is called the determination or restriction of the judgment (*determinatio et limitatio iudicii*).",[]],"meier:299":["If the condition of a judgment is 1) an internal, absolutely necessary and sufficient condition, then it is inseparable from the subject. Whether one thinks it or not, it is nevertheless there, and consequently the predicate also belongs or does not belong to the subject, according as it either affirms or negates. Truth then does not require that one combine this condition with the judgment §.295. But if it is 2) a contingent condition, then it is now there, now not there, and the judgment would be now true, now not true. Truth accordingly requires that one combine these conditions with the judgment. A determinate judgment (*iudicium determinatum, limitatum*) is a judgment that has a determination; a judgment that is not determinate is an indeterminate judgment (*iudicium indeterminatum, illimitatum*).",[]],"meier:300":["The analysis of a judgment (*analysis, resolutio iudicii*) consists in one's paying attention successively to all of its parts. All judgments can be analyzed §.139, and through this labor one finds not only the proofs of judgments §.297; but one also learns to grasp them rightly, and to present them to others.",[]],"meier:301":["The subject of a judgment is either a singular or an abstract concept §.293. 260. The former is a singular judgment (*iudicium singulare*), the latter a common judgment (*iudicium commune*). Which affirms or negates the predicate either of all those contained under the subject, or of some. The former is a universal judgment (*iudicium universale*), the latter a particular judgment (*iudicium particulare*). The last is either at the same time universally true, a not merely particular judgment (*iudicium non tantum particulare*), or not, a merely particular judgment (*iudicium tantum particulare*). All these judgments either affirm or negate §.294. The universal affirming judgments are called A; the universally negating E; the particularly affirming I; and the particularly negating O. The predicate of all universal judgments is, with regard to the subject, a universal concept, because the negating can be transformed into affirming ones §.294. 262. And because in singular as well as in universal judgments it is judged that the predicate belongs or does not belong to the whole subject; one can count singular judgments among universal ones. Insofar as a judgment is either a singular or a common one, to that extent one ascribes to it a magnitude (*quantitas iudicii*).",[]],"meier:302":["A universally affirming judgment is true if the predicate belongs to all those contained under the subject §.301. 295. It is therefore false if the predicate belongs to not a single one of those contained under the subject, or does not belong to some, or even only to a single one of them. A universally negating judgment is true if the predicate is contrary to all those contained under the subject §.301. 295. It is therefore false if the predicate belongs to all those contained under the subject, or to some of them, or even only to a single one of them.",[]],"meier:303":["If the sufficient condition of a common judgment 1) is absolutely necessary in the subject, then it is inseparable from it, and is found where the subject is found, consequently in all those contained under it §.263. Thus the judgment is then universally true, for where the condition is, there is also the predicate §.299. If therefore a predicate is affirmed or negated of an abstract concept for the sake of its essence, or essential part, or attribute, or definition, then the judgment is universally true §.298. 299. 2) If this condition is a determination, then it becomes absolutely necessary as soon as it is combined with the subject, because thereby all things are excluded from it to which this determination does not belong §.299. And thus the determinate judgment is universally true.",[]],"meier:304":["A judgment has either only *one* subject and *one* predicate, or several. The former is a simple judgment (*iudicium simplex*), but the latter a composite one (*iudicium compositum*). If the subject and predicate are composed of several concepts, then either some of them are thought for the sake of the rest, or none is thought for the sake of the other. In the first case the concepts for the sake of which the rest were thought are the principal subjects and principal predicates (*subiectum et praedicatum principale*), and the rest the subordinate subjects and subordinate predicates (*subiectum et praedicatum minus principale*). In the other case the composite judgment is a copulative judgment (*iudicium copulativum*).",[]],"meier:305":["A judgment that affirms that a judgment follows from the condition, without either the former or the latter being given out as true or false, is a conditioned judgment (*iudicium hypotheticum, conditionale*). The condition of conditioned judgments is called the first, or preceding (*prius, antecedens*), but the judgment that follows from it the last or following (*posterius, consequens*). Accordingly not all judgments are conditioned that have a condition §.297.",[]],"meier:306":["For the truth of conditioned judgments it is not required that the first and last be true; but that it have a correct sequence, or that the first be the sufficient ground of the truth of the last. In the opposite case the conditioned judgment is false §.305. 295.",[]],"meier:307":["A disjunctive judgment (*iudicium disiunctivum*) is a judgment that affirms that among several judgments one is true and the rest false, yet in such a way that it is not determined which is true and which is false. The several judgments of which it is composed are called the members of the disjunction, or of the opposition (*membra disiunctionis, disiunctiva*), e.g., the soul is either simple, or composite.",[]],"meier:308":["If a disjunctive judgment is to be true, then the members of the disjunction must relate to one another just as one represents to oneself in it §.295. Consequently 1) not more members than one must be true. If therefore all members, or even only two, are true at the same time, then the judgment is false; 2) One member must necessarily be true; if therefore all members are false, or one is only contingently true, then the judgment is false; 3) no member must be left out, for even if the one left out were false, it would still not be evident from the disjunction that among those adduced *one* is necessarily true §.307.",[]],"meier:309":["The representation of the way and manner in which the predicate belongs or does not belong to the subject is the determination of the concept of combination and of its negation (*modus formalis*). A judgment either has such a determination, or not. The former is an impure judgment (*iudicium modale, modificatum, complexum qua copulam*), but the latter a pure judgment (*iudicium purum*), e.g., this world exists necessarily, it does not exist necessarily. In the truth of impure judgments one must pay attention especially to the determination of the concept of combination and of its negation.",[]],"meier:310":["A judgment that is composed of an affirming and a negating one in a very concealed way is called an exponible judgment (*iudicium exponibile*). If it is to be true, then both judgments must be correct, and one must accordingly, in order to assure oneself of this, analyze it. E.g., GOD alone is absolutely immortal.",[]],"meier:311":["Judgments that at the same time represent the state of the mind with regard to a certain judgment are judgments that are not logical (*iudicia non logica*). For example: O how greatly the sinner deceives himself! Such judgments are very practical, and so that learned cognition does not become merely learned, one must guard against all learned judgments being simple or merely logical.",[]],"meier:312":["All learned judgments are either deliberative judgments (*iudicia theoretica*), or judgments of exercise (*iudicia practica*) §.217. The latter judge that something ought to be done or left undone, e.g., we must observe the laws; but the former not, e.g., virtue makes us happy.",[]],"meier:313":["All learned judgments are either demonstrable (*iudicia demonstrativa*), or indemonstrable judgments (*iudicia indemonstrabilia*). The truth of the latter is evident from themselves, as soon as we cognize them distinctly; but the former cannot be certain without proof §.192.",[]],"meier:314":["The predicate of an affirming judgment is either the same as the subject, or it is different from it. The former is an empty judgment (*iudicium identicum*), which is either wholly empty (*iudicium ex toto identicum*), or in part (*iudicium ex parte identicum*). The predicate of the former is not at all different from the subject, but the predicate of the latter is only a part of the subject. Because no concept is contrary to itself, we cognize the truth of all empty judgments as soon as we understand them §.295; they are therefore indemonstrable §.313. If a judgment is not empty, then one must fear that between the subject and predicate there could be so great a difference that they do not belong to one another. Consequently they are not indemonstrable, and there are therefore, besides the empty ones, no indemonstrable judgments §.313.",[]],"meier:315":["The indemonstrable judgments are either deliberative judgments, or judgments of exercise. The former are fundamental judgments (*axioma*), but the latter demanding judgments (*postulatum*). One must hold no demonstrable judgment to be an indemonstrable one §.313.",[]],"meier:316":["As often as we affirm a concept of itself, or a part of it of it, so often we have a fundamental judgment §.315.314. One can therefore find fundamental judgments from definitions if one affirms of the defined concept: 1) the whole definition, and insofar logical definitions are indemonstrable; 2) some marks of the definition; 3) its individual marks §.268.",[]],"meier:317":["As often as I represent to myself a thing that one can consider as an effect, and I affirm of him who wants to produce it that he must produce it or a part of it, so often I have a demanding judgment §.315.314. If therefore the defined concept can be considered as an effect, and I affirm of him who wants to produce it that he must produce 1) the whole definition, 2) or some marks, or 3) individual marks: then I find demanding judgments from the definitions §.268.",[]],"meier:318":["In a demonstration from reason all grounds of proof must be completely certain §.193.204; they are therefore either demonstrable or not §.313. In the first case they must be proved again. Consequently a proof does not become a demonstration until I come to nothing but indemonstrable grounds of proof. The empty judgments, the fundamental judgments and demanding judgments are accordingly the first beginnings of all demonstrations from reason §.314. 315. Then the understanding is completely at rest, when the proof has been carried on to such judgments.",[]],"meier:319":["The demonstrable judgments are either certain merely through experience, or not. The former are intuiting judgments (*iudicium intuitivum*), but the latter derived judgments (*iudicium discursivum*). The intuiting judgment consists of nothing but concepts of experience, and is an immediate experience §.201, and a singular judgment §.301. No intuiting judgment is indemonstrable §.313. 314, for I must always recall a singular case, and from this cognize how and whether I have arrived at such a judgment without deception §.202. All intuiting judgments are the first beginnings of all demonstrations from experience §.202.",[]],"meier:320":["If one wants to find an intuiting judgment, then 1) take the thing that one senses as the subject; 2) analyze the sensation, according to §.142. 257; 3) affirm the discovered marks of the subject §.319.",[]],"meier:321":["The predicates of intuiting judgments can be contingent constitutions, alterations, relations, effects, causes, actions and passions; but never the essence, the essential parts, the attributes, and negating marks §.256. No negating judgment is an intuiting judgment, although it can be derived from an intuiting judgment, inasmuch as the marks that are opposed to those that we sense in the subject can be negated of it with truth §.295.",[]],"meier:322":["From intuiting judgments universal ones are derived: 1) If one passes an intuiting judgment, according to §.320, of all things of one species, and then concludes that the predicate can be universally affirmed of the whole species §.263. 2) If one wants to derive a universal one from *one* intuiting judgment, then a) seek the higher concept under which the subject belongs, according to §.259. b) Seek the condition of the intuiting judgment. c) Investigate whether it is absolutely necessary in the higher concept, or contingent. In the latter case combine it with the subject, and then in both cases one can affirm the predicate universally of the higher concept §.299.",[]],"meier:323":["The derived judgments are demonstrated either from experience or from reason §.319. The former are mediate experiences §.203. 204.A judgment of experience is every judgment that is certain through experience, whether it be an intuiting judgment §.319, or a derived judgment.",[]],"meier:324":["The derived judgments require either a shorter or a longer proof §.319. The former are called additions (*consectarium, corollarium*), and they can be either deliberative judgments or judgments of exercise §.312. They are for the most part adduced without proof, and easily found if one compares a few definitions, indemonstrable judgments and other truths with one another.",[]],"meier:325":["The derived judgments that require a longer proof (either from experience or from reason) are either deliberative judgments, or judgments of exercise §.312. The former are called theorems (*theorema*), but the latter problems (*problema*).",[]],"meier:326":["If one wants to invent a theorem, then one must 1) invent the judgment, or it can already be known; 2) one must invent a longer proof, consequently a) one must seek out all grounds of proof, whether they be already known to us, or one learn them from others, or first invent them anew; b) one must put them into a distinct interconnection, and c) one must understand the art of composing a proof out of many grounds of proof without causing a confusion §.325.",[]],"meier:327":["Whoever wants to exercise himself in the invention of theorems must make the beginning with the easier ones, and with such as can be tested through experience, so that he may all the more easily recognize his missteps.",[]],"meier:328":["For the sake of distinctness a problem is analyzed into three parts: 1) The question (*quaestio problematis*) is the representation of the action that ought to be done or omitted. As often as we represent to ourselves a thing that can be considered as an effect, so often can one raise a question. 2) The solution (*solutio problematis*) analyzes the manner of origination of the question. It must therefore adduce either all the actions of which the question consists, or all the causes, or both at once, and if one wants to find it, one must logically define the object of the question. 3) The proof of the problem (*demonstratio problematis*), which demonstrates that through the observance of the solution the question becomes actual. It accordingly presupposes the solution as a condition, and the whole problem can be regarded as a conditioned theorem §.305.The test of the problem (*proba, examen problematis*) is that whereby one is convinced that one has observed the solution.",[]],"meier:329":["If one can discover no impossibility in the question, then one can venture upon the solution, and one can invent it either through experience, or from reason and through abstraction, or in an arbitrary way. If one wants to find it through experience, then one must 1) seek to be present at the thing when it originates, and make for oneself a distinct concept of its manner of origination through experience §.257. 2) One must exactly observe all causes, and everything that goes on in the originating, namely if it falls within our senses. 3) The remaining causes, and the remaining parts of the origination, which do not fall within our senses, one must seek to guess, by means of that part of learning in whose domain the object belongs. 4) Compare the effect whose manner of origination we cannot experience with another that we can resolve through experience, and conclude: that the former originates in a similar way and through similar causes.",[]],"meier:330":["If one assumes an effect merely arbitrarily, 1) then one must be well versed in those parts of learning where it belongs, make for oneself a distinct concept of it, and reflect upon all forces and causes that are known to us, whether we might perhaps come upon something that belongs in the solution of the question. 2) If one arbitrarily connects many actions and causes with one another, and pays attention to what comes out, then one also finds many a solution to many a problem.",[]],"meier:331":["In the midst of a system of doctrine one comes, through the pursuit of demonstrations from reason, unnoticed upon many solutions; and if one has already found the solutions of the lower questions, then one need only abstract their difference, and one has the solution of the higher question, whether it speak of an abstract action, or of another abstract effect.",[]],"meier:332":["For the sake of the distinctness of the problem, one must find, from the distinct concepts of the effect and of the causes contained in the solution, what each cause contributes to the effect.",[]],"meier:333":["In order to preserve the brevity of the problems in a system of doctrine, 1) one must in what precedes resolve the actions and effects of which the actions and effects of the following problems are composed; secondly 2) one must in what precedes resolve those actions and effects under which the questions of the following problems are contained.",[]],"meier:334":["A problem is 1) true (*veritas problematis*) if it contains nothing impossible in itself, consequently if the solution is not contrary to the question; 2) complete (*completum problema*) if through the observance of the solution that follows which the question is about; 3) exact (*problema accuratum, adaequatum*) if it contains neither too little nor too much in itself. And these are three perfections of problems.",[]],"meier:335":["A problem is false 1) if the solution is absolutely impossible; 2) if the solution is impossible in a certain respect, e.g., if it is not possible through the powers of human beings, or in certain circumstances; 3) if the solution can indeed become actual, but contributes nothing to the question; 4) if it even contradicts the question §.334.",[]],"meier:336":["A problem is incomplete 1) if through the most exact observance of the solution the end is not attained; 2) if one does not know how the solution can be made actual; 3) if the solution cannot be carried out in all cases where it is needed §.334.",[]],"meier:337":["A problem is not exact if it is indeed true and complete, but leads by detours, and contains too much in itself §.334.",[]],"meier:338":["Occult qualities (*qualitates occultae*) are constitutions of which we have no clear and distinct cognition, and which we assume without sufficient ground. Now since they conflict with the nature of learned cognition §.21. 168, he who assumes them is either stupid, or a deceiver. Consequently one must especially avoid them when one investigates the grounds of things, of their nature and alterations, consequently also in the solution of problems.",[]],"meier:339":["A borrowed judgment (*lemma*) is a demonstrable judgment that is assumed in a system of doctrine without proof, because it belongs with its proof to another system of doctrine.",[]],"meier:340":["Remarks (*scholia*) are judgments that in a system of doctrine are neither grounds of proof, nor are proved, but which have been mixed in among the rest for the sake of greater utility.",[]],"meier:341":["Equivalent judgments (*iudicia aequipollentia*) are different judgments in which the logical relation of one and the very same subject and predicate is the same. E.g., not all human beings are virtuous, some human beings are not virtuous; not no human being is learned, some human beings are learned; everything has a ground, nothing is without ground. Now since the truth and incorrectness of a judgment is met with in the logical relation §.295. 296, 1) all the remaining equivalent judgments are true if *one* is true; and 2) all the remaining false if *one* is false. The equivalence of judgments depends on such modifications of the judgment as do not alter its truth or incorrectness; otherwise they are not equivalent judgments.",[]],"meier:342":["Judgments that have the same subject, predicate and constitution, and among which only *one* is universal, contain one another in themselves (*iudicia subalternata*).The universal contains the others in itself (*iudicium subalternans*), and the rest are contained in it (*iudicium subalternatum*). E.g., all human beings can err, some human beings can err, *one* human being can err; no human being is sinless, some human beings are not sinless, *one* human being is not sinless. 1) If the universal judgment is true, then those that are contained in it are also true, but not conversely; 2) if the judgments that are contained in the universal are false, then the universal is also false, but not conversely §.302.",[]],"meier:343":["Judgments contradict one another (*iudicia contradictoria*) of which the one negates accurately what the other affirms. E.g., the judgments A and O, likewise E and I contradict one another, if they have one and the very same subjects and predicates §.301. 341. Now since every concept either belongs to every other concept, or not: for it cannot at all be thought that both at once or neither of the two could be; so among all contradictory judgments either the affirming is true, and the negating false, or the negating true and the affirming false §.295. Consequently one can conclude 1) from the truth of the one among contradictory judgments to the incorrectness of the other; and 2) from the incorrectness of the one to the truth of the other.",[]],"meier:344":["If a judgment particularly affirms what the other particularly negates, then they are opposed to one another in a particular way (*iudicia subcontraria*). Consequently the judgments I and O, if they have the same subjects and predicates. 1) These judgments can be true at the same time. For since their predicate can be, with regard to the subject, a particular concept §.262; it can be particularly affirmed and negated of it with truth §.295. 2) These judgments can never both be false at the same time. For if I and O are false, then A and E are also false §.342. Consequently A and O, E and I, which have the same predicates and subjects, could be false at the same time, which is impossible §.343.",[]],"meier:345":["If a judgment universally affirms what the other universally negates, consequently if A and E have the same subject and predicate, then they are opposed to one another in a universal way (*iudicia contraria.*) 1) These judgments can both be false. For their predicate can be, with regard to the subject, a particular concept §.262. Consequently it can be neither universally affirmed, nor universally negated of it §.302. 2) These judgments cannot both be true. For if A and E were true, then I and O would also be true §.342. Consequently A and O, likewise E and I, although they had the same subject and predicate, could be true at once, and that is impossible §.343.",[]],"meier:346":["That alteration by virtue of which the predicate is made out of the subject of a judgment, and the subject out of the predicate, is called the conversion of a judgment (*conversio iudicii*). The judgment with which the alteration takes place is called the converted (*iudicium conversum*), and which arises from this, that converting judgment (*iudicium convertens*). In conversion either the magnitude of the judgment is changed, or not. In the first case the conversion takes place contingently (*conversio per accidens*), but in the other simply (*conversio simplex*). Insofar as a true judgment also remains true after conversion, to that extent it can be converted (*iudicium converti potest, iudicium reciprocabile*).",[]],"meier:347":["All affirming judgments whose subject and predicate are reciprocal concepts can be simply converted, whether they be universal or singular judgments. For the subject also belongs to all those contained under the predicate §.262. Consequently it can be universally affirmed of the predicate §.302. 346. E.g., the judgments that affirm the definition of the defined concept, and the members of the division, taken together under a disjunction, of the divided concept §.270. 287.",[]],"meier:348":["All true particularly affirming judgments can be simply converted. For the abstract concept of the subject belongs to the some contained under it §.260, and can therefore be particularly affirmed of them §.295. And since the predicate belongs to some contained under the subject §.295, it can, as their higher concept §.260, be taken as the subject. Consequently the former subject can be particularly affirmed of the former predicate §.346.",[]],"meier:349":["A universally negating judgment can be converted 1) simply. Otherwise the subject would have to be not contrary to all those contained under the predicate §.302, consequently it would have to belong to some of them §.343. Consequently the predicate would also have to belong to some contained under the subject §.348. Consequently the universally negating judgment would be false §.302, which is absurd; 2) contingently. For since it can be simply converted, the particular judgment is also true which is contained in the universal converting judgment §.342, and through it it is contingently converted §.346.",[]],"meier:350":["All universally affirming judgments can be contingently converted. For if they are true, then the particularly affirming judgments contained in them are also true §.342. These can be simply converted §.348, and precisely thereby the universal ones are contingently converted §.346.",[]],"meier:351":["If in particularly negating judgments one puts the negation to the predicate, then they become particularly affirming judgments §.294, and can therefore then be simply converted §.348.",[]],"meier:352":["A universally affirming judgment is contraposed (*contrapositio*) if one transforms its predicate into a negating concept, and universally negates the former subject of it. All true universally affirming judgments can be contraposed, that is, to whatever their predicate does not belong, to those their subject also does not belong. Otherwise the predicate would have to be contrary to some things, and the subject belong to them §.343. One could therefore with truth negate the predicate of some contained under the subject §.295. Thus the universally affirming judgment would be false §.302, and that is absurd.on learned syllogisms.",[]],"meier:353":["If some true judgments contain the sufficient ground of the truth of another, then they are combined with one another §.15, and in this relation of true judgments consists the interconnection of truths (*nexus veritatum*).",[]],"meier:354":["A syllogism (*ratiocinium*) is a distinct representation of the interconnection of truths; which, if it is perfect in a higher degree, is called a learned, or a logical syllogism (*ratiocinium logicum, eruditum*).",[]],"meier:355":["In a syllogism we derive a truth from other truths §.353, 354. And since these other truths are thus the grounds of proof of the former §.191, we make a syllogism when we distinctly derive a truth from its grounds of proof. Consequently the distinct representation of the sequence of a proof is a syllogism §.191.",[]],"meier:356":["That judgment which in a syllogism is derived from others is the concluding judgment (*conclusio, probandum, principiatum*). But those judgments from which the concluding judgment is derived are the premise judgments (*praemissae, data, sumtiones, principia*).",[]],"meier:357":["The subjects and predicates of the judgments of which a syllogism consists are called the principal concepts of a syllogism (*termini*). The subject of the concluding judgment is the smaller principal concept (*terminus minor*), but its predicate the greater principal concept (*terminus maior*). The principal concept which, besides the smaller and greater, is encountered in the premise judgments, is called the middle principal concept (*terminus medius*).",[]],"meier:358":["Since the premise judgments contain the argument of the concluding judgment §.355.356, but in them, besides the parts of the concluding judgment, nothing further is contained than the middle principal concept §.357; so it is the argument §.191, consequently the condition of the concluding judgment §.297. One therefore finds the middle principal concept according to the guidance of the 297th and 298th paragraphs; and a syllogism consists in this, when we derive a judgment from its condition in a distinct way §.355.",[]],"meier:359":["The matter of the syllogism (*ratiocinii materia*) consists in its premise judgments, but its form (*ratiocinii forma*) in the consequence of the concluding judgment from the premise judgments.",[]],"meier:360":["A correct syllogism (*ratiocinium verum*) must be correct both in the matter and also in the form §.359.355. 193. If therefore either the matter is false, or the form, or both at the same time, then it is a false incorrect syllogism (*ratiocinium falsum*). An erroneous syllogism (*ratiocinium erroneum*) is a false syllogism insofar as it is held to be a correct one §.109. From §.193 and 194 it is evident when a syllogism has a demonstrative certainty, or not.",[]],"meier:361":["Because the consequence in every syllogism must be distinct §.355, but no distinctness is possible without order §.142; so the judgments of a syllogism must be duly ordered together. Now since no order is possible without rules, certain rules of inferring are necessary, according to which a syllogism must be arranged if it is to have a correct and distinct consequence.",[]],"meier:362":["It is impossible that something at the same time be, and not be. Or, if of a thing one and the very same is at the same time affirmed or denied, then it is nothing. This proposition is called the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*), and it is the first inner mark of truth §.95, on which all consequences of syllogisms must rest if they are to be true §.361.",[]],"meier:363":["What can with truth be universally affirmed or denied of a concept, that can also with truth be affirmed or denied of every other concept which belongs under the former §.342. Otherwise two contradictory judgments would have to be true at once §.343, and that is against the principle of contradiction §.362. This proposition is called the inference from the universal to the particular (*dictum de omni et nullo*).",[]],"meier:364":["If the sufficient ground is true, then its consequence is also true, otherwise the sufficient ground would be no sufficient ground §.15, which is against §.362. Consequently, if the consequence is false, then the sufficient ground is also false, because otherwise the sufficient ground could be without consequence. This inference is called the inference from the sufficient ground to its consequence (*a ratione sufficiente ad rationatum valet consequentia*).",[]],"meier:365":["If one among contradictory judgments is true, then the other is false; and if the one is false, then the other is true §.343.362. This proposition is called: the inference from the opposite (*ab uno oppositorum ad alterum valet consequentia*).",[]],"meier:366":["If a judgment is true, then that one must also be true which has arisen through a logical alteration of the first, which can be undertaken without detriment to the truth. Otherwise the logical alteration would have to be detrimental to the truth, and not be detrimental, which is impossible §.362. This inference is called: the inference from the logical alteration of a true judgment.",[]],"meier:367":["An ordinary syllogism (*ratiocinium ordinarium*) is a syllogism in which there is inference from the universal to the particular §.363. For example: all virtues contribute something to my happiness; now all philosophical virtues are virtues, therefore all philosophical virtues contribute something to my happiness. All remaining syllogisms are extraordinary syllogisms (*ratiocinium extraordinarium*).",[]],"meier:368":["All ordinary syllogisms have two premise judgments, which have the middle principal concept in common with one another §.367. That premise judgment in them which contains the greater principal concept is called the major premise of the ordinary syllogisms (*propositio maior ratiociniorum ordinariorum*); but that which contains the smaller principal concept, their minor premise (*propositio minor*).",[]],"meier:369":["That genus of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle principal concept stands in the subject of the major premise and in the predicate of the minor premise is called the first figure (*figura prima ratiociniorum*). For example: all human beings can err; the learned are human beings, therefore the learned can err. In this figure the minor premise and the concluding proposition have One subject §.368. Consequently these two judgments always have the same magnitude §.301.",[]],"meier:370":["That genus of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle principal concept stands in the predicate of both premise judgments is called the second figure (*figura secunda*). E.g. no absurd thing is true, everything that stands in the Bible is true; therefore nothing that stands in the Bible is absurd.",[]],"meier:371":["That genus of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle principal concept stands in the subject of both premise judgments is called the third figure (*figura tertia*). For example: all learned men have an improved understanding, some learned men are vicious; therefore some vicious men have an improved understanding.",[]],"meier:372":["That genus of ordinary syllogisms in which the middle principal concept stands in the predicate of the major premise, and in the subject of the minor premise, is called the fourth, or the Galenic figure (*figura quarta, galenica*). For example: no stupid human being is learned, some learned men are pious; therefore some pious men are not stupid people.",[]],"meier:373":["The figures of syllogisms (*figurae ratiociniorum*) are accordingly different genera of ordinary syllogisms, which arise from the different ordering together of the middle principal concept with the remaining principal concepts in the premise judgments; and there are no more than four figures §.369. 372.",[]],"meier:374":["From the inference from the universal to the particular §.363 flow the following rules of all ordinary syllogisms: 1) In an ordinary syllogism neither more nor fewer principal concepts can be contained than three. Such an inference contains only three judgments, consequently only six places for the principal concepts §.368.357. Now it contains 1) a principal concept of which something is universally affirmed or denied, and of which it is said in the other premise judgment that another concept belongs to it. It therefore occurs twice, because it is the middle principal concept §.368. 2) The smaller principal concept occurs in the concluding judgment and the minor premise §.357. 368; and 3) the greater principal concept in the concluding judgment and the major premise §.357. 368.",[]],"meier:375":["2) The middle principal concept must not be posited in the concluding judgment. For in ordinary syllogisms the greater principal concept is affirmed or denied of the smaller in the concluding proposition, because according to the statement of the premise judgments the smaller principal concept belongs to the middle one, of which the greater can be affirmed or denied §.364. Consequently the concluding proposition can consist only of the smaller and greater principal concept.",[]],"meier:376":["3) The premise judgments may not all deny, for One must affirm that the smaller or greater principal concept is combined with the middle one §.363, or from nothing but negative premise judgments nothing follows, although from nothing but affirmative ones something follows. If the middle principal concept is a negative concept, then at least one premise judgment is merely infinite §.294, and then it merely seems as if all premise judgments denied.",[]],"meier:377":["4) The premise judgments may not all be particular judgments; otherwise one would not infer from the universal to the particular §.363, or from nothing but particular premise judgments nothing follows, although from nothing but universal ones something can follow.",[]],"meier:378":["5) If a premise judgment denies, then the concluding judgment must also deny: for then the syllogism conforms to the negative part of the inference from the universal to the particular §.363, and it must therefore have a negative concluding judgment.",[]],"meier:379":["6) If a premise judgment is a particular judgment, then the concluding judgment must also be a particular one; for then one infers: because some things of a kind belong to that concept of which something is universally affirmed or denied, this can also be affirmed or denied of the some §.363.",[]],"meier:380":["7) The concluding judgment always conforms to the weaker part of the syllogism: for the negative and particular premise judgments are called the weaker part of the syllogism (*pars ratiocinii debilior*) §.378. 379.",[]],"meier:381":["8) In the concluding judgment there must not be contained less than in the premise judgments. For otherwise the smaller and greater principal concept would contain less in themselves in the concluding judgment than in the premise judgments, and there would therefore be encountered in the syllogism more than three principal concepts §.374.",[]],"meier:382":["9) In the concluding judgment there must not be contained more than in the premise judgments. Otherwise the smaller and greater principal concept would contain more in the concluding judgment than in the premise judgments, and there would therefore be more than three principal concepts in the syllogism §.374.",[]],"meier:383":["Besides these rules two further rules must be observed in the first figure: 1) The minor premise must in the first figure always affirm. For since it has for its subject the subject of the concluding proposition §.369, it affirms of it that it belongs to the middle principal concept §.363. If the middle principal concept is negative, then the minor premise is infinite §.376, and therefore still an affirmative judgment §.294.",[]],"meier:384":["2) The major premise must in the first figure always be universal. For, if the concluding proposition is universal, then the major premise can only be universal §.379; but if it is a particular judgment, then the minor premise is also such §.369, and therefore the major premise must again be universal §.377. Because singular judgments belong to the universal ones §.301, they make no exception to the rules which demand the universality of the judgments of a syllogism.",[]],"meier:385":["The modes of ordinary syllogisms (*modi ratiociniorum ordinariorum*) are different kinds of syllogisms of one figure, which arise from the different constitution and magnitude of the judgments of a syllogism.",[]],"meier:386":["If the concluding proposition in the first figure affirms universally, then §.383. 378 the minor premise also affirms universally §.379. 369, and the major premise must also affirm §.378 and be universal §.384. 379. The mode of syllogisms in the first figure whose concluding proposition affirms universally is called Barbara, e.g. all truths are useful, all philosophical truths are truths; therefore all philosophical truths are useful.",[]],"meier:387":["If the concluding proposition in the first figure denies universally, then the minor premise must universally §.369. 379 affirm, §.383, and the major premise universally §.384. 379 deny §.380. The mode of syllogisms in the first figure which have a universally negative concluding proposition are called Celarent, e.g. no vice makes me more perfect, all pride is a vice; therefore no pride makes me more perfect.",[]],"meier:388":["If the concluding proposition in the first figure affirms particularly, then the minor premise must particularly §.369. 380 affirm §.383. 378, and the major premise universally §.384. 377 affirm §.378. The mode of syllogisms in the first figure whose concluding proposition affirms particularly is called Darii, e.g. all persistently unbelieving are damned, some learned men are persistently unbelieving; therefore some learned men are damned.",[]],"meier:389":["If the concluding proposition in the first figure denies particularly, then the minor premise must particularly §.369 affirm §.383, and the major premise universally §.384. 377 deny §.380. The mode of syllogisms in the first figure whose concluding proposition denies particularly is called Ferio; e.g. no good action is punished by GOd, some mere natural works of human beings are good actions; therefore some mere natural works of human beings are not punished by GOd. In the first figure only four modes of syllogisms are possible §.385-388.",[]],"meier:390":["All true judgments which can be concluding judgments in ordinary syllogisms are e[i]ther A or E, or I or O §.301. Consequently, in the first figure, all these judgments can be concluded §.386-389, and for this reason it is called a perfect figure. Hence the remaining figures are not necessary.",[]],"meier:391":["If, against the previous rules of inferring, one makes syllogisms whose premise propositions and concluding propositions are correct; then the latter do not follow necessarily from the former, but they are only contingently true. Such inferences are therefore no objections against the correctness of these rules.",[]],"meier:392":["A conditioned syllogism (*ratiocinium hypotheticum, conditionale, connexum*) is a syllogism which infers from the sufficient ground to the consequence §.364. Now since in no kind of judgments is the consequence of a judgment from its condition affirmed, but in the conditioned ones §.305; so these syllogisms have a conditioned premise judgment, which is called their major premise. It must have a correct consequence §.306, otherwise one infers from the stick in the corner (*argumentum a baculo ad angulum*).",[]],"meier:393":["In a conditioned syllogism one infers either 1) from the correctness of the former to the correctness of the latter §.364. 305. Then the minor premise affirms that the first is true, and the concluding proposition that the last is true (*modus ratiociniorum hypotheticorum ponens*). For example: if there is a providence of GOd, then all anxious cares are in vain: now the first is true, therefore also the last; Or 2) from the incorrectness of the latter to the incorrectness of the former §.364. 305. Then the minor premise affirms the incorrectness of the latter, and the concluding proposition the incorrectness of the former (*modus ratiociniorum hypotheticorum tollens*), e.g. if there is a blind fate, then there are no free actions; now the last is false, therefore also the first.",[]],"meier:394":["Because a thing can have several sufficient grounds, one can in conditioned syllogisms neither always infer from the incorrectness of the first to the incorrectness of the last, nor from the correctness of the last to the correctness of the first §.305.",[]],"meier:395":["The disjunctive syllogisms (*ratiocinium disiunctivum*) are syllogisms which infer from one opposite to the other §.365. Consequently they contain a disjunctive premise proposition, which is called their major premise §.307. If it is contrary to one of the rules proven in §.308, then it has no consequence, and the disjunctive syllogism is incorrect in the form §.360.",[]],"meier:396":["In the disjunctive syllogisms there is, either 1) inference from the correctness of One member of the disjunction to the incorrectness of the remaining ones §.395. 365. Then the minor premise must give out one member as true, and the concluding proposition the remaining ones as false (*modus ratiociniorum disiunctivorum ponendo tollens*). E.g. matter can either think or not, now the other is true, therefore the first is false; or 2) from the incorrectness of all members except One to the correctness of this One §.395. 365. Then in the minor premise it must be affirmed of all members except One that they are false, and in the concluding proposition of this One that it is true (*modus ratiociniorum disiunctivorum tollendo ponens*). For example: matter can either think or not; now the first is false, therefore the other is true.",[]],"meier:397":["A dilemma (*ratiocinium cornutum, crocodillinum, dilemma, trilemma etc.*) is a conditioned syllogism whose latter is a disjunctive judgment in which all members are false. The conditioned judgment whose latter is disjunctive is the major premise; the minor premise affirms that the latter is altogether false, and the concluding proposition affirms that the first is false. A dilemma must therefore be in accordance with the rules of conditioned and of disjunctive syllogisms at the same time §.392. 393. 395. For example: if this world were not the best, then either no best world would be possible, or GOd would have had no knowledge of it, or he would not have been able to create it, or he would not have willed to create it; now the last is altogether false, therefore also the first.",[]],"meier:398":["The syllogisms which infer from the logical alteration of a judgment to the judgment arisen through the alteration are called the immediate consequences (*consequentia immediata*) §.366. For example: all human beings can err, therefore some human beings can also err; or, there is a GOd, therefore it is false that there is no GOd.",[]],"meier:399":["A syllogism is either so constituted that its correct form is manifest, or it is hidden: the former is a formal syllogism (*ratiocinium formale*), but the latter a hidden one (*ratiocinium crypticum*), which can therefore seem to be incorrect in the form. In order to conceal the logical art, and to avoid pedantry, the hidden syllogisms are to be recommended.",[]],"meier:400":["To the hidden syllogisms belong chiefly the mutilated syllogisms (*enthymema*), in which a judgment is left out, that is, is not thought as distinctly as the remaining ones, because it is entirely certain and so familiar to someone that it occurs to him at once. E.g. all human beings can err, therefore I can also err.",[]],"meier:401":["To the mutilated syllogisms belong 1) the contracted syllogisms (*ratiocinium contractum*), when one merely adds the middle principal concept to the concluding proposition, yet so that it does not become a conditioned judgment. For example: this world is the best, because it has been chosen by GOd. 2) The inferences by dissection (*inductio*), which lay the following major premise at the ground, but leave it out: what can be affirmed or denied of every lower concept, that can be universally affirmed or denied of their higher concept §.263. If in the minor premise all lower concepts are adduced, then it is an exhaustive inference by dissection (*inductio completa*). For example: the first person of the Godhead is GOd, the second also, the third also; therefore all persons of the Godhead are GOd. 3) The inferences by example (*exemplum in ratiociniis*), when one affirms or denies that which one affirms or denies of a lower concept for the sake of its higher concept, of another lower concept which belongs to the very same higher concept. For example: human beings can sin, therefore all holy angels can also sin.",[]],"meier:402":["A syllogism which is incorrect in the form (*paralogismus*), when its error is hidden, is called a deceptive inference (*sophisma, fallacia, captio*).",[]],"meier:403":["A deceptive inference can arise: 1) when we connect separated things in an incorrect way, and separate connected things in an incorrect way (*sophisma sensus compositi et divisi*). For example: where there are three thalers, there are two thalers; now three and two thalers make five thalers, therefore where there are three thalers, there are five thalers. 2) When a principal concept is taken in a twofold way (*sophisma figurae dictionis*). For example: a philosopher is a genus of the learned, Leibniz is a philosopher, therefore Leibniz is a genus of the learned.",[]],"meier:404":["3) When one takes a principal concept once with a restriction and the other time without restriction (*fallacia accidentis, seu a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter, aut vice versa*). E.g. whoever says that you are an animal speaks the truth; now whoever says that you are an ass says that you are an animal, therefore he speaks the truth. 4) When one assumes a wrong middle principal concept (*fallacia medii*). E.g. whoever looks pale studies diligently; now Caius looks pale, therefore he studies diligently.",[]],"meier:405":["5) When one takes the judgment which one has proven for that which one was to have proven (*sophisma heterozeteseos*). For example: when one is to prove the immortality of the soul, and one proves its incorruptibility. 6) When one takes the judgment which one has refuted for that which one was to refute (*sophisma ignorationis elenchi*). E.g. when, against him who asserts that the soul can die, one proves that it lives eternally. 7) When a composite judgment is regarded merely as a simple one in a syllogism (*sophisma polyzeteseos*). E.g. it is either true that dogs alone among all four-footed animals have reason, or it is not true; if the first, then dogs have reason, if the last, then all four-footed animals have reason.",[]],"meier:406":["A syllogism consists either of several syllogisms, or of only One. The latter is a simple one (*ratiocinium simplex, probatio simplex*), but the former a composite syllogism (*ratiocinium compositum*). If a syllogism is composite, then the several syllogisms of which it consists either hang together or not §.353. In the latter case it is a *ratiocinium copulatum*, e.g. all spirits are immortal, GOd and all human souls are spirits, therefore they are immortal. In the first case the premise proposition of the one is the concluding proposition of the other, and it is called a series of connected syllogisms (*ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, probatio composita*). For example: what is in accordance with the laws of nature makes me more perfect, virtue is in accordance with the laws of nature, therefore virtue makes me more perfect; what makes me more perfect, to that I am obligated, virtue makes me more perfect, therefore I am obligated to it.",[]],"meier:407":["In a series of connected syllogisms there occur not only some whose concluding propositions are premise propositions of others, but also such whose premise propositions are concluding propositions of others §.406. The former are called pre-syllogisms (*prosyllogismus*), and the latter post-syllogisms (*episyllogismus*). And some judgments occur twice in such a series §.406.",[]],"meier:408":["So that one may avoid all confusion in a long series of connected syllogisms, 1) let one divide a long proof into many parts, by proving the most important premise propositions particularly, as particular theorems. 2) Let one not connect nothing but ordinary and formal syllogisms with one another. 3) Let one prove either only the major premises of the pre-syllogisms, or only their minor premises. 4) Let one derive the concluding propositions from contracted syllogisms §.401 (*epicherema*). 5) Let one leave out all judgments that occur twice §.407, and this is called a heaped syllogism (*sorites*). E.g. youth is in accordance with the laws of nature, what is in accordance with the laws of nature, that makes me more perfect, what makes me more perfect, to that I am obligated; therefore I am obligated to virtue.",[]],"meier:409":["If the syllogisms of which the heaped syllogism consists are of the first figure, and the minor premises of the post-syllogisms are left out, then it is a common heaped syllogism (*sorites communis*). E.g. the example adduced before. But if it consists of nothing but conditioned premise propositions, then it is called a conditioned one (*sorites hypotheticus*). E.g. if the human being has a restricted understanding, then he has confused representations; if he has confused representations, then he can err; if he can err, then he can sin: therefore, if the human being has a restricted understanding, then he can sin.",[]],"meier:410":["If in a series of connected syllogisms One or several judgments are left out, then a leap in proof is committed (*saltus in probando*). The judgments left out are either certain to him who is to be convinced through the proof, and occur to him easily; or not. In the first case the leap is legitimate (*saltus legitimus*), but in the other illegitimate (*saltus illegitimus*).",[]],"meier:411":["If a concluding proposition is derived from premise propositions which are just as uncertain as it itself, then the arguments are begged (*petitio principii seu quaesiti.*) But if a concluding proposition is assumed as its own premise proposition, then one names this error the return in proof (*circulus in probando*).",[]],"meier:412":["A proof proves too little (*probatio minus probans*) if through it the whole concluding proposition does not become certain; but if from a proof there follows, besides the concluding proposition, something which is false or all too different from the concluding proposition, then it proves too much (*probatio plus probans*).",[]],"meier:413":["Through syllogisms one can 1) attain the most distinct certainty that is possible to a human being §.355. 2) Most easily resist errors; 3) discover new truths; and 4) discover all arguments that are necessary in order to erect a learned doctrinal system etc. of the method of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:414":["Method (*methodus*) is a more noticeable or greater order of thoughts. Insofar as several thoughts are either together, or follow upon one another, in the same way, insofar is there among them an order, which arises when different thoughts are coordinated to one another according to the same rules.",[]],"meier:415":["Method is in cognition §.414. 11, and therefore either an order of common cognition, common method (*methodus vulgaris*), or of rational cognition §.17. 18, rational method (*methodus rationalis*). To the former belongs aesthetic method §.19, but to the latter learned method (*methodus erudita, logica, philosophica*), the method of learned cognition §.21.",[]],"meier:416":["Learned method promotes 1) the distinctness of learned cognition §.142 n. 4. 2) Truth in a doctrinal system and thoroughness §.105. 3) The unity and the thoroughgoing interconnection of a doctrinal system. 4) The perfection of learned cognition: for all order is a perfection. 5) The retaining of truths through memory §.414.",[]],"meier:417":["The more thoughts are connected with one another, according to the more and more important common rules this connection takes place, the greater and more perfect is the method §.414. Consequently it is required for the greatest perfection of a learned cognition that it be as methodical as possible §.416.",[]],"meier:418":["A method combines thoughts either only according to a single common rule, or according to several. The former is a simple method (*methodus simplex*), but the latter a composite method (*methodus composita*). The latter is more perfect than the former §.417.",[]],"meier:419":["The difference of methods stems from the difference of the rules according to which thoughts are coordinated to one another §.414, and the difference of the rules flows from the difference of the cognitive powers, and from the different constitution of the cognition which is to be thought according to a method.",[]],"meier:420":["Since learned cognition is a distinct cognition from grounds §.21. 17, in learned method the thoughts must follow upon one another in such a way that their interconnection thereby becomes distinctly certain §.415. 419. This is the fundamental law of learned method.",[]],"meier:421":["The method of dogmatic truths is different from the method of historical ones §.419. 104. In the former the thoughts must be proven from one another, either through a demonstration, or through an insufficient proof §.191. If the first is the case, then it is called a demonstrative, or scientific method (*methodus demonstrativa, scientifica, apodictica*).",[]],"meier:422":["In the learned method of dogmatic truths nothing but thoughts are coordinated to one another which are either the grounds of the truth, distinctness and certainty of others, or the consequences, or both at once §.421. 420.15. Therefore the grounds are either placed before the consequences, or after. The former is the synthetic method (*methodus synthetica*), but the latter the analytic method (*methodus analytica*). Consequently learned method is either synthetic or analytic, whether it be demonstrative or not §.421.",[]],"meier:423":["The analytic method is very inconvenient if one wanted to treat or to learn an extensive doctrinal system according to it. But it is very well suited, 1) if one wants to draw an outline of a doctrinal system, in order afterwards to treat it according to the synthetic method; 2) if one wants to discover, from the consequences and concluding propositions, the grounds and premise propositions; 3) if one wants often to repeat a doctrinal system already learned; 4) if one wants properly to think through the single shorter parts of a doctrinal system §.422.",[]],"meier:424":["The synthetic method is always combined with many inconveniences if one makes use of it in such cases where the analytic has been recommended §.423. But it has great advantages if one makes use of it: 1) if one wants to think through and treat a doctrinal system already discovered, considered as a whole; 2) if one wants to learn it; 3) if one wants to discover from the grounds and premise propositions the consequences and concluding propositions §.422.",[]],"meier:425":["In the synthetic method the grounds must always be thought sooner than the consequences §.422. Consequently 1) the concepts which are marks of other concepts must be defined sooner than the others §.115, and therefore the higher sooner than the lower §.261. 2) The indemonstrable truths must precede the demonstrable ones §.314-317. 3) The premise propositions must be thought sooner than the concluding propositions §.356, and therefore the pre-syllogisms sooner than the post-syllogisms §.407. 4) Those demonstrable truths from which others follow must precede these. In the analytic method everything is the other way around §.422.",[]],"meier:426":["The synthetic method which has merely the aim of promoting the mathematical certainty of cognition §.161 is called the mathematical method (*methodus mathematica*). It must therefore not only be in accordance most exactly with the rules of the synthetic §.425, but by virtue of it one also either leaves out everything that is not indispensably required for mathematical certainty, or one puts it in remarks §.340. In very difficult demonstrations, particularly for beginners, it has a great use, but if one accustoms oneself to it alone, then one gets a merely learned cognition §.161, and it is accordingly not to be advised §.40.",[]],"meier:427":["Art (*ars*) is a sum total of rules which are thought according to an order. Artificial method (*methodus artificialis, scholae*) is method insofar as it is learned and exercised through art. Natural method (*methodus naturalis*) is method insofar as it is not artificial, but follows from the nature of the cognition and of him who thinks. Mixed method (*methodus mixta*) is natural method insofar as the artificial comes to its aid.",[]],"meier:428":["Among the artificial learned methods the best are 1) the syllogistic method (*methodus syllogistica*), when one analyzes all proofs into nothing but formal inferences, and commits no leaps. It does a beginner good service. Otherwise it is too long-winded, too tedious and not distinct enough. 2) The method according to tables (*methodus tabellaris*), when one orders together all members of the divisions and parts of the whole in such a way that it is evident from this to what higher concept and whole each thought belongs. It promotes the distinct concept of a whole doctrinal system, and memory; but it often falls into the forced, attention is overloaded, and thoroughness cannot be duly attained through it. 3) The method according to a partition of a whole doctrinal system, when one lets the judgments follow upon one another according to the difference of their subjects; whence chapters, sections etc. arise. If it is combined with the synthetic, then it does not hinder thoroughness, and procures the use of the method according to tables.",[]],"meier:429":["If one wants to treat a doctrinal system, it must take place according to the synthetic, and in some cases according to the analytic method §.423. 424, for they are suited to the nature of a doctrinal system §.420. Now whatever of all the remaining artificial methods can be combined with them, either to promote distinctness and certainty, or memory, or to increase order, that let one connect with the synthetic and analytic method. Consequently the forced character in method (*affectatio in methodo*) is an error, 1) if the artificial method is good for nothing, is contrary to nature, and is entirely arbitrary. 2) If one binds oneself too exactly to a simple method, and will never make an exception from it. 3) If one observes the rules of method all too exactly and anxiously.",[]],"meier:430":["Learned method must be natural and unaffected §.429. It must therefore be in accordance with the nature of learned cognition, and be observed without anxious effort. Consequently it must be in accordance with the head of him who wants to think according to it. Whoever, therefore, has the gift for it can make use 1) of the Socratic method (*methodus socratica*), by virtue of which the thoughts follow upon one another as they occasion one another, as questions and answers in the conversation of several persons; 2) of the Platonic method (*methodus platonica*), by virtue of which the thoughts follow upon one another as in a free speech, so that all kinds of learned thoughts are mingled among one another, without one's making formal and manifest inferences.",[]],"meier:431":["If one wants to treat a system according to the synthetic method, then one must 1) lay at the ground only One truth, from which all the rest is derived, so that the system may obtain a unity and a thoroughgoing interconnection, so that it may contain neither more nor less than is necessary. 2) One lays a division at the ground, and separates the system into some sections, yet in such a way that no exception to the synthetic method is made thereby. 3) The learned thoughts themselves must follow upon one another in the following way. a) First come the definitions; b) then follow either the indemonstrable truths or the intuitive judgments; c) then the demonstrable truths. d) The corollaries and remarks are strewn in everywhere they are necessary and useful §.420. 425.",[]],"meier:432":["The method of historical truth §.421 must be in conformity with the nature of historical truths §.419; consequently one must think them in the order in which their objects are combined with one another either according to space or according to time. To this therefore belongs the chronological method (*methodus chronologica*), when one thinks the occurrences in just the sequence in which they actually followed upon one another; and the geographical method (*methodus geographica*), by virtue of which one thinks the occurrences that take place, or have taken place, in one place according to the chronological method. Now because the actual occurrences of the world are also interconnected, and a human being can often have insight into this interconnection, one can, at least often, also make use of the rational method in the case of historical truths §.415.",[]],"meier:433":["The best learned composite method can seem to be a disorder to a human being who does not know its rules, or who has accustomed himself to another method, or only to a simple one. But on that account one can make no well-grounded reproach to such a method.",[]],"meier:434":["A doctrine, a teaching (*doctrina*) is a sum total of dogmatic truths which have one and the very same object. A discipline (*disciplina*) is a teaching insofar as it is cognized methodically. A demonstrated discipline is a science (*scientia obiective spectata*). Learned cognition is always in the beginning a doctrine, then one gives it the shape of a discipline, and finally the shape of a science, and then it has attained its greatest perfection.",[]],"meier:435":["Whoever thinks, thinks either according to a method, or according to none §.414. The former thinks methodically (*methodice cogitare, methodicum, acroamaticum, disciplinale in cognitione*), but the latter tumultuarily (*tumultuaria cognitio*). No tumultuary cognition can be properly learned §.416.",[]],"meier:436":["Meditating (*meditatio*) is that occupation of our cognitive powers through which we reflect upon a thing according to the rules of a method. If it happens according to the rules of the learned method, then it is a learned meditating (*meditatio erudita*), which is as manifold as there are different species of the learned method. Without this learned meditating we can, attain no learned cognition.",[]],"meier:437":["Whoever wants to meditate in a learned way 1) let him choose for himself an object upon which he directs his attention; 2) let him think gradually the marks of the same, which make up its definition, the indemonstrable, and finally the demonstrable predicates of the same. These one has either already learned, and then one does well if one writes while meditating; or one wants to learn them, and then one must read in a book, or listen to the oral presentation; or one wants first to discover them. 3) One investigates whether what one thinks is a concept, a judgment, a theorem, etc., and reflects upon it until one has done justice to all the rules which the doctrine of reason prescribes for each species of learned thoughts.",[]],"meier:438":["It is a disgrace if out of pedantry one despises all ways of thinking that are not struck over the last of that method with which one has fallen in love, and if, like a charlatan, one makes too much fuss about the method one loves.of the learned presentation. of the use of words.",[]],"meier:439":["The logical art of signification (*logica characteristica heuristica*) is the science of the rules that one must observe if one wants to signify learned cognition in a skillful way. The logical art of interpretation (*hermeneutica logica*) is the science of the rules of how one is to cognize, in a learned way, the signified things from the signs.",[]],"meier:440":["A sign (*signum, symbolum*) is a means through the use of which the actuality of another thing can be cognized, which other thing is called the signified thing or the meaning (*signatum, significatus*). An expression (*terminus*) is a sign of cognition; the expressions which customarily consist in a human voice are called words (*vocabulum*). The cognition signified through the expressions and words is called the meaning of the same (*significatus vocabuli et termini*).",[]],"meier:441":["That meaning which the one who uses the word wants to signify through the word is the true meaning (*significatus hermeneutice verus*). But every other meaning is the false one (*significatus hermeneutice falsus*). The true meaning can be a false concept, and the false one a true concept.",[]],"meier:442":["Discourse (*oratio*) is a series of words which signifies representations that are connected with one another. The series of representations is called the sense of the discourse (*sensus orationis*), which is either a true sense (*sensus hermeneutice verus*), if the speaker has wanted to signify it, or a false one (*sensus hermeneutice falsus*), if it is not the true one. Presentation (*propositio*) is the production of a cognition in others, by means of a discourse, whether the same be an oral or a written presentation.",[]],"meier:443":["As many species of cognition as there are, so many species of discourse and of presentation there also are §.442. There are thus common discourses, aesthetic discourses, rational discourses, and also such species of presentation §.17. 18.19. A learned discourse (*oratio erudita*) is a discourse whose sense is a learned cognition §.21, and we present something in a learned way (*erudite proponere*) when we, through a learned discourse, produce a learned cognition in others.",[]],"meier:444":["The learned discourse must, together with all its parts, be so constituted that the highest perfection of learned cognition is not hindered, but promoted and signified §.442. One must thus in the same 1) observe all aesthetic rules of discourse which are not contrary to the logical perfection of cognition §.34. 2) Avoid all aesthetic perfections which are contrary to the required logical perfection of the discourse§.34.",[]],"meier:445":["A discourse is either merely learned (*oratio mere erudita*), if it signifies nothing further than a merely learned cognition, but in the opposite case it is not a merely learned discourse (*oratio non mere erudita*). And just so is the learned presentation different §.443. The latter are the best §.40. 32.",[]],"meier:446":["The learned expression must be ample §.25. 444. Consequently 1) a learned man must know as many expressions as are required to signify all his learned thoughts. 2) One must know all necessary technical terms of one's principal science, and in case of need it is permitted to invent new expressions. A technical term (*terminus technicus*) is an expression which may not be used at all outside an art or discipline. 3) One must know all expressions of equal meaning (*synonyma*), or all expressions that signify the same. 4) The learned expression must signify everything that one thinks, and signify neither more nor less than one wants to say. 5) One must know all meanings of an expression which it has already received through the common and the learned use of speech. The common use of speech (*usus loquendi*) is the agreement in common life by virtue of which people who speak a language combine one or more meanings with certain words. The learned use of speech (*receptus terminorum significatus*) is just such an agreement of those who apply themselves to a discipline.",[]],"meier:447":["The learned expression must be great, important and decent enough for learned cognition §.444. 26. Consequently in the learned presentation one must avoid all base, ridiculous and vulgar expressions, which are customary only among the rabble.",[]],"meier:448":["The learned expression must be correct §.444. 27. A true expression (*terminus verus*) must correctly signify its true meaning §.441: an expression which is not true is a false expression (*terminus falsus*).",[]],"meier:449":["An expression is false: 1) if its meaning is a false concept, and the one who uses the expression is convinced of the incorrectness of the meaning; 2) if we believe we understand an expression, although we yet do not understand it; such a false expression is an empty expression, or an empty word (*terminus inanis*). We understand an expression (*terminum intelligere*) when we cognize its meaning from it. An empty word arises: a) if we take the concept of the word itself for its meaning §.440. b) If the meaning of the word is an erroneous or, deceptive concept (*conceptus deceptor*), a concept which we believe we have, although we yet have none. 3) If it does not correctly signify a true concept§.448.",[]],"meier:450":["A familiar expression (*terminus familiaris*) is an expression which we have become accustomed to, or which we believe we understand, although we do not understand it, because we pay no attention to its meaning. Such expressions can be true expressions, but in their use we stand in the greatest danger of hatching empty expressions, if we combine a couple of familiar expressions with one another whose meanings are impossible, or contradict one another §.449. Hence people can speak of nothing, and yet seem to understand one another, namely when they use expressions that are familiar to them.",[]],"meier:451":["Although in learned discourses one cannot avoid the false expressions of the first kind §.449 when one must refute errors; yet one must avoid all other false and empty expressions, because from their use thoughtlessness and errors arise. To that end one must use no expression 1) whose meaning one has not cognized, and 2) whose meaning one has not investigated, whether it is a true or a false concept.",[]],"meier:452":["A crude expression (*terminus crassus*) is an expression whose meaning is a crude cognition §.102. The use of empty expressions in learned discourses is the word-peddling of the learned, where one sells words for things. E.g., if a learned man is supposed to state the ground of a thing, and he has signified the thing with an expression which he afterwards adduces as the ground, then he sells words for things. All this one must avoid §.451.",[]],"meier:453":["The learned expression must be clear §.444. 28. One must thus not only be able oneself to distinguish them properly from other expressions, but their meaning must also be sufficiently clear. An obscure expression (*terminus obscurus*) has an obscure meaning, a clear one (*terminus clarus*) a clear one, etc. An obscure expression is not on that account an empty one §.449, and an expression which is obscure to me is not on that account absolutely and to other people obscure §.126.",[]],"meier:454":["From an expression its meaning can either be clearly cognized, or not. The former is an intelligible expression (*terminus intelligibilis*), but the latter an unintelligible expression (*terminus non intelligibilis*). Now although in a learned discourse one cannot use nothing but expressions which are intelligible to all people; yet one must use nothing but such expressions as can be intelligible also to people of middling understanding, and who are middlingly in command of the language, if they pay even only middling attention to the discourse §.453.",[]],"meier:455":["Whoever wants to make use of the most intelligible expressions of all in a learned discourse §.454, 1) must prefer the better known and more customary expressions to the less known and less customary ones, if they are otherwise equal to one another. 2) He must always combine with the expression that meaning which is combined with it through the use of speech, both in common life and also in the disciplines §.446, until need forces him to the contrary; 3) he must, if the use of speech is manifold, connect with the expressions the most customary meanings of all (*significatus famosissimus*), that is, those which are combined with the expression by most people in most cases; 4) if in a certain case the three preceding rules do not suffice, then one defines that meaning in which one takes an expression §.268.",[]],"meier:456":["The learned expression must be certain §.444. 29. A certain expression (*terminus certus*) is so constituted that one not only knows certainly that it has a meaning, but also that it has just this and no other meaning. An expression is uncertain (*terminus incertus*) if it is not certain; and through the use of uncertain expressions the discourse is set upon screws.",[]],"meier:457":["An ambiguous expression (*terminus ambiguus, homonymus, vagus*) does not always have One meaning; as soon as it receives it, its meaning is fixed (*terminus fixus*). The ambiguity of the expression hinders its certainty §.456, one must thus prevent all ambiguity. And if the use of speech does not suffice for this §.446, then one must fix the meanings through logical definitions.",[]],"meier:458":["The learned expression must please so much that one can be stimulated thereby to cognize from it the cognition lying hidden under it §.444.30. Consequently it must be adorned with all perfections which are not contrary to learned cognition, so that one reads or hears it gladly and with pleasure.",[]],"meier:459":["In the use of words in logical definitions four rules are still to be observed: 1) One must observe the use of speech also in definitions, as long as it is possible §.455. 2) One must put neither the expression of the defined concept, nor the equivalent expressions of the same, into the discourse which signifies the definition; for then the definition would not be more distinct than the defined concept §.275, 446. 3) A definition must be expressed with as few words as can happen without detriment to distinctness; so that the attention is not dispersed through too many words.",[]],"meier:460":["That meaning for the sake of which a word has been invented and is used, and even if it should otherwise have no meaning at all, is called the proper meaning (*significatus proprius*), the other meanings are improper (*significatus improprius*). If one uses a word to signify the former, then it is a proper one (*terminus proprius*); but if one uses it to signify the latter, then it is an improper word (*terminus improprius*). With an improper word we always think, besides the improper meaning, the proper one, although one could have an exhaustive concept of each without thinking of the other. Hence one must 4) prevent all improper expressions in the expression of logical definitions, because otherwise the definition would be too ample §.270, and if we sometimes can find no proper expressions in the language, then we must first transform an improper word, through the definition of its improper meaning, into a proper one, before we use it in another definition.",[]],"meier:461":["If one is supposed to define the meaning of a word of many meanings, then we can define one among its several meanings which pleases us, if the several meanings are customary in an equal degree. And in this respect definitions are often arbitrary.",[]],"meier:462":["A judgment which is signified through expressions is called a proposition (*propositio, enunciatio*). A proposition in which not all concepts are signified with particular expressions is called a hidden proposition (*propositio cryptica*). The expressions which signify the magnitude of the judgment are called the signs of magnitude. In a proposition either the signs of magnitude are present or not (*propositio definita, et indefinita*). The latter are understood universally through linguistic usage, and whoever therefore does not want to be understood wrongly must not omit the signs of magnitude in the propositions which he does not want to have understood universally. A proposition is understood when all the expressions found in it are understood §.449.",[]],"meier:463":["A syllogism, when it is signified through expressions, is called a syllogistic discourse (*syllogismus*). One should guard against always presenting syllogistic discourses according to one order, and with the same technical terms. And if one hides or mutilates the syllogistic discourses, then one should conform to the listener, so that he is not confused, and can remember all the omitted propositions. Those fallacies which rest merely on the ambiguity of expressions (*sophisma ambiguitatis, amphiboliae*) are hardly worth the trouble of making mention of them.of the learned style.",[]],"meier:464":["The learned style (*stilus eruditus, philosophicus*), is the agreement, or similarity of the way and manner in which one speaks in a learned way.",[]],"meier:465":["Since the learned style is a part of the learned discourse §.464. 443, it has the same aim as the latter, namely to promote learned cognition in oneself and in others. A good learned style (*stilus eruditus perfectus*) must thus promote learned cognition and its perfections, but one which hinders them is a bad learned style (*stilus eruditus imperfectus*).",[]],"meier:466":["To the perfections of the learned style belong 1) all properties of the same without which the perfections of learned cognition cannot be obtained; 2) all remaining beauties of style, if they are not contrary to the perfection of learned cognition. Without these the style would be merely learned. To the imperfections of the learned style belong: 1) all properties of the same whereby the perfection of learned cognition is hindered, even if they should be beauties; 2) all properties whereby imperfections are caused in learned cognition; 3) all imperfections which can be tolerated in no style §.465.",[]],"meier:467":["The learned style must be so arranged that through it, the distinctness of the cognition of truth from grounds, is promoted in the best way §.465. This is the fundamental law of the learned style.",[]],"meier:468":["1) The learned style must be distinct §.467. The distinctness of the learned style (*perspicuitas stili eruditi*) is the perfection of the same by virtue of which it does not hinder, but promotes the distinctness of the signified cognition. The opposed imperfection is its obscurity (*obscuritas stili eruditi*). In order to make the style distinct, one must 1) use nothing but clear expressions §.453-455; 2) one must not speak too briefly, but copiously; 3) one must combine the parts of the discourse with one another in such a way that from them the combination of the thoughts can be cognized most distinctly and most easily. Consequently one must a) avoid all rare and difficult constructions; b) not reject the constructions; c) one must not omit all too frequently the expressions which signify the combinations of the thoughts; d) one must not make too many insertions; e) one must not make the points all too long. A proposition which is no part of another proposition is called a point (*punctum*). Thus one must mix shorter and longer points among one another.",[]],"meier:469":["2) The learned style must, as much as is possible, be pure §.467.The purity of the learned style (*puritas stili eruditi*) consists in the agreement of the same with the improved nature of the language. The opposed imperfection is the impurity of the same (*impuritas stili eruditi*). Since a pure style pleases, it stimulates the attention of the reader and listener, and it thus promotes the distinctness of learned cognition §.142.",[]],"meier:470":["If the purity of the style should hinder its distinctness, then one must make an exception from the former §.35. 21. Such a necessary impurity is only an apparent imperfection of the learned style §.466.",[]],"meier:471":["For the sake of the purity of the learned style one must avoid: 1) all hybrid words (*vox hybrida*), whose parts are taken from different languages; 2) all grammatical errors; 3) all barbarous expressions; 4) all expressions from foreign languages, and foreign constructions, unless they have already obtained the right of citizenship; 5) all obsolete expressions and constructions; 6) all new words and technical terms; 7) all multiplicity of expressions, if instead of them one can make use of One expression, even though it is impure §.469. From all these rules one can make exceptions for the sake of §.470.",[]],"meier:472":["With regard to the purity of the learned style there must be avoided: 1) the negligent and careless barbarism of pedantic learned men, by virtue of which they entirely neglect the purity of style; 2) the all too great love of purity of style, when one seeks, even to the detriment of the perfection of learned cognition, to preserve purity in a quite forced way §.466.",[]],"meier:473":["3) The learned style must, as much as is possible, be well-fitted. The fittingness of style (*concinnitas stili*) consists in the proportioned agreement of the parts of a discourse, e.g., when one makes periods. In a merely learned discourse this perfection cannot be obtained, but in a learned discourse which is not merely learned, one must, in those places where one at the same time thinks beautifully, also take care of this perfection as much as possible §.467. 445, because it makes the expression more agreeable §.458.",[]],"meier:474":["4) The learned style must be ornate, as much as is possible §.467. The ornateness of style (*ornatus stili eruditi*) consists therein, when instead of certain expressions others are used whereby just the same concepts, but in a more beautiful way, are signified. In those parts where the discourse is merely learned, no ornate style is possible; but if the learned discourse must at the same time be beautiful, then the learned style must also be ornate §.445.",[]],"meier:475":["5) The learned style must, as much as it is possible, be euphonious §.467. The euphony of style (*sonoritas stili*) consists in that perfection by virtue of which it pleases the ears §.458. This perfection one must seek especially in those places of a learned discourse where the discourse is not merely learned §.445.",[]],"meier:476":["Nothing but simple propositions, and all too ample points obscure the style §.468. In a learned discourse, therefore, also for the sake of the fittingness and euphony of style §.473. 475 the simple and composite points must alternate with middlingly beautiful periods, according as the signified thoughts are merely learned, or at the same time more or less beautiful.",[]],"meier:477":["6) The learned style must, as much as is possible, be suitable §.467.The suitability of style (*congruentia stili*), is that perfection by virtue of which the style suits the signified cognition, the speaking person and the listeners, and is in conformity with them. Consequently the learned style must be important enough for learned cognition §.447, and thus not ridiculous, vulgar, base. And it must be natural to the one who makes use of it, and thus not forced.",[]],"meier:478":["For the sake of the suitability of the learned style it must 1) in those places where one expresses merely learned thoughts, not be so pure, well-fitted, ornate and euphonious; as 2) in those places where thoughts are expressed which are at the same time beautiful §.477.of a learned discourse.",[]],"meier:479":["The learned presentation is either an oral presentation, or a written one, that is, a book or a writing (*liber, scriptum*). Whoever gives a learned presentation is a teacher, and whoever obtains his learned cognition from the presentation of another learns it, and is a pupil, whether he be a reader or a listener.",[]],"meier:480":["The sense of a learned discourse must be a learned cognition which is in conformity with all the rules of the perfection of the same §.10-438, and the expression must be so constituted as we have shown so far §.439-478. We must thus in a learned discourse present all the parts of learned cognition, and indeed in the sequence in which they must follow upon one another if a learned cognition is to arise.",[]],"meier:481":["A learned discourse must be ample in an exhaustive way §.480. 41-65. It must thus be neither too brief (*oratio erudita nimis brevis*), if it says less of the object than suffices for the most perfect learned cognition; nor all too ample (*oratio erudita nimis prolixa*), if it says more than is necessary for the attainment of a perfect learned cognition.",[]],"meier:482":["A learned discourse can with regard to one be too ample, with regard to another too brief, and with regard to the third exhaustive §.481. Consequently the less capacity a pupil possesses, the less he has already learned, and the less he applies himself to an object, the briefer the learned presentation must be for him. A pupil of the opposite kind must be instructed through a more ample presentation.",[]],"meier:483":["A learned discourse must be important enough §.480. 66-91. 447.477. The object of the same must thus be no worthless trifle.",[]],"meier:484":["A learned discourse must be as correct as possible §.480.92-114. 448-452. A learned man who himself errs, and on top of that spreads his errors, is a seducer of other people, and doubly imperfect. One must thus first investigate one's own cognition most carefully before one presents it.",[]],"meier:485":["A learned discourse must be distinct and intelligible §.480. 114-154. 468. It is thus a fault, 1) if the teacher speaks without understanding, or if his discourse has no sense at all; 2) if his words and constructions are obscure and too difficult; 3) if he himself cannot be understood. A speaker is understood (*proponens intelligitur*) when one combines with his words just the series of representations which he has wanted to signify through the same. One can thus often understand the discourse without understanding the speaker §.449. A teacher must thus either himself define in what meanings he takes his words, or he must connect with them no other concept than those of which he knows that the pupil already has them, and connects them with the words.",[]],"meier:486":["If a teacher presents confused cognition, then his pupil must either already possess it, and he only brings it to his memory through the presentation, or he must impart it to him in another way than through the presentation. Distinct cognition can only be produced in others through presentation §.14. A teacher must thus possess a very distinct cognition.",[]],"meier:487":["The learned presentation becomes distinct: 1) through a distinct style §.468. 2) Through the observance of a convenient method §.416. 3) If one clarifies abstract cognition through beautiful examples. 4) One presents a thing, if it deserves it on account of its importance, in a manifold and varied way, and one makes use of the aesthetic clarifying arguments. 5) What is agreeable and lively in the presentation promotes attention, and thus also distinctness.",[]],"meier:488":["A learned discourse must be convincing and thorough §.480. 155-215. It is thus a fault, 1) if a teacher conducts no proofs at all, then, when he presents a demonstrable cognition; 2) if his proofs are not certain in the degree, and of the kind of certainty, that the cognition to be presented requires; 3) if he puts a blue haze before his pupils (*fucus logicus*), that is, if instead of conviction he produces a mere persuasion in them §.184.",[]],"meier:489":["A learned discourse must be practical §.480. 216-248. Everything that we have remarked so far about the learned discourse must also be applied to the learned presentation §.443.",[]],"meier:490":["The gift of learned presentation (*donum didacticum*) is the sum total of all aptitudes of the soul and of the body without which no perfect learned presentation is possible. Without this gift no one can be a teacher §.479.",[]],"meier:491":["A teacher must conform in his learned presentation to his pupils §.479. Consequently one must commend to him 1) condescension (*condescendentia*), when he has before him pupils of lesser capacity, and arranges his presentation in such a way that it is not too high for them; 2) elevation (*coadscendentia*) to the greater capacity of his pupils, so that the presentation is not too poor for them.",[]],"meier:492":["A pupil of learned cognition 1) must be disposed by nature to learned cognition; 2) he must already have exercised his understanding; 3) he must often already possess a store of learned cognition; 4) he must apply the necessary diligence to the learning of learned cognition. A learned man must thus present learned cognition to no one but whoever is a skillful pupil of the same; and he must not become angry when he sees that many people learn nothing from his presentation, unless he should turn his anger against himself, because he sees that he applies his trouble to the wrong persons.",[]],"meier:493":["In a learned presentation one has either chiefly the aim of refuting someone and his error, and it is then a refutation of another (*refutatio alterius*), or not. In the latter case one considers the reader or listener as a pupil who is ignorant, but not as one who errs, and one thus seeks to produce a perfect learned cognition in him; such a presentation is called an instruction (*docere*).",[]],"meier:494":["In learned instruction the following rules must be observed: 1) one must make the beginning with the definition or description of all the concepts that occur in the object, if they are not otherwise already sufficiently clear to the pupil; and one must convince him of the correctness of these concepts according to the rules of the 258th. 265th. 267th. and 278th paragraph.",[]],"meier:495":["2) In instruction about an indemonstrable proposition one needs nothing further than that one analyze it, and if it can be done, clarify it through an example §.313-318. 300. 487.",[]],"meier:496":["3) In instruction about an intuitive judgment one must either bring back to the memory of the pupil all the sensations of which this judgment consists; or one must impart them to him in the instruction §.319-321. 202; or one must convince him through probable testimonies that others have had these sensations §.206-215.",[]],"meier:497":["4) In instruction about demonstrable propositions a) one analyzes the proposition, and the solution of a problem, so that they are rightly understood; b) one presents the proof according to the analytic method, and brings it into One or into a couple of formal syllogistic discourses, if it is long §.423. 203. 204. 206-215; c) one carries on the whole proof until one comes either to indemonstrable and intuitive judgments, or to such demonstrable judgments as the other is already sufficiently convinced of. From this stems the citation of paragraphs in writings.",[]],"meier:498":["5) One seals the conviction of the truth through a practical representation of its practical property: for what the heart loves, the understanding believes §.216-248.",[]],"meier:499":["A correct proof that an error is an error is a true refutation (*refutatio vera*); but an incorrect proof that either an error is an error, or a truth is an error, is an apparent refutation (*impugnatio*). Only errors can be correctly refuted. The one who undertakes the refutation is the attacking party (*adversarius opponens*), and whoever refutes the refutation, the defending one (*adversarius defendens*). The defense is thus either correct or false. A learned dispute (*controversia*) consists of the refutation and defense of an opinion.",[]],"meier:500":["That proposition which the attacking party refutes, and the attacked one defends, is called the disputed proposition (*thesis controversa*), and the proposition of the attacking party which cannot be true at the same time as the disputed proposition is called the counter-proposition (*antithesis*). It is thus opposed to the disputed proposition either in a contradictory §.343, or universal way §.345. The question of dispute is determined (*status controversiae, seu quaestionis formatur*) when the attacking party proves distinctly which is the disputed proposition, and which the counter-proposition.",[]],"meier:501":["If a question of dispute is not correctly determined, then the opponents either conduct no dispute at all, or they fall into a dispute of words (*logomachia*), that is, into a dispute which arises from this, that one does not understand one another §.500, and that is always a foolish and vain matter. But different from this are the disputes about words (*controversia philologica*), which are true disputes, whose object however is words or other expressions.",[]],"meier:502":["Learned disputes must be conducted according to the following rules: 1) the attacking party seeks an error in the proof of his opponent §.194, or in general a deviation of the thoughts of the opponent from the rules of the doctrine of reason. Then he has, to be sure, not refuted the matter itself, but yet the way of thinking of his opponent. Against such an attack one either cannot defend oneself at all, if it is correct; or one must show that one has not violated the rules of the doctrine of reason, the transgression of which has been charged against us.",[]],"meier:503":["2) The attacking party demonstrates the opposite, then it follows from this that the disputed proposition is false, and that the opponent errs §.500. Against this attack one can defend oneself only if the opponent has committed an error in his demonstration, and this one must show according to the first rule §.502. If one party can show the other that it accepts provable propositions without proof, then this objection remains unanswered until this proof is carried out.",[]],"meier:504":["3) The attacking party refutes the disputed proposition through an apagogic proof §.196. To this belongs also when one shows that the disputed proposition contradicts another opinion of the opponent, although we do not ourselves accept the same (*argumentatio ad hominem*). Against such an attack one can defend oneself only if there is an error in the apagogic proof, and this one must show to the opponent §.502.",[]],"meier:505":["4) If one of the opponents leaves the question in dispute, and brings forward things through which the contested proposition is not refuted, and the opposite is not proved; then one must not engage with such matters, but concede it in the present dispute, so that it does not become all too prolix §.481. The other party can in this case defend itself only if the first has conceded something out of haste, and then it must show that what is conceded does indeed concern the question in dispute.",[]],"meier:506":["5) The attacking party can, through a restriction §.299 which he adds to the contested proposition, show that the same is not as universally true as the opponent gives it out to be. Against this attack we can defend ourselves only by either showing that this restriction has no influence on the present question in dispute, or that it is false, or if we soundly prove that our proposition is true without restriction.",[]],"meier:507":["6) The attacking party brings against his opponent a distinction (*distinctio*), or he shows the difference of the things which the opponent has confused, and through the confusion of which he has been led to engage in a dispute, or to err. Against a distinction one can defend oneself just as against a restriction §.506.",[]],"meier:508":["7) The attacking party can, through instances, refute the universality of the contested propositions. An instance (*instantia*) is an exception to a proposition, from which it sufficiently becomes evident that it is not universally true. Against an instance one can defend oneself only if one either accepts it, or shows that it does not fit, or restricts the contested proposition so that it does not fit; or establishes the universality of the contested proposition, without regard to the instance.",[]],"meier:509":["8) The attacked party uses reprisals (*retorsio*) when he shows that that which the opponent has derived through the apagogic proof from the contested proposition §.504 rather follows from the opposite. And then the attacking party cannot defend himself otherwise than if he shows that his opponent commits an error in his proof.",[]],"meier:510":["9) After the completed refutation one should seek to instill in the opponent an abhorrence of the error, by showing, yet without violation of the duties which one owes to the opponent, that the same is dangerous, harmful and ridiculous. Against this attack one can defend oneself only if one shows that the opponent views our opinion from the wrong side. Through a correct observation of these rules, the refutations become at the same time practical §.489.",[]],"meier:511":["If one derives bad and absurd consequences from the contested proposition, not, say, in order to convince the opponent of his error, but in order to offend him in some way, then one is a maker of consequences or consequence-maker (*consequentiarius*). Consequence-making is not necessarily an error against the doctrine of reason, but it is always a sin, and thus worthy of being abhorred by all honest people.",[]],"meier:512":["The defense against a consequence-maker is called the vindication (*apologia*); this one can conduct either with logical weapons, if the consequence-maker has committed errors against the doctrine of reason §.511, in which case one must at the same time show him his lack of understanding and wicked heart, yet without consequence-making; or one must make use of the weapons of right, and then the doctrine of reason has nothing more to remark about it.",[]],"meier:513":["Although learned disputes have utility §.178, a learned person must nevertheless 1) not make a trade of refuting others; 2) not begin a dispute of its own over any trifle; 3) conduct the important and necessary refutations with complete thoroughness, and 4) not defend himself against all attacks.",[]],"meier:514":["Two disputing parties dispute with each other (*disputatio formaliter sumta*) when the opposite is presented and judged in a learned way, and when both happen orally and in the presence of both parties. He who presents the opposite in a learned way is the opponent (*opponens*), and he who judges it, that is, uncovers what is false and uncertain in the same and its proof, is the respondent (*respondens*). Now so that the disputing does not become disorderly and all too prolix, only the opponent must carry out proofs, and the respondent do nothing further than judge the proofs.",[]],"meier:515":["Since the speeches which are given in disputing do not belong in the district of the doctrine of reason; it is clear that the opponent makes the beginning of the disputing, and then he must 1) determine the question in dispute §.500. 501. If he does not understand the opinion of the respondent, then he must have it explained to him by the latter. 2) He attacks the respondent according to one of the rules presented in §.502-510. 3) He presents, for the sake of brevity and distinctness, his proofs in formal syllogisms §.399, and 4) for the very same reason he makes use of the analytic method of teaching §.423. The Socratic and Platonic method of teaching makes the disputing too prolix and burdensome §.430. Unless one should see oneself compelled through the first to keep the other party to the question in dispute.",[]],"meier:516":["The respondent takes up the argument (*assumere argumentum*) when he repeats the objection of the opponent, and this is useful in order to reflect properly on the same, so that he can soundly answer it. For the sake of brevity the respondent must do nothing further than show where the opponent has committed an error either in the matter or in the form of his proof §.502, and then he must demand that the opponent either change his syllogism, or prove the premise which seems to the respondent to be false or uncertain.",[]],"meier:517":["The assistant of the respondent (*praeses*) is to prevent the respondent from conceding the opposite which has not yet been sufficiently proved. He must thus, if the respondent knows nothing or nothing apt any more to answer, take over his office §.516.of learned writings.",[]],"meier:518":["The presentation of past occurrences is history (*historia*). Learned writings present either history, or dogmatic truths §.104. The former are historical (*scripta erudita historica*), and the latter dogmatic writings (*scripta dogmatica*). Which present either a common cognition of dogmatic truths, or a learned one §.18. 21. The former are historical-dogmatic (*scriptum historico-dogmaticum*), the latter however systematic writings (*scriptum systematicum*), which are scientific writings (*scriptum scientificum*) if they present a science §.434. Every writing is with regard to its content either very prolix, or very short. The former isa great work (*systema*), the latter however an abridgment (*compendium*)",[]],"meier:519":["History either narrates matters which belong to nature and art, insofar as one takes into consideration neither the free actions of human beings nor their nearer effects; or it narrates the free actions of human beings, together with all that which stands in a nearer combination with them. The former is the history of nature and art (*historia naturae et artis*), the latter however history simply (*historia stricte dicta*). To the latter belongs 1) political history (*historia politica*), which narrates the civil and political actions of human beings; 2) church history (*historia ecclesiastica*), narrates the fortunes of the churches, and the actions of human beings which have a noticeable influence on the same; 3) private history (*historia privata*), narrates the course of life of individual persons, insofar as the same has no noticeable influence on the state, the church and learning; 4) learned history (*historia litteraria*), narrates the occurrences of the learned world, and it is either a history of the disciplines, or of the learned, or of learned writings.",[]],"meier:520":["A historical writing must 1) be exhaustive §.481.482, neither too prolix, nor too short. It can thus be exhaustive in one aim and for one reader, in another aim and for another reader too prolix, and in a third aim and for a third reader too short. With time every history becomes too short. 2) Important, as regards both the whole content and that which is narrated of the same §.483. 3) Correct §.484. 4) Distinct §.485, hence pictures and copperplate engravings are often necessary. 5) Thorough §.488, consequently prove everything, according to the rules of the 206th-215th sections. 6) Practical or pragmatic(*historia pragmatica*) §.489. 7) Methodical§.487. 432.",[]],"meier:521":["If history is to be pragmatic and exhaustive at the same time, then it must be presented in such a way that an aim is attained which is practical enough §.520. Now since everything which is actual in this world, if it can be cognized by us human beings, aims not only at the honor of GOD, but also at our happiness; a writer of history must seek to attain these two aims through history. In particular one must have a twofold aim in natural history §.519: 1) to come to know better the perfections of GOD from natural things, and 2) to instill in the readers distinct concepts of natural things, and thereby to pave the way to the learned cognition of the same.",[]],"meier:522":["History in the narrower sense §.519 serves the honor of GOD when one cognizes from the occurrences of human beings the divine government of the human race, and the perfections which GOD reveals in the same. To the happiness of human beings serves 1) civil history, when one can learn from it political prudence in affairs of war and peace; 2) church history, when one can learn from it the prudence to govern the church, and when it develops the marks of the true and false church; 3) learned history, when it furthers learning; 4) private history, when one can learn from it human prudence in private life.",[]],"meier:523":["Historical-dogmatic writings are not learned writings §.518. But if they are exhaustive, important, intelligible, correct and practical; then they are very useful to people who cannot and will not become learned, likewise also to children who are to be led to the sciences, in order to instill in them a foretaste of learning.",[]],"meier:524":["A systematic book must 1) be exhaustive §.481. 482. Thus it must either contain everything which, at the time when the book was written, is known of a doctrine, or it must contain as much of the same as the particular aim of the author requires. The most exhaustive writings can with time become unexhaustive, and one must thus carefully distinguish from one another the exhaustiveness of a dogmatic book with regard to the time, the author and the reader; 2) important §.483; 3) correct §.484, in that not only are all thoughts which are contained in it true, but each also accords with the rules of the doctrine of reason. 4) Distinct and intelligible §.485. 5) Thorough §.488, it must thus be written according to the learned method of teaching and interconnectedly. 6) Practical §.489. 7) Methodical §.414-438.",[]],"meier:525":["Compiling (*compilatio*) consists in an author's writing together everything which he finds here and there of the matter of which he wishes to write, without properly combining it with one another. Although a compiled work can be useful to other learned persons, it nevertheless remains, with regard to the author, a wretched work. A writing is stolen together (*plagiarius*) when the author gives himself out as the inventor of those thoughts which he has learned from others.",[]],"meier:526":["He who makes an abridgment out of a great work (*epitomator*), by leaving out of the former everything which is superfluous with regard to his smaller end, can write a very useful and necessary book, if only he has chosen his aim rationally §.518.of the character of a learned person.",[]],"meier:527":["The character of a learned person (*character eruditi*) consists in the sum total of those of his marks which are the nearer grounds and causes of learned cognition.",[]],"meier:528":["The general character of a learned person (*generalis character eruditi*) is the character of a learned person insofar as it contains the nearer grounds of learning in general; the particular, however (*character eruditi specialis*), insofar as it contains the nearer grounds of this or that kind, and of this or that part of learning.",[]],"meier:529":["The first piece of the general character of a learned person consists in the learned natural disposition (*natura erudita*), or in that proportion of all the powers of the soul by virtue of which a human being is apt and inclined to learned cognition §.528.",[]],"meier:530":["To the learned natural disposition belongs 1) mother wit, the learned head (*ingenium eruditum*), the proportion of the cognitive powers through which a human being is apt for learned cognition. To this belongs a) reason (*ratio*), the faculty of distinctly having insight into the interconnection of things §.21, which must by nature be disposed to become sound, widely extended, strong, thorough and beautiful. b) The understanding (*intellectus*), the faculty of distinct cognition §.21. 17, which must by nature be disposed to become widely extended, profound, pure and beautiful. Consequently there belongs to mother wit a great attention, contemplation, reflection and abstraction §.142. 2) A beautiful mind (*ingenium pulchrum*), the proportion of the cognitive powers by virtue of which a human being is disposed to beautiful thinking §.32.",[]],"meier:531":["To the learned natural disposition belongs 2) the learned temperament (*temperamentum eruditum*), or the proportion of the appetitive powers, and the agreement of the same with mother wit, by force of which a human being is not only inclined to attain a learned cognition, but by virtue of which also the cognitive powers are properly exerted to effect the most perfect learned cognition §.529.",[]],"meier:532":["The learned natural disposition is a mere gift of fortune, which must be inborn in a human being, without which, however, it is impossible to attain learned cognition through any art.",[]],"meier:533":["Mother wit is called the inborn natural doctrine of reason (*logica naturalis connata*), but the science of learned cognition and of learned presentation school wit, or the artificial doctrine of reason (*logica artificialis*). The indistinct cognition of the rules of mother wit, together with the facility of observing them, which one attains through the mere use of mother wit, is called the acquired natural doctrine of reason (*logica naturalis acquisita theoretica et practica*), and one counts it to mother wit in the wider sense. Mother wit does not contradict school wit, it is indispensable both in the theory and in the exercise of the latter. It is also sufficient outside the disciplines. But without school wit no learned cognition can be attained, it improves mother wit, and if a great school wit is combined with a great mother wit, then a human being can bring it much higher than through the best mother wit taken alone.",[]],"meier:534":["He who wishes to become a great learned person must follow nature, and apply himself to learned cognition only if he possesses the learned natural disposition, and insofar as he possesses it §.532.",[]],"meier:535":["One can cognize whether one possesses the learned natural disposition: 1) if one has a rational inclination to learned cognition, because its perfections please us; 2) if it goes easily for us whenever we think in a learned way; 3) if one becomes aware through an attempt that one can actually think in a learned way.",[]],"meier:536":["The other piece, of the general character of a learned person, consists in the facilities which he gradually attains through exercises §.527. To the facilities of a learned person belongs: 1) The extension of the understanding and of reason (*extensio intellectus et rationis*), the facility of cognizing many things distinctly and in a manifold interconnection, and of each thing many clear marks §.25. 2) The strength of reason and of the understanding (*intensio intellectus et rationis*), the facility of cognizing matters in a great degree of completeness and mathematical certainty §.27. 28.29. 3) The facility of thinking beautifully §.32 4) A great heart §.91 (*magnitudo pectoris*), the facility of thinking and acting in accordance with the learned temperament §.531, which presupposes the facility of thinking not otherwise than greatly and practically §.26. 30.",[]],"meier:537":["The learned exercises are A) the more general (*exercitia generaliora*), the frequent repetitions of learned meditating in general. To this belongs 1) learned naturalizing, the exercises which one undertakes without art, and which constitute the natural acquired doctrine of reason §.433.",[]],"meier:538":["2) The more perfect, regular and artificial exercises (*exercitia oculatiora, logica artificialis practica*), or the frequent observation of the rules of the artificial doctrine of reason. To this end a) one should learn a good artificial doctrine of reason; b) one should seek to exercise all the rules of the same; c) when one has produced and written down a series of thoughts according to the logical rules, one should hold them against these rules, and investigate where one has observed them or not. Through these exercises one must finally bring it so far that one observes the rules of the artificial doctrine of reason in thinking and speaking without being conscious of them.",[]],"meier:539":["B) The particular learned exercises, or studying (*exercitia erudita specialiora*) consists in all actions through which learned cognition arises and is improved in him who undertakes these actions. To this belong six exercises.",[]],"meier:540":["I) Learning from an oral presentation. Because this exercise is the easiest and safest, one must make the beginning with it. He who wishes to become learned through this exercise must 1) have prepared his head for learning through the lower and the beautiful sciences. 2) He must seek out at least a moderately able teacher, from whose oral presentation he can hope to attain a good learned cognition. 3) He must pay proper attention to the oral presentation, so that he rightly understands the teacher and his presentation. 4) He must meditate through everything which the teacher presents, or at least the main matters of his presentation §.436. 437. 5) He must judge logically (*logice diiudicare*) everything which he has heard, that is, seek to cognize what sort of logical perfections or imperfections are to be met with in the same. Hence he must investigate to what sort of species and genus of learned cognition that belongs which he wishes to judge; he must recall the logical rules of this species or genus, and from the comparison of the object with these rules seek to cognize whether it is in accordance or not in accordance with the same.",[]],"meier:541":["II) The reading of learned writings. To this end one must a) set oneself a rational end, for the sake of which one wishes to read learned writings. A student must thus read books, 1) in order to repeat a discipline which one has already learned; 2) in order to improve our learned cognition ever more and more; 3) in order to learn a part of learning which one does not yet understand; 4) in order to exercise oneself in the observation of the rules of the doctrine of reason. b) One must seek out a book which is apt to further this aim. Consequently one must 1) not read all books just as they fall into one's hands. 2) At the beginning read only such books of which one has found, after a logical judgment, that they are apt to further our aim. 3) If one already possesses a facility of thinking in a learned way, then one can also read with utility such books as are full of deficiencies and errors. c) One must seek rightly to understand the book and the author. d) One must meditate through and logically judge every thought which has been presented in the book, as §.540. n. 4. 5, so that one learns to have proper insight into the matters presented.",[]],"meier:542":["The useful reading of learned writings is furthered through the observation of the following rules: 1) One should read with the greatest attention, without tolerating foreign thoughts. 2) One should read the book through from the beginning, if it is written systematically. 3) One should never interrupt the reading in an exactly interconnected matter. 4) One should read the book through hastening slowly, and neither too quickly nor too slowly. 5) One must not become weary of reading before one has come to the end. 6) One must not read all too many books, especially of different content, intermixed. 7) One should make the beginning with the shorter abridgments, progress to the greater works, and finally read writings which treat particular matters exhaustively. 8) One should read the writings which belong to a main part of learning according to the synthetic method of teaching.",[]],"meier:543":["The learning of learned cognition is very much furthered, partly if at first one learns from only One teacher the first principles of the learning to which one has devoted oneself; partly if one at once uses the truths learned and brings them into exercise.",[]],"meier:544":["III) Frequent repetition (*repetitio*), or recollection of that which one has learned. For this it is required that one observe the following rules: 1) One must not make of this repetition a mere learning by heart. 2) In repetition one must meditate through and judge each thought just as if one wished to learn it for the first time, although this always goes more quickly and easily. 3) One should repeat the truths learned according to the synthetic method of teaching according to the analytic and inferring method of teaching §.423. 428. 4) One should repeat the repetition often, with some alteration. 5) One should always learn more in addition, and exercise the truths learned. 6) If in repetition one writes down one's meditations, this exercise is thereby very much furthered. 7) One should present the truths learned in writing or orally in a learned way. 8) One should converse with others about the truths learned.",[]],"meier:545":["Repetition and the learning of learned cognition is very much furthered if one writes down for oneself briefly, and with a rational choice, that which one fears to forget, and which at the same time brings the rest back into our memory.",[]],"meier:546":["IV) Inquiry (*investigatio*), or the rational imitation of other learned persons in their learned thinking and presentation. To this end 1) one should choose for oneself a great learned person as a model for emulation. 2) One should investigate how the same manages to think so learnedly, so thoroughly, so orderly etc. 3) One should investigate how he has discovered the truths, or how they could be discovered if they were not yet invented. 4) One should seek in similar cases to think and to speak just so.",[]],"meier:547":["V) One should present the disciplines to others in a learned way, either orally or in writing. Or learned presentation is an exercise in learned cognition; for through teaching we learn.",[]],"meier:548":["VI) The invention of new truths (*inventio*), when we cognize something in a learned way without learning it from other human beings; whether we may be the first human beings who cognize the same, or others may already have known it before us. This exercise is the most difficult, which one must save until last.",[]],"meier:549":["An *autodidactus* is a learned person who has become learned without a teacher, whether it be that he has either invented his whole learning §.548, or only has not enjoyed oral instruction. A rational human being chooses the easiest and safest way when he first seeks to learn from an oral presentation, then reads books, and rather applies his power of invention to discovering entirely new truths than such as have long been known, if all this is otherwise only possible for him.",[]],"meier:550":["Just as it is an error if one wishes to become learned without mother wit §.534; so it is a negligent way of thinking in a learned way (*negligens eruditionis genus et impolitum*) if one does not properly combine art with mother wit §.533.",[]],"meier:551":["The affected and forced way of thinking in a learned way (*affectatum et coactum eruditionis genus*) arises from this, when school wit is combined with mother wit in a bad way: 1) if the rules of the artificial doctrine of reason are false and ungrounded; 2) if its correct rules are badly applied, in that they are suited either not to the truths, or not to the natural disposition of the learned person.",[]],"meier:552":["A cognition and a presentation which are merely learned are the dry, sober, rough, scholastic and pedantic way of learning (*macilentum, ieiunum, pedanticum, scholasticum eruditionis genus*). In order to prevent the same, a student must daily devote some time, in the hours of recreation, to the beautiful sciences.",[]],"meier:553":["The third piece of the general character of a learned person consists in learned diligence (*diligentia erudita*), or in so great an exertion of all the acquired learned facilities as is required each time if one wishes to think and present in a learned way in a certain degree of perfection §.527. To this end one must observe ten rules.",[]],"meier:554":["1) A student must, before all things, delineate the horizon of his learned cognition most correctly, so that he already knows each time on what he has to direct his learned diligence. And so that this horizon is assumed neither too small nor too large, one must gradually extend it further as our powers and skills increase.",[]],"meier:555":["2) A student must most exactly assess his powers, whether they suffice for learning in general, and for that part of the same to which he applies himself, and for the cognition of this or that truth. To this end a) he must test himself whether he possesses the learned natural disposition §.529-535. b) One should transform that with regard to which one wishes to test one's powers into a problem §.328-339, and investigate whether one has powers enough actually to make the solution. c) One should guard against the prejudice of all too great confidence and mistrust in the judgment of one's powers §.170. d) One should test one's powers through a faithful attempt. e) One should let oneself be judged by others.",[]],"meier:556":["3) In studying one must not apply too much diligence, and not direct learned diligence to things which are met with above, below and outside the horizon of our learned cognition §.554.",[]],"meier:557":["4) One must also not apply too little diligence, but as much as is required to undertake all learned exercises properly, and gradually to study through the whole horizon of our learned cognition.",[]],"meier:558":["5) One must begin to study as early as is possible; what one wishes to do, one should do soon.",[]],"meier:559":["6) One must, if it is otherwise possible for us, study daily, and think in a learned way. He who does not go further forward goes backward.",[]],"meier:560":["7) One must, in the midst of the course of studying, always hasten slowly. So that one does not hasten too much, one must not leave a learned occupation before one has not satisfied all rules of the doctrine of reason in it. But so that one is also not too slow, one must properly kindle the desire to study.",[]],"meier:561":["8) One must never cease to learn, in that one either always learns something new in addition, or learns better to cognize what has already been learned.",[]],"meier:562":["9) One must constantly seek to improve the learning already attained, and to remove its still remaining deficiencies and errors.",[]],"meier:563":["10) One must connect with studying daily experience, intercourse with the respectable world, and the use of the things themselves about which one meditates in a learned way; so that one does not live as a mere learned worm on school dust, and dream of Platonic republics.",[]]};
